    <article class="card" id="ep1" data-n="1" data-search="1 roblox studio basics learn the studio windows, the game hierarchy, the three script types, and how to run your first line of code. studio setup services script types the four windows you actually need everything in this whole series happens inside these four panels. if one is missing, open the view tab at the top of studio and click its name. window what it does how to open explorer the tree of every object in your game. this is your file browser. view → explorer properties every setting on whatever object you clicked in explorer. size, color, anchored, etc. view → properties output where print() messages and red error messages appear. you will live here. view → output command bar a single line where you can run code instantly without making a script. great for testing. view → command bar dock output at the bottom of your screen and never close it. 90% of &quot;my script isn&#x27;t working&quot; is an error sitting unread in output. the game hierarchy (services) everything under game in explorer is a service . each one has a specific job, and where you put a script decides whether it runs at all . this table is worth memorizing. service what lives there who can see it workspace the physical 3d world: parts, models, spawn locations, and player characters. server + client players one player object per connected person. holds leaderstats, playergui, backpack. server + client lighting time of day, fog, atmosphere, post-processing effects. server + client replicatedstorage shared storage. remoteevents, shared modulescripts, tool templates. server + client replicatedfirst loads before anything else. custom loading screens go here. server + client serverscriptservice home for server scripts. the safest place to put game logic. server only serverstorage server-only assets. clients literally cannot see or steal these. server only startergui copied into each player&#x27;s playergui when they spawn. your ui lives here. client starterpack copied into each player&#x27;s backpack when they spawn. tools go here. client starterplayer holds starterplayerscripts (run once per player) and startercharacterscripts (run every respawn). client soundservice global sound settings and shared sound objects. server + client teams team objects for team-based games. server + client the three script types this is the single most important concept in roblox scripting. roblox games run on two machines at once : the server (roblox&#x27;s computer, one for everybody) and each player&#x27;s client (their own computer). which script type you use decides which machine your code runs on. type runs on works when placed in use it for script server serverscriptservice, workspace, serverstorage(no) game rules, awarding points, spawning parts, anything cheaters shouldn&#x27;t touch localscript one player&#x27;s client starterplayerscripts, startercharacterscripts, startergui, starterpack, replicatedfirst, the player&#x27;s character ui, camera, keyboard/mouse input, effects only that player should see modulescript nowhere by itself anywhere (usually replicatedstorage or serverscriptservice) reusable functions and data that other scripts require() a localscript inside workspace or serverscriptservice will silently do nothing . no error, no output. if your client code isn&#x27;t running, check where it lives first. modern studio added a runcontext property to script. set it to client and a regular script behaves like a localscript and runs anywhere. thedevking&#x27;s 2018 videos predate this, so the videos only show localscript — both approaches are valid today. your first script right-click serverscriptservice in explorer → insert object → script . delete whatever is in it and type this: -- this is a comment. roblox ignores everything after the two dashes. print(&quot;hello world!&quot;) press play (or f5). look at output. you should see hello world! in white text. press stop (shift+f5) when you&#x27;re done. comments -- a single-line comment --[[ a block comment. everything in here is ignored, across as many lines as you want. ]] print(&quot;this runs&quot;) -- comments can also sit at the end of a line select several lines and press ctrl + / to comment or uncomment them all at once. testing modes mode shortcut what happens play f5 you spawn in as a player. server and client both run. this is the normal test. play here — same as play, but you spawn wherever your camera is pointing. run f8 the world runs with no player character. good for testing server-only logic. stop shift+f5 ends the test. any edits you made while playing are thrown away. clients &amp; servers test tab launch 2+ fake players to test multiplayer behavior. edits made during a play test are discarded when you press stop. studio shows an orange border around the viewport to remind you. stop first, then edit. insert a script into serverscriptservice that prints your username. then insert a localscript into starterplayerscripts that prints something different, and confirm both messages appear in output when you press play."><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge">1</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">Roblox Studio Basics</span><span class="ctag">Learn the Studio windows, the game hierarchy, the three script types, and how to run your first line of code.</span></span><span class="cmeta">10:42</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button><button class="yt" type="button" data-vid="BkYwRdCukZA" data-n="1" data-title="Roblox Studio Basics"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M8 5v14l11-7z"/></svg>Watch</button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">studio</span><span class="chip">setup</span><span class="chip">services</span><span class="chip">script types</span></div><h3 class="bh">The four windows you actually need</h3><p>Everything in this whole series happens inside these four panels. If one is missing, open the <b>View</b> tab at the top of Studio and click its name.</p><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Window</th><th>What it does</th><th>How to open</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Explorer</td><td>The tree of every object in your game. This is your file browser.</td><td>View &rarr; Explorer</td></tr><tr><td>Properties</td><td>Every setting on whatever object you clicked in Explorer. Size, Color, Anchored, etc.</td><td>View &rarr; Properties</td></tr><tr><td>Output</td><td>Where <code>print()</code> messages and red error messages appear. You will live here.</td><td>View &rarr; Output</td></tr><tr><td>Command Bar</td><td>A single line where you can run code instantly without making a Script. Great for testing.</td><td>View &rarr; Command Bar</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>Dock Output at the bottom of your screen and never close it. 90% of "my script isn't working" is an error sitting unread in Output.</div></div><h3 class="bh">The game hierarchy (services)</h3><p>Everything under <code>game</code> in Explorer is a <b>service</b>. Each one has a specific job, and <i>where you put a script decides whether it runs at all</i>. This table is worth memorizing.</p><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Service</th><th>What lives there</th><th>Who can see it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Workspace</td><td>The physical 3D world: parts, models, spawn locations, and player Characters.</td><td>Server + Client</td></tr><tr><td>Players</td><td>One Player object per connected person. Holds leaderstats, PlayerGui, Backpack.</td><td>Server + Client</td></tr><tr><td>Lighting</td><td>Time of day, fog, atmosphere, post-processing effects.</td><td>Server + Client</td></tr><tr><td>ReplicatedStorage</td><td>Shared storage. RemoteEvents, shared ModuleScripts, tool templates.</td><td>Server + Client</td></tr><tr><td>ReplicatedFirst</td><td>Loads before anything else. Custom loading screens go here.</td><td>Server + Client</td></tr><tr><td>ServerScriptService</td><td>Home for server Scripts. The safest place to put game logic.</td><td>Server only</td></tr><tr><td>ServerStorage</td><td>Server-only assets. Clients literally cannot see or steal these.</td><td>Server only</td></tr><tr><td>StarterGui</td><td>Copied into each player's PlayerGui when they spawn. Your UI lives here.</td><td>Client</td></tr><tr><td>StarterPack</td><td>Copied into each player's Backpack when they spawn. Tools go here.</td><td>Client</td></tr><tr><td>StarterPlayer</td><td>Holds StarterPlayerScripts (run once per player) and StarterCharacterScripts (run every respawn).</td><td>Client</td></tr><tr><td>SoundService</td><td>Global sound settings and shared Sound objects.</td><td>Server + Client</td></tr><tr><td>Teams</td><td>Team objects for team-based games.</td><td>Server + Client</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class="bh">The three script types</h3><p>This is the single most important concept in Roblox scripting. Roblox games run on <b>two machines at once</b>: the <b>server</b> (Roblox's computer, one for everybody) and each player's <b>client</b> (their own computer). Which script type you use decides which machine your code runs on.</p><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Runs on</th><th>Works when placed in</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Script</td><td>Server</td><td>ServerScriptService, Workspace, ServerStorage(no)</td><td>Game rules, awarding points, spawning parts, anything cheaters shouldn't touch</td></tr><tr><td>LocalScript</td><td>One player's client</td><td>StarterPlayerScripts, StarterCharacterScripts, StarterGui, StarterPack, ReplicatedFirst, the player's Character</td><td>UI, camera, keyboard/mouse input, effects only that player should see</td></tr><tr><td>ModuleScript</td><td>Nowhere by itself</td><td>Anywhere (usually ReplicatedStorage or ServerScriptService)</td><td>Reusable functions and data that other scripts <code>require()</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>A LocalScript inside Workspace or ServerScriptService will <b>silently do nothing</b>. No error, no output. If your client code isn't running, check where it lives first.</div></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>Modern Studio added a <b>RunContext</b> property to Script. Set it to <code>Client</code> and a regular Script behaves like a LocalScript and runs anywhere. TheDevKing's 2018 videos predate this, so the videos only show LocalScript &mdash; both approaches are valid today.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Your first script</h3><p>Right-click <b>ServerScriptService</b> in Explorer &rarr; <b>Insert Object</b> &rarr; <b>Script</b>. Delete whatever is in it and type this:</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- This is a comment. Roblox ignores everything after the two dashes.</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Hello world!"</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><p>Press <b>Play</b> (or F5). Look at Output. You should see <code>Hello world!</code> in white text. Press <b>Stop</b> (Shift+F5) when you're done.</p><h3 class="bh">Comments</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- A single-line comment</span>

<span class="c-com">--[[
  A block comment.
  Everything in here is ignored,
  across as many lines as you want.
]]</span>

<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"this runs"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-com">-- comments can also sit at the end of a line</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>Select several lines and press <b>Ctrl + /</b> to comment or uncomment them all at once.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Testing modes</h3><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Shortcut</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Play</td><td>F5</td><td>You spawn in as a player. Server and client both run. This is the normal test.</td></tr><tr><td>Play Here</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Same as Play, but you spawn wherever your camera is pointing.</td></tr><tr><td>Run</td><td>F8</td><td>The world runs with no player character. Good for testing server-only logic.</td></tr><tr><td>Stop</td><td>Shift+F5</td><td>Ends the test. <b>Any edits you made while playing are thrown away.</b></td></tr><tr><td>Clients & Servers</td><td>Test tab</td><td>Launch 2+ fake players to test multiplayer behavior.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>Edits made <i>during</i> a Play test are discarded when you press Stop. Studio shows an orange border around the viewport to remind you. Stop first, then edit.</div></div><div class="callout try"><span class="ci">&#9654;</span><div><b>Try it yourself &mdash; </b>Insert a Script into ServerScriptService that prints your username. Then insert a LocalScript into StarterPlayerScripts that prints something different, and confirm both messages appear in Output when you press Play.</div></div></div></div></article>
<article class="card" id="ep2" data-n="2" data-search="2 printing how to make your code talk to you. print, warn, error, and joining text together. print warn error output strings the three output functions function color in output stops the script? when to use it print(...) white no normal debugging. &quot;did this line run? what&#x27;s this value?&quot; warn(...) yellow no something is off but survivable. missing optional data, fallback used. error(...) red yes something is genuinely broken and continuing would make it worse. print(&quot;normal message&quot;) warn(&quot;something looks wrong&quot;) error(&quot;everything is broken&quot;) -- script stops here print(&quot;this line never runs&quot;) printing more than one thing separate values with commas. roblox prints them all, separated by spaces. you can mix types freely — numbers, strings, booleans, even whole objects. print(&quot;health:&quot;, 100) --&gt; health: 100 print(&quot;alive?&quot;, true) --&gt; alive? true print(&quot;part:&quot;, workspace.baseplate) --&gt; part: baseplate print(1, 2, 3) --&gt; 1 2 3 joining text together (concatenation) two dots .. glue strings together. this is not a plus sign — that&#x27;s the #1 beginner mistake here. local name = &quot;nay&quot; print(&quot;hello &quot; .. name .. &quot;!&quot;) --&gt; hello nay! -- numbers get converted to text automatically local coins = 250 print(&quot;you have &quot; .. coins .. &quot; coins&quot;) --&gt; you have 250 coins print(&quot;hello &quot; + name) throws &quot;attempt to perform arithmetic on a string value&quot; . use .. for text, + for math. they are not interchangeable. modern luau has string interpolation , which is much cleaner. wrap the string in backticks and put variables in curly braces. thedevking&#x27;s videos predate this feature, but you can use it today: print(`hello {name}, you have {coins} coins`) quotes local a = &quot;double quotes&quot; local b = &#x27;single quotes&#x27; -- identical, pick one and stay consistent local c = [[a long string spanning multiple lines]] -- escaping print(&quot;she said \&quot;hi\&quot;&quot;) --&gt; she said &quot;hi&quot; print(&quot;line one\nline two&quot;) --&gt; prints on two lines print(&quot;tab\there&quot;) --&gt; tab here reading the output window every message shows where it came from, like workspace.part.script:3 . that means: the object path, then the line number . click the message and studio jumps straight to that line. make three variables for a player&#x27;s name, level, and coins. print one sentence that uses all three, formatted like: nay is level 7 and has 250 coins. "><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge">2</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">Printing</span><span class="ctag">How to make your code talk to you. print, warn, error, and joining text together.</span></span><span class="cmeta">4:57</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button><button class="yt" type="button" data-vid="IQQWcDNwVRE" data-n="2" data-title="Printing"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M8 5v14l11-7z"/></svg>Watch</button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">print</span><span class="chip">warn</span><span class="chip">error</span><span class="chip">output</span><span class="chip">strings</span></div><h3 class="bh">The three output functions</h3><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Color in Output</th><th>Stops the script?</th><th>When to use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>print(...)</td><td>White</td><td>No</td><td>Normal debugging. "Did this line run? What's this value?"</td></tr><tr><td>warn(...)</td><td>Yellow</td><td>No</td><td>Something is off but survivable. Missing optional data, fallback used.</td></tr><tr><td>error(...)</td><td>Red</td><td><b>Yes</b></td><td>Something is genuinely broken and continuing would make it worse.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Normal message"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-api">warn</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Something looks wrong"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-api">error</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Everything is broken"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-com">-- script stops here</span>

<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"This line never runs"</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Printing more than one thing</h3><p>Separate values with commas. Roblox prints them all, separated by spaces. You can mix types freely &mdash; numbers, strings, booleans, even whole objects.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Health:"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">100</span><span class="c-op">)</span>              <span class="c-com">--&gt; Health: 100</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Alive?"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span><span class="c-op">)</span>              <span class="c-com">--&gt; Alive? true</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Part:"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-api">workspace</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Baseplate<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-com">--&gt; Part: Baseplate</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">2</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">3</span><span class="c-op">)</span>                     <span class="c-com">--&gt; 1 2 3</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Joining text together (concatenation)</h3><p>Two dots <code>..</code> glue strings together. This is <b>not</b> a plus sign &mdash; that's the #1 beginner mistake here.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Nay"</span>

<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Hello "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> name <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">"!"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">--&gt; Hello Nay!</span>

<span class="c-com">-- numbers get converted to text automatically</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> coins <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">250</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"You have "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> coins <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" coins"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-com">--&gt; You have 250 coins</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div><code>print("Hello " + name)</code> throws <i>"attempt to perform arithmetic on a string value"</i>. Use <code>..</code> for text, <code>+</code> for math. They are not interchangeable.</div></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>Modern Luau has <b>string interpolation</b>, which is much cleaner. Wrap the string in backticks and put variables in curly braces. TheDevKing's videos predate this feature, but you can use it today:<br><code>print(&#96;Hello {name}, you have {coins} coins&#96;)</code></div></div><h3 class="bh">Quotes</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> a <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"double quotes"</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> b <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">'single quotes'</span>          <span class="c-com">-- identical, pick one and stay consistent</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> c <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">[[a long string
spanning multiple lines]]</span>

<span class="c-com">-- escaping</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"She said \"hi\""</span><span class="c-op">)</span>           <span class="c-com">--&gt; She said "hi"</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Line one\nLine two"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>        <span class="c-com">--&gt; prints on two lines</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Tab\there"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>                 <span class="c-com">--&gt; Tab    here</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Reading the Output window</h3><p>Every message shows where it came from, like <code>Workspace.Part.Script:3</code>. That means: the object path, then the <b>line number</b>. Click the message and Studio jumps straight to that line.</p><div class="callout try"><span class="ci">&#9654;</span><div><b>Try it yourself &mdash; </b>Make three variables for a player's name, level, and coins. Print one sentence that uses all three, formatted like: <code>Nay is level 7 and has 250 coins.</code></div></div></div></div></article>
<article class="card" id="ep3" data-n="3" data-search="3 variables storing values in named containers, the local keyword, and every data type you&#x27;ll meet. variables local data types nil making a variable local playername = &quot;nay&quot; local coins = 250 local isalive = true local target = nil print(playername, coins, isalive, target) read local x = 5 as: &quot;make a container named x that holds 5.&quot; the = is assignment , not &quot;equals&quot; — comparing uses == (two equals signs), which you&#x27;ll meet in episode 8. local vs global leaving off local makes a global variable, visible to the entire script (and slower to access). leaving it on makes a local , visible only inside the block it was created in. local speed = 16 -- good: a local. use this 99% of the time. myspeed = 16 -- no &#x27;local&#x27; anywhere = a global. avoid. speed = 20 -- careful: this just reassigns the local above, -- it does not make a global rule of thumb: type local every single time until you have a specific reason not to. globals cause name collisions and are measurably slower in luau. naming rules rule legal illegal letters, digits, underscores only playerhealth , _temp , gun2 player-health , gun#2 cannot start with a digit slot1 1slot case sensitive coins and coins are two different variables no reserved words myend end , local , then , function convention in roblox: camelcase for variables and functions, pascalcase for classes and modulescripts, all_caps for constants that never change. every data type you will meet type example notes nil local x = nil &quot;nothing here.&quot; an unassigned variable is nil. deleting a table key sets it to nil. boolean true / false only two possible values. number 7 , -3.5 , 1e6 lua has one number type. no separate int/float. string &quot;hello&quot; text. immutable — string functions return new strings. table {1, 2, 3} lists and dictionaries. episode 18. function local f = function() end functions are values you can store and pass around. episode 5. instance workspace.baseplate any object in explorer: part, model, script, folder, humanoid... vector3 vector3.new(0, 10, 0) a 3d point or direction. used by position and size. vector2 vector2.new(5, 5) a 2d point. used by mouse and screen positions. cframe cframe.new(0, 10, 0) position plus rotation. how you really move parts. color3 color3.fromrgb(255, 0, 0) a color, 0-255 per channel. use for part.color. brickcolor brickcolor.new(&quot;bright red&quot;) the old named-color palette. still works. udim2 udim2.new(0.5, 0, 0.5, 0) gui size/position: scale + offset, for x and y. enumitem enum.material.neon a fixed option from a roblox-provided list. checking a type print(type(5)) --&gt; number print(type(&quot;hi&quot;)) --&gt; string print(type(workspace.baseplate)) --&gt; userdata (not helpful!) -- typeof() is the roblox version and is what you actually want print(typeof(workspace.baseplate)) --&gt; instance print(typeof(vector3.new(0,0,0))) --&gt; vector3 print(typeof(color3.new())) --&gt; color3 reassigning local coins = 100 print(coins) --&gt; 100 coins = 250 -- no &#x27;local&#x27; the second time! print(coins) --&gt; 250 local coins = 999 -- this makes a second variable that shadows the first writing local again creates a brand-new variable rather than updating the old one. inside the same block that&#x27;s harmless but confusing; across blocks it causes real bugs. make a variable holding a part from workspace ( local part = workspace.baseplate ), then print the part&#x27;s name, size, and whether it&#x27;s anchored."><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge">3</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">Variables</span><span class="ctag">Storing values in named containers, the local keyword, and every data type you&#x27;ll meet.</span></span><span class="cmeta">4:58</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button><button class="yt" type="button" data-vid="0Dc2dCYoxxs" data-n="3" data-title="Variables"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M8 5v14l11-7z"/></svg>Watch</button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">variables</span><span class="chip">local</span><span class="chip">data types</span><span class="chip">nil</span></div><h3 class="bh">Making a variable</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> playerName <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Nay"</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> coins <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">250</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> isAlive <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> target <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">nil</span>

<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>playerName<span class="c-op">,</span> coins<span class="c-op">,</span> isAlive<span class="c-op">,</span> target<span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><p>Read <code>local x = 5</code> as: "make a container named x that holds 5." The <code>=</code> is <b>assignment</b>, not "equals" &mdash; comparing uses <code>==</code> (two equals signs), which you'll meet in Episode 8.</p><h3 class="bh">local vs global</h3><p>Leaving off <code>local</code> makes a <b>global</b> variable, visible to the entire script (and slower to access). Leaving it on makes a <b>local</b>, visible only inside the block it was created in.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> speed <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">16</span>   <span class="c-com">-- GOOD: a local. Use this 99% of the time.</span>
mySpeed <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">16</span>       <span class="c-com">-- no 'local' anywhere = a GLOBAL. Avoid.</span>

speed <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">20</span>         <span class="c-com">-- careful: this just REASSIGNS the local above,</span>
                   <span class="c-com">-- it does not make a global</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>Rule of thumb: type <code>local</code> every single time until you have a specific reason not to. Globals cause name collisions and are measurably slower in Luau.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Naming rules</h3><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Legal</th><th>Illegal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Letters, digits, underscores only</td><td><code>playerHealth</code>, <code>_temp</code>, <code>gun2</code></td><td><code>player-health</code>, <code>gun#2</code></td></tr><tr><td>Cannot start with a digit</td><td><code>slot1</code></td><td><code>1slot</code></td></tr><tr><td>Case sensitive</td><td><code>Coins</code> and <code>coins</code> are two different variables</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>No reserved words</td><td><code>myEnd</code></td><td><code>end</code>, <code>local</code>, <code>then</code>, <code>function</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Convention in Roblox: <code>camelCase</code> for variables and functions, <code>PascalCase</code> for classes and ModuleScripts, <code>ALL_CAPS</code> for constants that never change.</p><h3 class="bh">Every data type you will meet</h3><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Example</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>nil</td><td><code>local x = nil</code></td><td>"Nothing here." An unassigned variable is nil. Deleting a table key sets it to nil.</td></tr><tr><td>boolean</td><td><code>true</code> / <code>false</code></td><td>Only two possible values.</td></tr><tr><td>number</td><td><code>7</code>, <code>-3.5</code>, <code>1e6</code></td><td>Lua has one number type. No separate int/float.</td></tr><tr><td>string</td><td><code>"hello"</code></td><td>Text. Immutable &mdash; string functions return new strings.</td></tr><tr><td>table</td><td><code>{1, 2, 3}</code></td><td>Lists and dictionaries. Episode 18.</td></tr><tr><td>function</td><td><code>local f = function() end</code></td><td>Functions are values you can store and pass around. Episode 5.</td></tr><tr><td>Instance</td><td><code>workspace.Baseplate</code></td><td>Any object in Explorer: Part, Model, Script, Folder, Humanoid...</td></tr><tr><td>Vector3</td><td><code>Vector3.new(0, 10, 0)</code></td><td>A 3D point or direction. Used by Position and Size.</td></tr><tr><td>Vector2</td><td><code>Vector2.new(5, 5)</code></td><td>A 2D point. Used by mouse and screen positions.</td></tr><tr><td>CFrame</td><td><code>CFrame.new(0, 10, 0)</code></td><td>Position <i>plus</i> rotation. How you really move parts.</td></tr><tr><td>Color3</td><td><code>Color3.fromRGB(255, 0, 0)</code></td><td>A color, 0-255 per channel. Use for Part.Color.</td></tr><tr><td>BrickColor</td><td><code>BrickColor.new("Bright red")</code></td><td>The old named-color palette. Still works.</td></tr><tr><td>UDim2</td><td><code>UDim2.new(0.5, 0, 0.5, 0)</code></td><td>GUI size/position: scale + offset, for X and Y.</td></tr><tr><td>EnumItem</td><td><code>Enum.Material.Neon</code></td><td>A fixed option from a Roblox-provided list.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class="bh">Checking a type</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">type</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">5</span><span class="c-op">))</span>                    <span class="c-com">--&gt; number</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">type</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"hi"</span><span class="c-op">))</span>                 <span class="c-com">--&gt; string</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">type</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">workspace</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Baseplate<span class="c-op">))</span>  <span class="c-com">--&gt; userdata  (not helpful!)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- typeof() is the Roblox version and is what you actually want</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">typeof</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">workspace</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Baseplate<span class="c-op">))</span> <span class="c-com">--&gt; Instance</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">typeof</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">Vector3</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">,</span><span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">,</span><span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">)))</span>  <span class="c-com">--&gt; Vector3</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">typeof</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">Color3</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">()))</span>        <span class="c-com">--&gt; Color3</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Reassigning</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> coins <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">100</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>coins<span class="c-op">)</span>  <span class="c-com">--&gt; 100</span>

coins <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">250</span>        <span class="c-com">-- no 'local' the second time!</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>coins<span class="c-op">)</span>  <span class="c-com">--&gt; 250</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> coins <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">999</span>  <span class="c-com">-- this makes a SECOND variable that shadows the first</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>Writing <code>local</code> again creates a brand-new variable rather than updating the old one. Inside the same block that's harmless but confusing; across blocks it causes real bugs.</div></div><div class="callout try"><span class="ci">&#9654;</span><div><b>Try it yourself &mdash; </b>Make a variable holding a Part from Workspace (<code>local part = workspace.Baseplate</code>), then print the part's Name, Size, and whether it's Anchored.</div></div></div></div></article>
<article class="card" id="ep4" data-n="4" data-search="4 arithmetic + object-oriented programming doing math, and reaching into an object to read or change its properties with the dot. math operators properties parts enum arithmetic operators operator meaning example result + add 5 + 3 8 - subtract 5 - 3 2 * multiply 5 * 3 15 / divide 7 / 2 3.5 // floor divide (luau) 7 // 2 3 % modulo / remainder 7 % 2 1 ^ exponent 2 ^ 10 1024 - negate -x opposite sign order of operations is standard math: ^ first, then * / // % , then + - . parentheses override everything. print(2 + 3 * 4) --&gt; 14 (multiply happens first) print((2 + 3) * 4) --&gt; 20 print(2 ^ 3 ^ 2) --&gt; 512 (exponent is right-associative: 2^(3^2)) % is more useful than it looks. i % 2 == 0 tests if a number is even. i % 5 == 0 fires every 5th loop. angle % 360 wraps a rotation. compound assignment (luau) local coins = 100 coins = coins + 50 -- the long way coins += 50 -- the luau way, identical result coins -= 10 coins *= 2 coins /= 4 local msg = &quot;hello&quot; msg ..= &quot; world&quot; -- works for concatenation too numbers and strings local text = &quot;42&quot; local num = tonumber(text) --&gt; 42 (a real number now) print(num + 8) --&gt; 50 local back = tostring(num) --&gt; &quot;42&quot; (a string again) print(tonumber(&quot;banana&quot;)) --&gt; nil (conversion failed) tonumber() returns nil when it can&#x27;t convert. always check the result before doing math with it, or you&#x27;ll get &quot;attempt to perform arithmetic on a nil value&quot; . object-oriented: reading and changing properties every object in explorer has properties. the dot . reaches inside an object to get at one. reading and writing use the exact same syntax. local part = workspace.baseplate -- reading print(part.name) print(part.size) print(part.anchored) -- writing part.name = &quot;floor&quot; part.transparency = 0.5 part.anchored = true part.cancollide = false anything you see in the properties window, you can set in a script using the exact name shown there. that&#x27;s the whole trick. script.parent every script knows what object it&#x27;s inside. script refers to the script itself; script.parent is the thing containing it. this is how you write a script that affects the part it lives in. -- put this script inside a part in workspace local part = script.parent part.brickcolor = brickcolor.new(&quot;bright red&quot;) part.material = enum.material.neon part.anchored = true the property types you&#x27;ll set most local part = script.parent -- vector3: three numbers (x, y, z) part.size = vector3.new(10, 1, 10) part.position = vector3.new(0, 20, 0) -- color3: fromrgb takes 0-255 (color3.new takes 0-1 -- easy trap) part.color = color3.fromrgb(255, 100, 0) -- brickcolor: the classic named palette part.brickcolor = brickcolor.new(&quot;really blue&quot;) -- enum: a fixed list of options roblox provides part.material = enum.material.neon part.shape = enum.parttype.ball -- booleans part.anchored = true -- ignores gravity, won&#x27;t move part.cancollide = false -- players walk through it part.cantouch = true -- can still fire .touched -- numbers 0 to 1 part.transparency = 0.5 part.reflectance = 0.2 not sure what enum options exist? type enum.material. in studio and autocomplete lists every single one. same for enum.keycode. , enum.easingstyle. , and the rest. position vs cframe -- position moves a part but keeps its rotation part.position = vector3.new(0, 50, 0) -- cframe is position and rotation together part.cframe = cframe.new(0, 50, 0) -- move relative to where it already is part.position = part.position + vector3.new(0, 10, 0) -- rotate 90 degrees around y part.cframe = part.cframe * cframe.angles(0, math.rad(90), 0) setting position on an unanchored part gets undone instantly by physics. anchor the part, or use a constraint, or accept that gravity wins. write a script inside a part that makes it neon green, 3x3x3 studs, anchored, floating 20 studs in the air, and 30% transparent."><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge">4</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">Arithmetic + Object-Oriented Programming</span><span class="ctag">Doing math, and reaching into an object to read or change its properties with the dot.</span></span><span class="cmeta">9:25</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button><button class="yt" type="button" data-vid="Ml934kVACbM" data-n="4" data-title="Arithmetic + Object-Oriented Programming"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M8 5v14l11-7z"/></svg>Watch</button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">math</span><span class="chip">operators</span><span class="chip">properties</span><span class="chip">parts</span><span class="chip">enum</span></div><h3 class="bh">Arithmetic operators</h3><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>+</td><td>add</td><td><code>5 + 3</code></td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>subtract</td><td><code>5 - 3</code></td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>*</td><td>multiply</td><td><code>5 * 3</code></td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>/</td><td>divide</td><td><code>7 / 2</code></td><td>3.5</td></tr><tr><td>//</td><td>floor divide (Luau)</td><td><code>7 // 2</code></td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>%</td><td>modulo / remainder</td><td><code>7 % 2</code></td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>^</td><td>exponent</td><td><code>2 ^ 10</code></td><td>1024</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>negate</td><td><code>-x</code></td><td>opposite sign</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Order of operations is standard math: <code>^</code> first, then <code>* / // %</code>, then <code>+ -</code>. Parentheses override everything.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">2</span> <span class="c-op">+</span> <span class="c-num">3</span> <span class="c-op">*</span> <span class="c-num">4</span><span class="c-op">)</span>     <span class="c-com">--&gt; 14  (multiply happens first)</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">((</span><span class="c-num">2</span> <span class="c-op">+</span> <span class="c-num">3</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-op">*</span> <span class="c-num">4</span><span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">--&gt; 20</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">2</span> <span class="c-op">^</span> <span class="c-num">3</span> <span class="c-op">^</span> <span class="c-num">2</span><span class="c-op">)</span>     <span class="c-com">--&gt; 512 (exponent is right-associative: 2^(3^2))</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div><code>%</code> is more useful than it looks. <code>i % 2 == 0</code> tests if a number is even. <code>i % 5 == 0</code> fires every 5th loop. <code>angle % 360</code> wraps a rotation.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Compound assignment (Luau)</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> coins <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">100</span>

coins <span class="c-op">=</span> coins <span class="c-op">+</span> <span class="c-num">50</span>   <span class="c-com">-- the long way</span>
coins <span class="c-op">+=</span> <span class="c-num">50</span>          <span class="c-com">-- the Luau way, identical result</span>

coins <span class="c-op">-=</span> <span class="c-num">10</span>
coins <span class="c-op">*=</span> <span class="c-num">2</span>
coins <span class="c-op">/=</span> <span class="c-num">4</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> msg <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Hello"</span>
msg <span class="c-op">..=</span> <span class="c-str">" world"</span>     <span class="c-com">-- works for concatenation too</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Numbers and strings</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> text <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"42"</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> num <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">tonumber</span><span class="c-op">(</span>text<span class="c-op">)</span>     <span class="c-com">--&gt; 42 (a real number now)</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>num <span class="c-op">+</span> <span class="c-num">8</span><span class="c-op">)</span>                 <span class="c-com">--&gt; 50</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> back <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">tostring</span><span class="c-op">(</span>num<span class="c-op">)</span>     <span class="c-com">--&gt; "42" (a string again)</span>

<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">tonumber</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"banana"</span><span class="c-op">))</span>      <span class="c-com">--&gt; nil  (conversion failed)</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div><code>tonumber()</code> returns <b>nil</b> when it can't convert. Always check the result before doing math with it, or you'll get <i>"attempt to perform arithmetic on a nil value"</i>.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Object-Oriented: reading and changing properties</h3><p>Every object in Explorer has properties. The dot <code>.</code> reaches inside an object to get at one. Reading and writing use the exact same syntax.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> part <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">workspace</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Baseplate

<span class="c-com">-- reading</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>part<span class="c-op">.</span>Name<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>part<span class="c-op">.</span>Size<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>part<span class="c-op">.</span>Anchored<span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- writing</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Floor"</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Transparency <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">0.5</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Anchored <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>CanCollide <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">false</span></code></pre></div><p><b>Anything you see in the Properties window, you can set in a script</b> using the exact name shown there. That's the whole trick.</p><h3 class="bh">script.Parent</h3><p>Every Script knows what object it's inside. <code>script</code> refers to the Script itself; <code>script.Parent</code> is the thing containing it. This is how you write a script that affects the part it lives in.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- Put this Script INSIDE a Part in Workspace</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> part <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">script</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Parent

part<span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">BrickColor</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">BrickColor</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Bright red"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Material <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Enum</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Material<span class="c-op">.</span>Neon
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Anchored <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">The property types you'll set most</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> part <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">script</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Parent

<span class="c-com">-- Vector3: three numbers (X, Y, Z)</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Size <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Vector3</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">10</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">10</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Position <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Vector3</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">20</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- Color3: fromRGB takes 0-255 (Color3.new takes 0-1 -- easy trap)</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Color <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Color3</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">fromRGB</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">255</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">100</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- BrickColor: the classic named palette</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">BrickColor</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">BrickColor</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Really blue"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- Enum: a fixed list of options Roblox provides</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Material <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Enum</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Material<span class="c-op">.</span>Neon
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Shape <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Enum</span><span class="c-op">.</span>PartType<span class="c-op">.</span>Ball

<span class="c-com">-- booleans</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Anchored <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span>      <span class="c-com">-- ignores gravity, won't move</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>CanCollide <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">false</span>   <span class="c-com">-- players walk through it</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>CanTouch <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span>      <span class="c-com">-- can still fire .Touched</span>

<span class="c-com">-- numbers 0 to 1</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Transparency <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">0.5</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Reflectance <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">0.2</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>Not sure what Enum options exist? Type <code>Enum.Material.</code> in Studio and autocomplete lists every single one. Same for <code>Enum.KeyCode.</code>, <code>Enum.EasingStyle.</code>, and the rest.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Position vs CFrame</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- Position moves a part but keeps its rotation</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Position <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Vector3</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">50</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- CFrame is position AND rotation together</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">CFrame</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">CFrame</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">50</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- Move relative to where it already is</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Position <span class="c-op">=</span> part<span class="c-op">.</span>Position <span class="c-op">+</span> <span class="c-api">Vector3</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">10</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- Rotate 90 degrees around Y</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">CFrame</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> part<span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">CFrame</span> <span class="c-op">*</span> <span class="c-api">CFrame</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">Angles</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">rad</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">90</span><span class="c-op">),</span> <span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>Setting <code>Position</code> on an <b>unanchored</b> part gets undone instantly by physics. Anchor the part, or use a constraint, or accept that gravity wins.</div></div><div class="callout try"><span class="ci">&#9654;</span><div><b>Try it yourself &mdash; </b>Write a Script inside a Part that makes it neon green, 3x3x3 studs, anchored, floating 20 studs in the air, and 30% transparent.</div></div></div></div></article>
<article class="card" id="ep5" data-n="5" data-search="5 functions packaging code into a named block you can run over and over. functions reuse calling defining and calling local function sayhello() print(&quot;hello!&quot;) end sayhello() -- now it runs sayhello() -- and again sayhello() -- and again defining a function does not run it. it just stores the instructions under a name. nothing happens until you call it with parentheses. sayhello (no parentheses) is the function itself as a value. sayhello() (with parentheses) runs it. mixing these up is extremely common — and, as you&#x27;ll see in episode 10, passing the function without parentheses is exactly what events need. three ways to write the same thing -- 1. local function (use this one) local function greet() print(&quot;hi&quot;) end -- 2. global function function greet() print(&quot;hi&quot;) end -- 3. anonymous function stored in a variable local greet = function() print(&quot;hi&quot;) end prefer form 1. it&#x27;s local (faster, safer) and it lets the function refer to itself for recursion, which form 3 can&#x27;t do cleanly. anonymous functions a function with no name, written inline. you&#x27;ll use these constantly with events and loops. -- passed straight into a connection part.touched:connect(function(hit) print(hit.name .. &quot; touched me&quot;) end) -- stored in a table local actions = { jump = function() print(&quot;jumping&quot;) end, duck = function() print(&quot;ducking&quot;) end, } actions.jump() why functions matter three reasons, in order of importance: don&#x27;t repeat yourself. write the logic once, call it in ten places. fix a bug once instead of ten times. naming is documentation. resetplayerstats() tells you what the block does without reading it. events need them. every :connect() in this series takes a function. you cannot do event-driven code without them. local function makeglowingpart(color) local p = instance.new(&quot;part&quot;) p.material = enum.material.neon p.anchored = true p.brickcolor = brickcolor.new(color) p.parent = workspace return p end makeglowingpart(&quot;really red&quot;) makeglowingpart(&quot;lime green&quot;) makeglowingpart(&quot;electric blue&quot;) order matters sayhi() -- error: attempt to call a nil value local function sayhi() print(&quot;hi&quot;) end a local function must be defined above the line that calls it. scripts run top to bottom; at the moment the call happens, the variable doesn&#x27;t exist yet. define your helpers at the top of the file. write a function called countdown() that prints 3, 2, 1, go! then call it twice."><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge">5</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">Functions</span><span class="ctag">Packaging code into a named block you can run over and over.</span></span><span class="cmeta">5:09</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button><button class="yt" type="button" data-vid="wzobefw_ny0" data-n="5" data-title="Functions"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M8 5v14l11-7z"/></svg>Watch</button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">functions</span><span class="chip">reuse</span><span class="chip">calling</span></div><h3 class="bh">Defining and calling</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">sayHello</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Hello!"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-fn">sayHello</span><span class="c-op">()</span>   <span class="c-com">-- now it runs</span>
<span class="c-fn">sayHello</span><span class="c-op">()</span>   <span class="c-com">-- and again</span>
<span class="c-fn">sayHello</span><span class="c-op">()</span>   <span class="c-com">-- and again</span></code></pre></div><p>Defining a function does <b>not</b> run it. It just stores the instructions under a name. Nothing happens until you <b>call</b> it with parentheses.</p><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div><code>sayHello</code> (no parentheses) is the function itself as a value. <code>sayHello()</code> (with parentheses) runs it. Mixing these up is extremely common &mdash; and, as you'll see in Episode 10, passing the function <i>without</i> parentheses is exactly what events need.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Three ways to write the same thing</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- 1. Local function (use this one)</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">greet</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"hi"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-com">-- 2. Global function</span>
<span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">greet</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"hi"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-com">-- 3. Anonymous function stored in a variable</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> greet <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"hi"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>Prefer form 1. It's local (faster, safer) and it lets the function refer to itself for recursion, which form 3 can't do cleanly.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Anonymous functions</h3><p>A function with no name, written inline. You'll use these constantly with events and loops.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- passed straight into a connection</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Touched<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>hit<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>hit<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" touched me"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- stored in a table</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> actions <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span>
	jump <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">()</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"jumping"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">,</span>
	duck <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">()</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"ducking"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">,</span>
<span class="c-op">}</span>

actions<span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">jump</span><span class="c-op">()</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Why functions matter</h3><p>Three reasons, in order of importance:</p><ul class="bl"><li><b>Don't repeat yourself.</b> Write the logic once, call it in ten places. Fix a bug once instead of ten times.</li><li><b>Naming is documentation.</b> <code>resetPlayerStats()</code> tells you what the block does without reading it.</li><li><b>Events need them.</b> Every <code>:Connect()</code> in this series takes a function. You cannot do event-driven code without them.</li></ul><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">makeGlowingPart</span><span class="c-op">(</span>color<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> p <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Part"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	p<span class="c-op">.</span>Material <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Enum</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Material<span class="c-op">.</span>Neon
	p<span class="c-op">.</span>Anchored <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span>
	p<span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">BrickColor</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">BrickColor</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span>color<span class="c-op">)</span>
	p<span class="c-op">.</span>Parent <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">workspace</span>
	<span class="c-kw">return</span> p
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-fn">makeGlowingPart</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Really red"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-fn">makeGlowingPart</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Lime green"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-fn">makeGlowingPart</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Electric blue"</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Order matters</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-fn">sayHi</span><span class="c-op">()</span>   <span class="c-com">-- ERROR: attempt to call a nil value</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">sayHi</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"hi"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>A local function must be <b>defined above</b> the line that calls it. Scripts run top to bottom; at the moment the call happens, the variable doesn't exist yet. Define your helpers at the top of the file.</div></div><div class="callout try"><span class="ci">&#9654;</span><div><b>Try it yourself &mdash; </b>Write a function called <code>countdown()</code> that prints 3, 2, 1, Go! Then call it twice.</div></div></div></div></article>
<article class="card" id="ep6" data-n="6" data-search="6 scope &amp; returning where a variable can be seen, and how to send a value back out of a function. scope return local blocks what scope means a local variable only exists inside the block where it was created. a block is anything that ends with end (or until ): a function, an if statement, a loop, or a bare do ... end . local outer = &quot;i&#x27;m at the top&quot; local function test() local inner = &quot;i&#x27;m inside&quot; print(outer) -- works: inner code can see outer variables print(inner) -- works end test() print(inner) -- nil! the variable died when the function ended if true then local secret = 42 end print(secret) --&gt; nil (it only lived inside the if block) shadowing local name = &quot;outer&quot; local function show() local name = &quot;inner&quot; -- a new variable, hides the outer one print(name) --&gt; inner end show() print(name) --&gt; outer (untouched) shadowing isn&#x27;t a bug, but it makes code confusing to read. if you find yourself shadowing, a clearer name is usually the better fix. returning a value return sends a value back to whoever called the function, and immediately stops the function . local function double(n) return n * 2 end local result = double(21) print(result) --&gt; 42 print(double(5)) --&gt; 10 local function checkage(age) if age too young print(checkage(20)) --&gt; welcome returning nothing local function noreturn() print(&quot;doing something&quot;) end local x = noreturn() print(x) --&gt; nil (a function with no return gives back nil) multiple return values lua can return several values at once, which is unusual and genuinely useful. local function getstats() return 100, 50, &quot;nay&quot; end local health, mana, name = getstats() print(health, mana, name) --&gt; 100 50 nay -- take only the first local justhealth = getstats() print(justhealth) --&gt; 100 -- skip one with an underscore (a convention, not a rule) local _, justmana = getstats() print(justmana) --&gt; 50 you&#x27;ll meet this constantly with pcall , which returns success plus a result, and with string.find , which returns a start and end index. early return (guard clauses) returning early to bail out on bad input keeps your code flat and readable instead of deeply nested. local function damageplayer(player, amount) if not player then return end if not player.character then return end local humanoid = player.character:findfirstchild(&quot;humanoid&quot;) if not humanoid then return end humanoid.health -= amount end return must be the last statement in its block . return 5 print(&quot;hi&quot;) is a syntax error. if you truly need a bare return mid-block, wrap it: do return end . write a function getfullname(first, last) that returns the two joined with a space, and a second function that returns three values: a player&#x27;s name, level, and coins."><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge">6</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">Scope &amp; Returning</span><span class="ctag">Where a variable can be seen, and how to send a value back out of a function.</span></span><span class="cmeta">7:15</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button><button class="yt" type="button" data-vid="dH-q3QpFvYI" data-n="6" data-title="Scope &amp; Returning"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M8 5v14l11-7z"/></svg>Watch</button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">scope</span><span class="chip">return</span><span class="chip">local</span><span class="chip">blocks</span></div><h3 class="bh">What scope means</h3><p>A local variable only exists inside the <b>block</b> where it was created. A block is anything that ends with <code>end</code> (or <code>until</code>): a function, an if statement, a loop, or a bare <code>do ... end</code>.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> outer <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"I'm at the top"</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">test</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> inner <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"I'm inside"</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>outer<span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">-- WORKS: inner code can see outer variables</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>inner<span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">-- WORKS</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-fn">test</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>inner<span class="c-op">)</span>       <span class="c-com">-- nil! The variable died when the function ended</span></code></pre></div><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> secret <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">42</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>secret<span class="c-op">)</span>  <span class="c-com">--&gt; nil  (it only lived inside the if block)</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Shadowing</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"outer"</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">show</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"inner"</span>   <span class="c-com">-- a NEW variable, hides the outer one</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>name<span class="c-op">)</span>            <span class="c-com">--&gt; inner</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-fn">show</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>name<span class="c-op">)</span>                <span class="c-com">--&gt; outer  (untouched)</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>Shadowing isn't a bug, but it makes code confusing to read. If you find yourself shadowing, a clearer name is usually the better fix.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Returning a value</h3><p><code>return</code> sends a value back to whoever called the function, and <b>immediately stops the function</b>.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">double</span><span class="c-op">(</span>n<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">return</span> n <span class="c-op">*</span> <span class="c-num">2</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> result <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-fn">double</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">21</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>result<span class="c-op">)</span>        <span class="c-com">--&gt; 42</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-fn">double</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">5</span><span class="c-op">))</span>     <span class="c-com">--&gt; 10</span></code></pre></div><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">checkAge</span><span class="c-op">(</span>age<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> age <span class="c-op">&lt;</span> <span class="c-num">13</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		<span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-str">"too young"</span>     <span class="c-com">-- stops right here</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
	<span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-str">"welcome"</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-fn">checkAge</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">10</span><span class="c-op">))</span>  <span class="c-com">--&gt; too young</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-fn">checkAge</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">20</span><span class="c-op">))</span>  <span class="c-com">--&gt; welcome</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Returning nothing</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">noReturn</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"doing something"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> x <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-fn">noReturn</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>x<span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">--&gt; nil  (a function with no return gives back nil)</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Multiple return values</h3><p>Lua can return several values at once, which is unusual and genuinely useful.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">getStats</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
	<span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-num">100</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">50</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"Nay"</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> health<span class="c-op">,</span> mana<span class="c-op">,</span> name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-fn">getStats</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>health<span class="c-op">,</span> mana<span class="c-op">,</span> name<span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">--&gt; 100 50 Nay</span>

<span class="c-com">-- take only the first</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> justHealth <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-fn">getStats</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>justHealth<span class="c-op">)</span>           <span class="c-com">--&gt; 100</span>

<span class="c-com">-- skip one with an underscore (a convention, not a rule)</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> _<span class="c-op">,</span> justMana <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-fn">getStats</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>justMana<span class="c-op">)</span>             <span class="c-com">--&gt; 50</span></code></pre></div><p>You'll meet this constantly with <code>pcall</code>, which returns success plus a result, and with <code>string.find</code>, which returns a start and end index.</p><h3 class="bh">Early return (guard clauses)</h3><p>Returning early to bail out on bad input keeps your code flat and readable instead of deeply nested.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">damagePlayer</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">,</span> amount<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-kw">not</span> player <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-kw">not</span> player<span class="c-op">.</span>Character <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>

	<span class="c-kw">local</span> humanoid <span class="c-op">=</span> player<span class="c-op">.</span>Character<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">FindFirstChild</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Humanoid"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-kw">not</span> humanoid <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>

	humanoid<span class="c-op">.</span>Health <span class="c-op">-=</span> amount
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div><code>return</code> must be the <b>last statement in its block</b>. <code>return 5 print("hi")</code> is a syntax error. If you truly need a bare return mid-block, wrap it: <code>do return end</code>.</div></div><div class="callout try"><span class="ci">&#9654;</span><div><b>Try it yourself &mdash; </b>Write a function <code>getFullName(first, last)</code> that returns the two joined with a space, and a second function that returns three values: a player's name, level, and coins.</div></div></div></div></article>
<article class="card" id="ep7" data-n="7" data-search="7 parameters feeding values into a function so it can do different work each time it&#x27;s called. parameters arguments varargs parameters vs arguments the names in the definition are parameters. the values you pass when calling are arguments. same thing from two sides. -- v-- these are parameters local function greet(name, greeting) print(greeting .. &quot;, &quot; .. name .. &quot;!&quot;) end greet(&quot;nay&quot;, &quot;hello&quot;) --&gt; hello, nay! -- ^-- these are arguments order matters. the first argument lands in the first parameter, the second in the second, and so on. names don&#x27;t have to match anything. too few or too many arguments local function show(a, b, c) print(a, b, c) end show(1) --&gt; 1 nil nil (missing ones become nil) show(1, 2, 3, 4) --&gt; 1 2 3 (extras are silently dropped) lua will not stop you from calling a function with the wrong number of arguments. missing values silently become nil, which usually blows up a few lines later as &quot;attempt to index nil&quot; . this is the most common source of confusing beginner errors. default values lua has no built-in default parameters, so you fake it with or . if the argument is nil, the fallback is used. local function jump(power) power = power or 50 -- default to 50 print(&quot;jumping with power &quot; .. power) end jump() --&gt; jumping with power 50 jump(120) --&gt; jumping with power 120 this trick breaks for false and 0 … actually only for false . in lua, 0 is truthy, so 0 or 50 gives 0 correctly. but false or true gives true , so never use or defaults for boolean parameters. use an explicit nil check instead: if enabled == nil then enabled = true end passing objects and functions local function paintpart(part, color) part.brickcolor = brickcolor.new(color) end paintpart(workspace.baseplate, &quot;really red&quot;) -- functions can be arguments too local function dotwice(fn) fn() fn() end dotwice(function() print(&quot;hello&quot;) end) varargs: accepting any number of arguments local function printall(...) print(...) -- pass them straight through local args = {...} -- or collect into a table print(&quot;got &quot; .. #args .. &quot; arguments&quot;) end printall(&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;, &quot;c&quot;) --&gt; a b c / got 3 arguments -- mix named parameters with varargs (named ones must come first) local function log(prefix, ...) print(&quot;[&quot; .. prefix .. &quot;]&quot;, ...) end log(&quot;combat&quot;, &quot;player hit for&quot;, 25) --&gt; [combat] player hit for 25 tables are passed by reference local function additem(inventory, item) table.insert(inventory, item) end local mybag = {&quot;sword&quot;} additem(mybag, &quot;shield&quot;) print(#mybag) --&gt; 2 (the original table was modified!) numbers, strings, and booleans are copied when passed. tables and instances are not — the function gets a reference to the same object, so changes made inside are visible outside. this trips people up in both directions. write calculatedamage(base, multiplier, critical) where multiplier defaults to 1 and critical defaults to false. return the final damage, doubled if critical."><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge">7</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">Parameters</span><span class="ctag">Feeding values into a function so it can do different work each time it&#x27;s called.</span></span><span class="cmeta">4:55</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button><button class="yt" type="button" data-vid="u_ISBaMhrLA" data-n="7" data-title="Parameters"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M8 5v14l11-7z"/></svg>Watch</button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">parameters</span><span class="chip">arguments</span><span class="chip">varargs</span></div><h3 class="bh">Parameters vs arguments</h3><p>The names in the <b>definition</b> are parameters. The values you pass when <b>calling</b> are arguments. Same thing from two sides.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">--                 v-- these are PARAMETERS</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">greet</span><span class="c-op">(</span>name<span class="c-op">,</span> greeting<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>greeting <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">", "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> name <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">"!"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-fn">greet</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Nay"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"Hello"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">--&gt; Hello, Nay!</span>
<span class="c-com">--    ^-- these are ARGUMENTS</span></code></pre></div><p>Order matters. The first argument lands in the first parameter, the second in the second, and so on. Names don't have to match anything.</p><h3 class="bh">Too few or too many arguments</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">show</span><span class="c-op">(</span>a<span class="c-op">,</span> b<span class="c-op">,</span> c<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>a<span class="c-op">,</span> b<span class="c-op">,</span> c<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-fn">show</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">)</span>           <span class="c-com">--&gt; 1   nil  nil   (missing ones become nil)</span>
<span class="c-fn">show</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">2</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">3</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">4</span><span class="c-op">)</span>  <span class="c-com">--&gt; 1   2    3     (extras are silently dropped)</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>Lua will <b>not</b> stop you from calling a function with the wrong number of arguments. Missing values silently become nil, which usually blows up a few lines later as <i>"attempt to index nil"</i>. This is the most common source of confusing beginner errors.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Default values</h3><p>Lua has no built-in default parameters, so you fake it with <code>or</code>. If the argument is nil, the fallback is used.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">jump</span><span class="c-op">(</span>power<span class="c-op">)</span>
	power <span class="c-op">=</span> power <span class="c-kw">or</span> <span class="c-num">50</span>   <span class="c-com">-- default to 50</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Jumping with power "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> power<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-fn">jump</span><span class="c-op">()</span>     <span class="c-com">--&gt; Jumping with power 50</span>
<span class="c-fn">jump</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">120</span><span class="c-op">)</span>  <span class="c-com">--&gt; Jumping with power 120</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>This trick breaks for <code>false</code> and <code>0</code>&hellip; actually only for <code>false</code>. In Lua, <code>0</code> is truthy, so <code>0 or 50</code> gives <code>0</code> correctly. But <code>false or true</code> gives <code>true</code>, so never use <code>or</code> defaults for boolean parameters. Use an explicit nil check instead:<br><code>if enabled == nil then enabled = true end</code></div></div><h3 class="bh">Passing objects and functions</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">paintPart</span><span class="c-op">(</span>part<span class="c-op">,</span> color<span class="c-op">)</span>
	part<span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">BrickColor</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">BrickColor</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span>color<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-fn">paintPart</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">workspace</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Baseplate<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"Really red"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- functions can be arguments too</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">doTwice</span><span class="c-op">(</span>fn<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-fn">fn</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
	<span class="c-fn">fn</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-fn">doTwice</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"hello"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Varargs: accepting any number of arguments</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">printAll</span><span class="c-op">(...)</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(...)</span>                 <span class="c-com">-- pass them straight through</span>

	<span class="c-kw">local</span> args <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{...}</span>         <span class="c-com">-- or collect into a table</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Got "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-op">#</span>args <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" arguments"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-fn">printAll</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"a"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"b"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"c"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">--&gt; a b c  /  Got 3 arguments</span></code></pre></div><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- mix named parameters with varargs (named ones must come first)</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">log</span><span class="c-op">(</span>prefix<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-op">...)</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"["</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> prefix <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">"]"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-op">...)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-fn">log</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"COMBAT"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"player hit for"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">25</span><span class="c-op">)</span>  <span class="c-com">--&gt; [COMBAT] player hit for 25</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Tables are passed by reference</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">addItem</span><span class="c-op">(</span>inventory<span class="c-op">,</span> item<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-api">table</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">insert</span><span class="c-op">(</span>inventory<span class="c-op">,</span> item<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> myBag <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span><span class="c-str">"sword"</span><span class="c-op">}</span>
<span class="c-fn">addItem</span><span class="c-op">(</span>myBag<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"shield"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(#</span>myBag<span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">--&gt; 2  (the original table was modified!)</span></code></pre></div><p>Numbers, strings, and booleans are copied when passed. Tables and Instances are <b>not</b> &mdash; the function gets a reference to the same object, so changes made inside are visible outside. This trips people up in both directions.</p><div class="callout try"><span class="ci">&#9654;</span><div><b>Try it yourself &mdash; </b>Write <code>calculateDamage(base, multiplier, critical)</code> where multiplier defaults to 1 and critical defaults to false. Return the final damage, doubled if critical.</div></div></div></div></article>
<article class="card" id="ep8" data-n="8" data-search="8 if statements making code run only when something is true. comparison operators and logic. if conditionals comparison logic the basic shape local coins = 100 if coins &gt;= 50 then print(&quot;you can afford the sword!&quot;) end read it as: if this condition is true, then run everything up to end . if it&#x27;s false, skip the whole block. comparison operators operator meaning example true when == equal to a == b ~= not equal to a ~= b &gt; greater than a &gt; b &lt; less than a &lt; b &gt;= greater than or equal a &gt;= b &lt;= less than or equal a &lt;= b lua uses ~= for &quot;not equal&quot;, not != . and = assigns while == compares — writing if x = 5 then is a syntax error. truthiness: the rule that surprises everyone in lua, only false and nil are falsy . every other value — including 0 and the empty string &quot;&quot; — counts as true. if 0 then print(&quot;runs!&quot;) end -- yes, this prints if &quot;&quot; then print(&quot;runs!&quot;) end -- yes, this prints if nil then print(&quot;never&quot;) end -- no if false then print(&quot;never&quot;) end -- no this is different from javascript and python, where 0 is falsy. in lua you must compare explicitly: if health == 0 then print(&quot;dead&quot;) end the most useful consequence: if part then is a clean way to check &quot;does this object exist?&quot;, because a missing object is nil. logical operators operator meaning result and both must be true true and true → true or at least one must be true false or true → true not flips it not true → false local coins = 100 local level = 5 if coins &gt;= 50 and level &gt;= 3 then print(&quot;you qualify!&quot;) end if coins and / or short-circuit : if the left side already decides the answer, the right side is never evaluated. that makes this safe pattern possible: -- if humanoid is nil, the second half never runs, so no error if humanoid and humanoid.health &gt; 0 then print(&quot;still alive&quot;) end nesting local player = game.players.localplayer if player then if player.character then local humanoid = player.character:findfirstchild(&quot;humanoid&quot;) if humanoid then print(&quot;health: &quot; .. humanoid.health) end end end that pyramid is why guard clauses (episode 6) exist. the same logic flattened: if not player then return end … and so on, one check per line. comparing objects local part = workspace.baseplate if part.name == &quot;baseplate&quot; then print(&quot;found it&quot;) end if part.anchored then print(&quot;it&#x27;s anchored&quot;) end -- already a boolean if part:isa(&quot;basepart&quot;) then print(&quot;it&#x27;s a part&quot;) end -- class check if part.material == enum.material.neon then print(&quot;glowing&quot;) end comparing a number to a string never errors — it&#x27;s just always false. if &quot;5&quot; == 5 then is false . use tonumber() when data comes from a textbox or a string value. write a script that checks a player&#x27;s coins and prints &quot;gold&quot;, &quot;silver&quot;, or &quot;nothing&quot; — using only if blocks (no else yet). then check whether a part is both anchored and transparent."><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge">8</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">If Statements</span><span class="ctag">Making code run only when something is true. Comparison operators and logic.</span></span><span class="cmeta">7:40</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button><button class="yt" type="button" data-vid="5iQTzOaMjwE" data-n="8" data-title="If Statements"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M8 5v14l11-7z"/></svg>Watch</button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">if</span><span class="chip">conditionals</span><span class="chip">comparison</span><span class="chip">logic</span></div><h3 class="bh">The basic shape</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> coins <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">100</span>

<span class="c-kw">if</span> coins <span class="c-op">&gt;=</span> <span class="c-num">50</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"You can afford the sword!"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><p>Read it as: <b>if</b> this condition is true, <b>then</b> run everything up to <b>end</b>. If it's false, skip the whole block.</p><h3 class="bh">Comparison operators</h3><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example true when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>==</td><td>equal to</td><td><code>a == b</code></td></tr><tr><td>~=</td><td>NOT equal to</td><td><code>a ~= b</code></td></tr><tr><td>&gt;</td><td>greater than</td><td><code>a &gt; b</code></td></tr><tr><td>&lt;</td><td>less than</td><td><code>a &lt; b</code></td></tr><tr><td>&gt;=</td><td>greater than or equal</td><td><code>a &gt;= b</code></td></tr><tr><td>&lt;=</td><td>less than or equal</td><td><code>a &lt;= b</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>Lua uses <code>~=</code> for "not equal", <b>not</b> <code>!=</code>. And <code>=</code> assigns while <code>==</code> compares &mdash; writing <code>if x = 5 then</code> is a syntax error.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Truthiness: the rule that surprises everyone</h3><p>In Lua, <b>only <code>false</code> and <code>nil</code> are falsy</b>. Every other value &mdash; including <code>0</code> and the empty string <code>""</code> &mdash; counts as true.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-num">0</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"runs!"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>          <span class="c-com">-- YES, this prints</span>
<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-str">""</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"runs!"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>         <span class="c-com">-- YES, this prints</span>
<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-kw">nil</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"never"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>        <span class="c-com">-- no</span>
<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-kw">false</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"never"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>      <span class="c-com">-- no</span></code></pre></div><p>This is different from JavaScript and Python, where 0 is falsy. In Lua you must compare explicitly:</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">if</span> health <span class="c-op">==</span> <span class="c-num">0</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"dead"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>The most useful consequence: <code>if part then</code> is a clean way to check "does this object exist?", because a missing object is nil.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Logical operators</h3><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>and</td><td>both must be true</td><td><code>true and true</code> &rarr; true</td></tr><tr><td>or</td><td>at least one must be true</td><td><code>false or true</code> &rarr; true</td></tr><tr><td>not</td><td>flips it</td><td><code>not true</code> &rarr; false</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> coins <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">100</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> level <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">5</span>

<span class="c-kw">if</span> coins <span class="c-op">&gt;=</span> <span class="c-num">50</span> <span class="c-kw">and</span> level <span class="c-op">&gt;=</span> <span class="c-num">3</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"You qualify!"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-kw">if</span> coins <span class="c-op">&lt;</span> <span class="c-num">10</span> <span class="c-kw">or</span> level <span class="c-op">&lt;</span> <span class="c-num">2</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"You need more of something"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-kw">not</span> isBanned <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Welcome in"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><p><code>and</code> / <code>or</code> <b>short-circuit</b>: if the left side already decides the answer, the right side is never evaluated. That makes this safe pattern possible:</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- if humanoid is nil, the second half never runs, so no error</span>
<span class="c-kw">if</span> humanoid <span class="c-kw">and</span> humanoid<span class="c-op">.</span>Health <span class="c-op">&gt;</span> <span class="c-num">0</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"still alive"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Nesting</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> player <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">Players</span><span class="c-op">.</span>LocalPlayer

<span class="c-kw">if</span> player <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> player<span class="c-op">.</span>Character <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		<span class="c-kw">local</span> humanoid <span class="c-op">=</span> player<span class="c-op">.</span>Character<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">FindFirstChild</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Humanoid"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
		<span class="c-kw">if</span> humanoid <span class="c-kw">then</span>
			<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Health: "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> humanoid<span class="c-op">.</span>Health<span class="c-op">)</span>
		<span class="c-kw">end</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>That pyramid is why guard clauses (Episode 6) exist. The same logic flattened:<br><code>if not player then return end</code> &hellip; and so on, one check per line.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Comparing objects</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> part <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">workspace</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Baseplate

<span class="c-kw">if</span> part<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">==</span> <span class="c-str">"Baseplate"</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"found it"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">if</span> part<span class="c-op">.</span>Anchored <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"it's anchored"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>          <span class="c-com">-- already a boolean</span>
<span class="c-kw">if</span> part<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">IsA</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"BasePart"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"it's a part"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>     <span class="c-com">-- class check</span>
<span class="c-kw">if</span> part<span class="c-op">.</span>Material <span class="c-op">==</span> <span class="c-api">Enum</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Material<span class="c-op">.</span>Neon <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"glowing"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>Comparing a number to a string never errors &mdash; it's just always false. <code>if "5" == 5 then</code> is <b>false</b>. Use <code>tonumber()</code> when data comes from a TextBox or a string value.</div></div><div class="callout try"><span class="ci">&#9654;</span><div><b>Try it yourself &mdash; </b>Write a script that checks a player's coins and prints "gold", "silver", or "nothing" &mdash; using only <code>if</code> blocks (no else yet). Then check whether a part is both Anchored and Transparent.</div></div></div></div></article>
<article class="card" id="ep9" data-n="9" data-search="9 else &amp; elseif statements handling the other cases. chaining conditions cleanly. else elseif conditionals else local coins = 20 if coins &gt;= 50 then print(&quot;you can afford it&quot;) else print(&quot;not enough coins&quot;) end else catches everything the if didn&#x27;t. exactly one of the two branches always runs. there&#x27;s no then after else . elseif local score = 85 if score &gt;= 90 then print(&quot;a&quot;) elseif score &gt;= 80 then print(&quot;b&quot;) elseif score &gt;= 70 then print(&quot;c&quot;) elseif score &gt;= 60 then print(&quot;d&quot;) else print(&quot;f&quot;) end --&gt; b luau checks top to bottom and stops at the first match. everything after it is skipped, even if it would also be true. that&#x27;s why the order in the example above works with just &gt;= . it&#x27;s one word: elseif . writing else if starts a whole new nested if statement, which then needs its own end — and you&#x27;ll get &quot;expected &#x27;end&#x27;&quot; at the bottom of your file. order matters enormously -- wrong: everything 90+ is also 60+, so this always says d if score &gt;= 60 then print(&quot;d&quot;) elseif score &gt;= 90 then print(&quot;a&quot;) -- unreachable end put the most specific or most restrictive condition first. the whole structure if condition1 then -- runs if condition1 is true elseif condition2 then -- runs if condition1 was false and condition2 is true elseif condition3 then -- and so on else -- runs if none of the above matched end -- one &#x27;end&#x27; for the entire chain, no matter how long if-expressions (modern luau) luau added a version of if that produces a value instead of running statements. much cleaner than the old trick below. local status = if health &gt; 0 then &quot;alive&quot; else &quot;dead&quot; local rank = if score &gt;= 90 then &quot;a&quot; elseif score &gt;= 80 then &quot;b&quot; else &quot;c&quot; the old and/or ternary trick local status = (health &gt; 0) and &quot;alive&quot; or &quot;dead&quot; you&#x27;ll see this everywhere in older code, and it has a real trap: if the middle value is false or nil , you always get the last value. local x = true and false or &quot;oops&quot; gives &quot;oops&quot; , not false . prefer the if-expression above. a realistic example local players = game:getservice(&quot;players&quot;) local function getrank(player) local coins = player.leaderstats.coins.value if coins &gt;= 10000 then return &quot;legend&quot; elseif coins &gt;= 5000 then return &quot;master&quot; elseif coins &gt;= 1000 then return &quot;pro&quot; elseif coins &gt;= 100 then return &quot;novice&quot; else return &quot;newbie&quot; end end when you have more than about five branches all comparing the same variable to fixed values, a table lookup (episode 18) is usually cleaner than a long elseif chain. write a function that takes a humanoid&#x27;s health and returns &quot;healthy&quot; (above 75), &quot;hurt&quot; (25-75), &quot;critical&quot; (1-24), or &quot;dead&quot; (0)."><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge">9</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">Else &amp; Elseif Statements</span><span class="ctag">Handling the other cases. Chaining conditions cleanly.</span></span><span class="cmeta">5:24</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button><button class="yt" type="button" data-vid="H7579TaCFCQ" data-n="9" data-title="Else &amp; Elseif Statements"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M8 5v14l11-7z"/></svg>Watch</button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">else</span><span class="chip">elseif</span><span class="chip">conditionals</span></div><h3 class="bh">else</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> coins <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">20</span>

<span class="c-kw">if</span> coins <span class="c-op">&gt;=</span> <span class="c-num">50</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"You can afford it"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">else</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Not enough coins"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><p><code>else</code> catches everything the <code>if</code> didn't. Exactly one of the two branches always runs. There's no <code>then</code> after <code>else</code>.</p><h3 class="bh">elseif</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> score <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">85</span>

<span class="c-kw">if</span> score <span class="c-op">&gt;=</span> <span class="c-num">90</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"A"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">elseif</span> score <span class="c-op">&gt;=</span> <span class="c-num">80</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"B"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">elseif</span> score <span class="c-op">&gt;=</span> <span class="c-num">70</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"C"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">elseif</span> score <span class="c-op">&gt;=</span> <span class="c-num">60</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"D"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">else</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"F"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-com">--&gt; B</span></code></pre></div><p>Luau checks top to bottom and stops at the <b>first</b> match. Everything after it is skipped, even if it would also be true. That's why the order in the example above works with just <code>&gt;=</code>.</p><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>It's one word: <code>elseif</code>. Writing <code>else if</code> starts a whole new nested if statement, which then needs its own <code>end</code> &mdash; and you'll get <i>"Expected 'end'"</i> at the bottom of your file.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Order matters enormously</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- WRONG: everything 90+ is also 60+, so this always says D</span>
<span class="c-kw">if</span> score <span class="c-op">&gt;=</span> <span class="c-num">60</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"D"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">elseif</span> score <span class="c-op">&gt;=</span> <span class="c-num">90</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"A"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">-- unreachable</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><p>Put the <b>most specific</b> or most restrictive condition first.</p><h3 class="bh">The whole structure</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">if</span> condition1 <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-com">-- runs if condition1 is true</span>
<span class="c-kw">elseif</span> condition2 <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-com">-- runs if condition1 was false AND condition2 is true</span>
<span class="c-kw">elseif</span> condition3 <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-com">-- and so on</span>
<span class="c-kw">else</span>
	<span class="c-com">-- runs if none of the above matched</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-com">-- ONE 'end' for the entire chain, no matter how long</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">if-expressions (modern Luau)</h3><p>Luau added a version of <code>if</code> that produces a value instead of running statements. Much cleaner than the old trick below.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> status <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">if</span> health <span class="c-op">&gt;</span> <span class="c-num">0</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-str">"alive"</span> <span class="c-kw">else</span> <span class="c-str">"dead"</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> rank <span class="c-op">=</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> score <span class="c-op">&gt;=</span> <span class="c-num">90</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-str">"A"</span>
	<span class="c-kw">elseif</span> score <span class="c-op">&gt;=</span> <span class="c-num">80</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-str">"B"</span>
	<span class="c-kw">else</span> <span class="c-str">"C"</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">The old and/or ternary trick</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> status <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">(</span>health <span class="c-op">&gt;</span> <span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">and</span> <span class="c-str">"alive"</span> <span class="c-kw">or</span> <span class="c-str">"dead"</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>You'll see this everywhere in older code, and it has a real trap: if the middle value is <code>false</code> or <code>nil</code>, you always get the last value. <code>local x = true and false or "oops"</code> gives <code>"oops"</code>, not <code>false</code>. Prefer the if-expression above.</div></div><h3 class="bh">A realistic example</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-api">Players</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetService</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Players"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">getRank</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> coins <span class="c-op">=</span> player<span class="c-op">.</span>leaderstats<span class="c-op">.</span>Coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Value

	<span class="c-kw">if</span> coins <span class="c-op">&gt;=</span> <span class="c-num">10000</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		<span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-str">"Legend"</span>
	<span class="c-kw">elseif</span> coins <span class="c-op">&gt;=</span> <span class="c-num">5000</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		<span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-str">"Master"</span>
	<span class="c-kw">elseif</span> coins <span class="c-op">&gt;=</span> <span class="c-num">1000</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		<span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-str">"Pro"</span>
	<span class="c-kw">elseif</span> coins <span class="c-op">&gt;=</span> <span class="c-num">100</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		<span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-str">"Novice"</span>
	<span class="c-kw">else</span>
		<span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-str">"Newbie"</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>When you have more than about five branches all comparing the <i>same</i> variable to fixed values, a table lookup (Episode 18) is usually cleaner than a long elseif chain.</div></div><div class="callout try"><span class="ci">&#9654;</span><div><b>Try it yourself &mdash; </b>Write a function that takes a Humanoid's health and returns "healthy" (above 75), "hurt" (25-75), "critical" (1-24), or "dead" (0).</div></div></div></div></article>
<article class="card" id="ep10" data-n="10" data-search="10 events making code react to things happening instead of running once and stopping. events connect touched clickdetector debounce what an event is so far your scripts run top to bottom once and finish. an event is a signal that roblox fires when something happens — a part is touched, a player joins, a button is clicked. you connect a function to it, and roblox calls that function every time the event fires. local part = script.parent part.touched:connect(function(otherpart) print(otherpart.name .. &quot; touched me!&quot;) end) read that as: on the part&#x27;s touched signal, connect this function. the script reaches the bottom and then just… waits. forever. that&#x27;s the point. the two ways to write a connection -- inline anonymous function (most common) part.touched:connect(function(hit) print(hit.name) end) -- named function, passed by name local function ontouch(hit) print(hit.name) end part.touched:connect(ontouch) -- no parentheses! connect(ontouch()) with parentheses calls the function immediately and connects whatever it returned (usually nil), which errors. pass the function itself: connect(ontouch) . touched: getting the actual player touched hands you the part that hit you — a leg, an arm, a hat. you almost always want the humanoid or the player behind it. local players = game:getservice(&quot;players&quot;) local part = script.parent part.touched:connect(function(hit) -- hit.parent is the character model when a player touches it local humanoid = hit.parent:findfirstchildwhichisa(&quot;humanoid&quot;) if not humanoid then return end -- not a player, ignore local player = players:getplayerfromcharacter(hit.parent) if not player then return end print(player.name .. &quot; stepped on the part&quot;) humanoid.health -= 10 end) debounce: the fix for &quot;it fired 40 times&quot; touched fires for every body part , on every frame of contact. standing on a damage pad fires it dozens of times a second. a debounce flag stops that. local part = script.parent local debounce = false part.touched:connect(function(hit) if debounce then return end -- already running, bail out local humanoid = hit.parent:findfirstchildwhichisa(&quot;humanoid&quot;) if not humanoid then return end debounce = true -- lock humanoid.health -= 25 task.wait(2) -- cooldown debounce = false -- unlock end) for a per-player cooldown instead of a global one, store the flag in a table keyed by player: cooldowns[player] = true . see episode 18. clickdetector insert a clickdetector into a part (right-click → insert object). now the part is clickable and gives you the player directly. local part = script.parent local clickdetector = part.clickdetector clickdetector.mouseclick:connect(function(player) print(player.name .. &quot; clicked the part&quot;) part.brickcolor = brickcolor.random() end) modern alternative: a proximityprompt gives a nicer &quot;press e to interact&quot; prompt and works on console and mobile automatically. prompt.triggered:connect(function(player) ... end) connection objects local connection = part.touched:connect(function(hit) print(&quot;touched&quot;) end) -- later, stop listening connection:disconnect() -- fire only the first time, then auto-disconnect part.touched:once(function(hit) print(&quot;this only ever prints once&quot;) end) -- pause the script until the event fires next (no function needed) local hit = part.touched:wait() print(&quot;finally touched by &quot; .. hit.name) connections you never disconnect keep their function — and everything it references — alive in memory forever. if you connect inside a loop or per-player, store the connection and :disconnect() it when you&#x27;re done. this is the #1 cause of memory leaks in roblox games. events you&#x27;ll use constantly event fires when gives you part.touched something touches the part the other part part.touchended contact stops the other part clickdetector.mouseclick player clicks the part the player prompt.triggered player uses a proximityprompt the player players.playeradded someone joins the player players.playerremoving someone leaves the player player.characteradded their avatar spawns the character model humanoid.died a character dies nothing button.mousebutton1click a gui textbutton is clicked nothing make a part that turns red and damages any player who touches it, with a 3-second debounce, then turns back to its original color."><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge">10</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">Events</span><span class="ctag">Making code react to things happening instead of running once and stopping.</span></span><span class="cmeta">10:42</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button><button class="yt" type="button" data-vid="Cyjus9EQf48" data-n="10" data-title="Events"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M8 5v14l11-7z"/></svg>Watch</button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">events</span><span class="chip">connect</span><span class="chip">touched</span><span class="chip">clickdetector</span><span class="chip">debounce</span></div><h3 class="bh">What an event is</h3><p>So far your scripts run top to bottom once and finish. An <b>event</b> is a signal that Roblox fires when something happens &mdash; a part is touched, a player joins, a button is clicked. You <b>connect</b> a function to it, and Roblox calls that function every time the event fires.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> part <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">script</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Parent

part<span class="c-op">.</span>Touched<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>otherPart<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>otherPart<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" touched me!"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><p>Read that as: on the part's Touched signal, <b>connect</b> this function. The script reaches the bottom and then just&hellip; waits. Forever. That's the point.</p><h3 class="bh">The two ways to write a connection</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- inline anonymous function (most common)</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Touched<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>hit<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>hit<span class="c-op">.</span>Name<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- named function, passed by name</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">onTouch</span><span class="c-op">(</span>hit<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>hit<span class="c-op">.</span>Name<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

part<span class="c-op">.</span>Touched<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span>onTouch<span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">-- NO parentheses!</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div><code>Connect(onTouch())</code> with parentheses <b>calls the function immediately</b> and connects whatever it returned (usually nil), which errors. Pass the function itself: <code>Connect(onTouch)</code>.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Touched: getting the actual player</h3><p> <code>Touched</code> hands you the <b>part</b> that hit you &mdash; a leg, an arm, a hat. You almost always want the Humanoid or the Player behind it.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-api">Players</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetService</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Players"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> part <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">script</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Parent

part<span class="c-op">.</span>Touched<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>hit<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-com">-- hit.Parent is the Character model when a player touches it</span>
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> humanoid <span class="c-op">=</span> hit<span class="c-op">.</span>Parent<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">FindFirstChildWhichIsA</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Humanoid"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-kw">not</span> humanoid <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>   <span class="c-com">-- not a player, ignore</span>

	<span class="c-kw">local</span> player <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Players</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetPlayerFromCharacter</span><span class="c-op">(</span>hit<span class="c-op">.</span>Parent<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-kw">not</span> player <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>

	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" stepped on the part"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	humanoid<span class="c-op">.</span>Health <span class="c-op">-=</span> <span class="c-num">10</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Debounce: the fix for "it fired 40 times"</h3><p>Touched fires for <b>every body part</b>, on <b>every frame</b> of contact. Standing on a damage pad fires it dozens of times a second. A debounce flag stops that.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> part <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">script</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Parent
<span class="c-kw">local</span> debounce <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">false</span>

part<span class="c-op">.</span>Touched<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>hit<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> debounce <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>          <span class="c-com">-- already running, bail out</span>
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> humanoid <span class="c-op">=</span> hit<span class="c-op">.</span>Parent<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">FindFirstChildWhichIsA</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Humanoid"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-kw">not</span> humanoid <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>

	debounce <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span>                      <span class="c-com">-- lock</span>
	humanoid<span class="c-op">.</span>Health <span class="c-op">-=</span> <span class="c-num">25</span>
	<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">wait</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">2</span><span class="c-op">)</span>                         <span class="c-com">-- cooldown</span>
	debounce <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">false</span>                     <span class="c-com">-- unlock</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>For a per-player cooldown instead of a global one, store the flag in a table keyed by player: <code>cooldowns[player] = true</code>. See Episode 18.</div></div><h3 class="bh">ClickDetector</h3><p>Insert a <b>ClickDetector</b> into a Part (right-click &rarr; Insert Object). Now the part is clickable and gives you the Player directly.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> part <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">script</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Parent
<span class="c-kw">local</span> clickDetector <span class="c-op">=</span> part<span class="c-op">.</span>ClickDetector

clickDetector<span class="c-op">.</span>MouseClick<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" clicked the part"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	part<span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">BrickColor</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">BrickColor</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>Modern alternative: a <b>ProximityPrompt</b> gives a nicer "press E to interact" prompt and works on console and mobile automatically.<br><code>prompt.Triggered:Connect(function(player) ... end)</code></div></div><h3 class="bh">Connection objects</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> connection <span class="c-op">=</span> part<span class="c-op">.</span>Touched<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>hit<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"touched"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- later, stop listening</span>
connection<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Disconnect</span><span class="c-op">()</span>

<span class="c-com">-- fire only the first time, then auto-disconnect</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Touched<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Once</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>hit<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"this only ever prints once"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- pause the script until the event fires next (no function needed)</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> hit <span class="c-op">=</span> part<span class="c-op">.</span>Touched<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Wait</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"finally touched by "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> hit<span class="c-op">.</span>Name<span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>Connections you never disconnect keep their function &mdash; and everything it references &mdash; alive in memory forever. If you connect inside a loop or per-player, store the connection and <code>:Disconnect()</code> it when you're done. This is the #1 cause of memory leaks in Roblox games.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Events you'll use constantly</h3><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>Fires when</th><th>Gives you</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>part.Touched</code></td><td>Something touches the part</td><td>the other part</td></tr><tr><td><code>part.TouchEnded</code></td><td>Contact stops</td><td>the other part</td></tr><tr><td><code>clickDetector.MouseClick</code></td><td>Player clicks the part</td><td>the Player</td></tr><tr><td><code>prompt.Triggered</code></td><td>Player uses a ProximityPrompt</td><td>the Player</td></tr><tr><td><code>Players.PlayerAdded</code></td><td>Someone joins</td><td>the Player</td></tr><tr><td><code>Players.PlayerRemoving</code></td><td>Someone leaves</td><td>the Player</td></tr><tr><td><code>player.CharacterAdded</code></td><td>Their avatar spawns</td><td>the Character model</td></tr><tr><td><code>humanoid.Died</code></td><td>A character dies</td><td>nothing</td></tr><tr><td><code>button.MouseButton1Click</code></td><td>A GUI TextButton is clicked</td><td>nothing</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout try"><span class="ci">&#9654;</span><div><b>Try it yourself &mdash; </b>Make a part that turns red and damages any player who touches it, with a 3-second debounce, then turns back to its original color.</div></div></div></div></article>
<article class="card" id="ep11" data-n="11" data-search="11 built-in functions the library roblox gives you for free: math, strings, waiting, type checks, and object lookup. math string task wait builtin waiting task.wait(2) -- pause this script for ~2 seconds task.wait() -- pause until the next frame (~1/60 sec) task.spawn(fn) -- run fn now on its own thread, don&#x27;t block task.delay(3, fn) -- run fn in 3 seconds, don&#x27;t block task.defer(fn) -- run fn at the end of this frame the old globals wait() , spawn() , delay() , and spawn() are deprecated . they&#x27;re less accurate and can be throttled. thedevking&#x27;s 2018 videos use wait() because task didn&#x27;t exist yet — use task.wait() in anything you write today. they behave the same way otherwise. -- wait always returns the actual elapsed time local elapsed = task.wait(1) print(&quot;actually waited &quot; .. elapsed .. &quot; seconds&quot;) math function does example math.floor(x) round down math.floor(4.9) → 4 math.ceil(x) round up math.ceil(4.1) → 5 math.round(x) round to nearest math.round(4.5) → 5 math.abs(x) absolute value math.abs(-7) → 7 math.max(a,b,...) biggest math.max(3,9,1) → 9 math.min(a,b,...) smallest math.min(3,9,1) → 1 math.clamp(x,lo,hi) force into a range math.clamp(150,0,100) → 100 math.random(a,b) random integer episode 16 math.sqrt(x) square root math.sqrt(16) → 4 math.rad(deg) degrees → radians needed for cframe.angles math.deg(rad) radians → degrees math.pi 3.14159… a value, not a function math.huge infinity useful as a starting &quot;lowest so far&quot; string local s = &quot;hello roblox&quot; print(#s) --&gt; 12 (length) print(string.upper(s)) --&gt; hello roblox print(string.lower(s)) --&gt; hello roblox print(string.len(s)) --&gt; 12 print(string.sub(s, 1, 5)) --&gt; hello (characters 1 through 5) print(string.sub(s, 7)) --&gt; roblox (from 7 to the end) print(string.rep(&quot;ab&quot;, 3)) --&gt; ababab print(string.reverse(s)) --&gt; xolbor olleh print(string.find(s, &quot;roblox&quot;)) --&gt; 7 12 print(string.gsub(s, &quot;roblox&quot;, &quot;world&quot;)) --&gt; hello world 1 print(string.format(&quot;%.2f&quot;, 3.14159)) --&gt; 3.14 print(string.split(&quot;a,b,c&quot;, &quot;,&quot;)) --&gt; a table: {&quot;a&quot;,&quot;b&quot;,&quot;c&quot;} you can also call these with a colon on the string itself: s:upper() , s:sub(1,5) , s:split(&quot;,&quot;) . identical, just shorter. type and conversion tostring(42) --&gt; &quot;42&quot; tonumber(&quot;42&quot;) --&gt; 42 tonumber(&quot;abc&quot;) --&gt; nil type(x) --&gt; &quot;number&quot; | &quot;string&quot; | &quot;table&quot; | &quot;boolean&quot; | &quot;nil&quot; | &quot;function&quot; | &quot;userdata&quot; typeof(x) --&gt; roblox version: &quot;instance&quot;, &quot;vector3&quot;, &quot;color3&quot;, &quot;cframe&quot;... os.time() --&gt; seconds since 1970 (integer) -- tick() --&gt; deprecated. older tutorials use it; use os.time() -- for timestamps and os.clock() for measuring durations. os.clock() --&gt; high-precision cpu time, for benchmarking os.date(&quot;%x&quot;) --&gt; formatted date/time string getting services local players = game:getservice(&quot;players&quot;) local replicatedstorage = game:getservice(&quot;replicatedstorage&quot;) local tweenservice = game:getservice(&quot;tweenservice&quot;) local runservice = game:getservice(&quot;runservice&quot;) local debris = game:getservice(&quot;debris&quot;) always use game:getservice(&quot;x&quot;) instead of game.x . it works even if the service hasn&#x27;t been created yet, and it can&#x27;t break if a player renames an object. workspace is the one accepted shortcut. finding objects local model = workspace.mymodel model:findfirstchild(&quot;head&quot;) -- returns nil if missing (safe) model:waitforchild(&quot;head&quot;) -- yields until it exists model:findfirstchild(&quot;head&quot;, true) -- true = search all descendants model:findfirstchildofclass(&quot;humanoid&quot;) -- by exact class name model:findfirstchildwhichisa(&quot;basepart&quot;) -- by class or subclass model:findfirstancestorofclass(&quot;model&quot;) -- search upward model:getchildren() -- table of direct children model:getdescendants() -- table of everything inside model:isa(&quot;model&quot;) -- class check -&gt; boolean model:destroy() -- delete it permanently model:clone() -- make a copy waitforchild yields forever if the object never appears; after 5 seconds studio warns &quot;infinite yield possible.&quot; pass a timeout to be safe: model:waitforchild(&quot;head&quot;, 10) , which returns nil if it times out. safe calls with pcall local success, result = pcall(function() return someriskything() end) if success then print(&quot;worked:&quot;, result) else warn(&quot;failed:&quot;, result) -- result is the error message end pcall = &quot;protected call.&quot; it runs code and catches errors instead of letting them kill your script. essential for datastores and http requests. write a function that takes a number of seconds and prints it as mm:ss , using math.floor , % , and string.format(&quot;%02d&quot;, n) ."><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge">11</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">Built-In Functions</span><span class="ctag">The library Roblox gives you for free: math, strings, waiting, type checks, and object lookup.</span></span><span class="cmeta">7:42</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button><button class="yt" type="button" data-vid="QiEsMfkTauw" data-n="11" data-title="Built-In Functions"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M8 5v14l11-7z"/></svg>Watch</button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">math</span><span class="chip">string</span><span class="chip">task</span><span class="chip">wait</span><span class="chip">builtin</span></div><h3 class="bh">Waiting</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">wait</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">2</span><span class="c-op">)</span>        <span class="c-com">-- pause this script for ~2 seconds</span>
<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">wait</span><span class="c-op">()</span>         <span class="c-com">-- pause until the next frame (~1/60 sec)</span>

<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">spawn</span><span class="c-op">(</span>fn<span class="c-op">)</span>      <span class="c-com">-- run fn NOW on its own thread, don't block</span>
<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">delay</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">3</span><span class="c-op">,</span> fn<span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">-- run fn in 3 seconds, don't block</span>
<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">defer</span><span class="c-op">(</span>fn<span class="c-op">)</span>      <span class="c-com">-- run fn at the end of this frame</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>The old globals <code>wait()</code>, <code>spawn()</code>, <code>delay()</code>, and <code>Spawn()</code> are <b>deprecated</b>. They're less accurate and can be throttled. TheDevKing's 2018 videos use <code>wait()</code> because <code>task</code> didn't exist yet &mdash; use <code>task.wait()</code> in anything you write today. They behave the same way otherwise.</div></div><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- wait always returns the actual elapsed time</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> elapsed <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">wait</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"actually waited "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> elapsed <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" seconds"</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">math</h3><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Does</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>math.floor(x)</code></td><td>round down</td><td><code>math.floor(4.9)</code> &rarr; 4</td></tr><tr><td><code>math.ceil(x)</code></td><td>round up</td><td><code>math.ceil(4.1)</code> &rarr; 5</td></tr><tr><td><code>math.round(x)</code></td><td>round to nearest</td><td><code>math.round(4.5)</code> &rarr; 5</td></tr><tr><td><code>math.abs(x)</code></td><td>absolute value</td><td><code>math.abs(-7)</code> &rarr; 7</td></tr><tr><td><code>math.max(a,b,...)</code></td><td>biggest</td><td><code>math.max(3,9,1)</code> &rarr; 9</td></tr><tr><td><code>math.min(a,b,...)</code></td><td>smallest</td><td><code>math.min(3,9,1)</code> &rarr; 1</td></tr><tr><td><code>math.clamp(x,lo,hi)</code></td><td>force into a range</td><td><code>math.clamp(150,0,100)</code> &rarr; 100</td></tr><tr><td><code>math.random(a,b)</code></td><td>random integer</td><td>Episode 16</td></tr><tr><td><code>math.sqrt(x)</code></td><td>square root</td><td><code>math.sqrt(16)</code> &rarr; 4</td></tr><tr><td><code>math.rad(deg)</code></td><td>degrees &rarr; radians</td><td>needed for CFrame.Angles</td></tr><tr><td><code>math.deg(rad)</code></td><td>radians &rarr; degrees</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>math.pi</code></td><td>3.14159&hellip;</td><td>a value, not a function</td></tr><tr><td><code>math.huge</code></td><td>infinity</td><td>useful as a starting "lowest so far"</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class="bh">string</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> s <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Hello Roblox"</span>

<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(#</span>s<span class="c-op">)</span>                      <span class="c-com">--&gt; 12   (length)</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">string</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">upper</span><span class="c-op">(</span>s<span class="c-op">))</span>         <span class="c-com">--&gt; HELLO ROBLOX</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">string</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">lower</span><span class="c-op">(</span>s<span class="c-op">))</span>         <span class="c-com">--&gt; hello roblox</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">string</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">len</span><span class="c-op">(</span>s<span class="c-op">))</span>           <span class="c-com">--&gt; 12</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">string</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">sub</span><span class="c-op">(</span>s<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">5</span><span class="c-op">))</span>     <span class="c-com">--&gt; Hello  (characters 1 through 5)</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">string</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">sub</span><span class="c-op">(</span>s<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">7</span><span class="c-op">))</span>        <span class="c-com">--&gt; Roblox (from 7 to the end)</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">string</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">rep</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"ab"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">3</span><span class="c-op">))</span>     <span class="c-com">--&gt; ababab</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">string</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">reverse</span><span class="c-op">(</span>s<span class="c-op">))</span>       <span class="c-com">--&gt; xolboR olleH</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">string</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">find</span><span class="c-op">(</span>s<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"Roblox"</span><span class="c-op">))</span> <span class="c-com">--&gt; 7  12</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">string</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">gsub</span><span class="c-op">(</span>s<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"Roblox"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"World"</span><span class="c-op">))</span> <span class="c-com">--&gt; Hello World  1</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">string</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">format</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"%.2f"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">3.14159</span><span class="c-op">))</span>    <span class="c-com">--&gt; 3.14</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">string</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">split</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"a,b,c"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">","</span><span class="c-op">))</span>        <span class="c-com">--&gt; a table: {"a","b","c"}</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>You can also call these with a colon on the string itself: <code>s:upper()</code>, <code>s:sub(1,5)</code>, <code>s:split(",")</code>. Identical, just shorter.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Type and conversion</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-api">tostring</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">42</span><span class="c-op">)</span>          <span class="c-com">--&gt; "42"</span>
<span class="c-api">tonumber</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"42"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>        <span class="c-com">--&gt; 42</span>
<span class="c-api">tonumber</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"abc"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>       <span class="c-com">--&gt; nil</span>
<span class="c-kw">type</span><span class="c-op">(</span>x<span class="c-op">)</span>               <span class="c-com">--&gt; "number" | "string" | "table" | "boolean" | "nil" | "function" | "userdata"</span>
<span class="c-api">typeof</span><span class="c-op">(</span>x<span class="c-op">)</span>             <span class="c-com">--&gt; Roblox version: "Instance", "Vector3", "Color3", "CFrame"...</span>
<span class="c-api">os</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">time</span><span class="c-op">()</span>             <span class="c-com">--&gt; seconds since 1970 (integer)</span>
<span class="c-com">-- tick()             --&gt; DEPRECATED. Older tutorials use it; use os.time()</span>
<span class="c-com">--                       for timestamps and os.clock() for measuring durations.</span>
<span class="c-api">os</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">clock</span><span class="c-op">()</span>            <span class="c-com">--&gt; high-precision CPU time, for benchmarking</span>
<span class="c-api">os</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">date</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"%X"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>         <span class="c-com">--&gt; formatted date/time string</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Getting services</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-api">Players</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetService</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Players"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-api">ReplicatedStorage</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetService</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"ReplicatedStorage"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-api">TweenService</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetService</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"TweenService"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-api">RunService</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetService</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"RunService"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-api">Debris</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetService</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Debris"</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>Always use <code>game:GetService("X")</code> instead of <code>game.X</code>. It works even if the service hasn't been created yet, and it can't break if a player renames an object. <code>workspace</code> is the one accepted shortcut.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Finding objects</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> model <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">workspace</span><span class="c-op">.</span>MyModel

model<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">FindFirstChild</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Head"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>                <span class="c-com">-- returns nil if missing (safe)</span>
model<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">WaitForChild</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Head"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>                  <span class="c-com">-- yields until it exists</span>
model<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">FindFirstChild</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Head"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span><span class="c-op">)</span>          <span class="c-com">-- true = search all descendants</span>
model<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">FindFirstChildOfClass</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Humanoid"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>     <span class="c-com">-- by exact class name</span>
model<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">FindFirstChildWhichIsA</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"BasePart"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>    <span class="c-com">-- by class OR subclass</span>
model<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">FindFirstAncestorOfClass</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Model"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>     <span class="c-com">-- search upward</span>
model<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetChildren</span><span class="c-op">()</span>                         <span class="c-com">-- table of direct children</span>
model<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetDescendants</span><span class="c-op">()</span>                      <span class="c-com">-- table of everything inside</span>
model<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">IsA</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Model"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>                          <span class="c-com">-- class check -&gt; boolean</span>
model<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Destroy</span><span class="c-op">()</span>                             <span class="c-com">-- delete it permanently</span>
model<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Clone</span><span class="c-op">()</span>                               <span class="c-com">-- make a copy</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div><code>WaitForChild</code> yields <b>forever</b> if the object never appears; after 5 seconds Studio warns <i>"Infinite yield possible."</i> Pass a timeout to be safe: <code>model:WaitForChild("Head", 10)</code>, which returns nil if it times out.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Safe calls with pcall</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> success<span class="c-op">,</span> result <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">pcall</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
	<span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-fn">someRiskyThing</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-kw">if</span> success <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"worked:"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> result<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">else</span>
	<span class="c-api">warn</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"failed:"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> result<span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">-- result is the error message</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><p><code>pcall</code> = "protected call." It runs code and catches errors instead of letting them kill your script. Essential for DataStores and HTTP requests.</p><div class="callout try"><span class="ci">&#9654;</span><div><b>Try it yourself &mdash; </b>Write a function that takes a number of seconds and prints it as <code>MM:SS</code>, using <code>math.floor</code>, <code>%</code>, and <code>string.format("%02d", n)</code>.</div></div></div></div></article>
<article class="card" id="ep12" data-n="12" data-search="12 while and repeat loops running the same code over and over until you say stop. loops while repeat for while loop local count = 1 while count 1 2 3 4 5 checks the condition first , then runs the body. if the condition is false to begin with, the body never runs at all. while true do — the infinite loop while true do print(&quot;tick&quot;) task.wait(1) end you must have a task.wait() inside any while true do loop. without one it runs millions of times per second, freezes studio, and crashes the server. if studio hangs the moment you press play, this is why. -- studio will actually catch the worst case for you: while true do print(&quot;no wait&quot;) end -- output: &quot;script timeout: exhausted allowed execution time&quot; repeat … until local count = 1 repeat print(count) count += 1 until count &gt; 5 --&gt; 1 2 3 4 5 the mirror image of while : it runs the body first , then checks. so the body always runs at least once . also note the condition is when to stop , not when to continue. -- classic use: wait for something to exist repeat task.wait() until game:isloaded() -- variables declared inside are visible in the until condition repeat local answer = getanswer() until answer ~= nil -- legal in lua, unlike while numeric for loop when you know exactly how many times to loop, this is cleaner and faster than a while. for i = 1, 5 do print(i) end --&gt; 1 2 3 4 5 for i = 1, 10, 2 do -- start, stop, step print(i) end --&gt; 1 3 5 7 9 for i = 10, 1, -1 do -- counting down print(i) end --&gt; 10 9 8 ... 1 for i = 0, 1, 0.1 do -- steps can be decimals part.transparency = i task.wait(0.05) end the loop variable i is local to the loop and disappears when it ends. changing i inside the body doesn&#x27;t affect the loop&#x27;s counting. which loop to use situation use you know the exact count (10 times, 1 to #table) for i = 1, n do loop until a condition changes while cond do must run at least once, then check repeat … until run forever (a game loop, a spinning part) while true do + task.wait() loop over a table or getchildren() for i, v in — episode 19 real examples -- spinning part local part = script.parent while true do part.cframe = part.cframe * cframe.angles(0, math.rad(2), 0) task.wait() end -- countdown timer for i = 10, 1, -1 do print(i .. &quot;...&quot;) task.wait(1) end print(&quot;go!&quot;) -- fade a part out then delete it local part = script.parent for i = 0, 1, 0.05 do part.transparency = i task.wait(0.03) end part:destroy() -- build a staircase for i = 1, 20 do local step = instance.new(&quot;part&quot;) step.size = vector3.new(4, 1, 4) step.position = vector3.new(0, i * 2, i * 4) step.anchored = true step.parent = workspace end loops that run every frame are usually better written as runservice.heartbeat:connect(...) , which is properly synced to the frame rate and gives you the delta time. write a loop that makes a part rise 50 studs over 5 seconds, then a repeat loop that waits until a player&#x27;s humanoid exists before doing anything."><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge">12</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">While and Repeat Loops</span><span class="ctag">Running the same code over and over until you say stop.</span></span><span class="cmeta">9:55</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button><button class="yt" type="button" data-vid="Hpki7i3xDOM" data-n="12" data-title="While and Repeat Loops"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M8 5v14l11-7z"/></svg>Watch</button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">loops</span><span class="chip">while</span><span class="chip">repeat</span><span class="chip">for</span></div><h3 class="bh">while loop</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> count <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">1</span>

<span class="c-kw">while</span> count <span class="c-op">&lt;=</span> <span class="c-num">5</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>count<span class="c-op">)</span>
	count <span class="c-op">+=</span> <span class="c-num">1</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-com">--&gt; 1 2 3 4 5</span></code></pre></div><p>Checks the condition <b>first</b>, then runs the body. If the condition is false to begin with, the body never runs at all.</p><h3 class="bh">while true do &mdash; the infinite loop</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">while</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"tick"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">wait</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div><b>You must have a <code>task.wait()</code> inside any <code>while true do</code> loop.</b> Without one it runs millions of times per second, freezes Studio, and crashes the server. If Studio hangs the moment you press Play, this is why.</div></div><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- Studio will actually catch the worst case for you:</span>
<span class="c-kw">while</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"no wait"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-com">-- Output: "Script timeout: exhausted allowed execution time"</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">repeat &hellip; until</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> count <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">1</span>

<span class="c-kw">repeat</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>count<span class="c-op">)</span>
	count <span class="c-op">+=</span> <span class="c-num">1</span>
<span class="c-kw">until</span> count <span class="c-op">&gt;</span> <span class="c-num">5</span>
<span class="c-com">--&gt; 1 2 3 4 5</span></code></pre></div><p>The mirror image of <code>while</code>: it runs the body <b>first</b>, then checks. So the body <b>always runs at least once</b>. Also note the condition is when to <i>stop</i>, not when to continue.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- classic use: wait for something to exist</span>
<span class="c-kw">repeat</span> <span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">wait</span><span class="c-op">()</span> <span class="c-kw">until</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">IsLoaded</span><span class="c-op">()</span>

<span class="c-com">-- variables declared inside are visible in the until condition</span>
<span class="c-kw">repeat</span>
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> answer <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-fn">getAnswer</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
<span class="c-kw">until</span> answer <span class="c-op">~=</span> <span class="c-kw">nil</span>   <span class="c-com">-- legal in Lua, unlike while</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Numeric for loop</h3><p>When you know exactly how many times to loop, this is cleaner and faster than a while.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">for</span> i <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">5</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>i<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-com">--&gt; 1 2 3 4 5</span>

<span class="c-kw">for</span> i <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">10</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">2</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>        <span class="c-com">-- start, stop, step</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>i<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-com">--&gt; 1 3 5 7 9</span>

<span class="c-kw">for</span> i <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">10</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-op">-</span><span class="c-num">1</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>       <span class="c-com">-- counting down</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>i<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-com">--&gt; 10 9 8 ... 1</span>

<span class="c-kw">for</span> i <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">0.1</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>       <span class="c-com">-- steps can be decimals</span>
	part<span class="c-op">.</span>Transparency <span class="c-op">=</span> i
	<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">wait</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">0.05</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><p>The loop variable <code>i</code> is <b>local to the loop</b> and disappears when it ends. Changing <code>i</code> inside the body doesn't affect the loop's counting.</p><h3 class="bh">Which loop to use</h3><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>You know the exact count (10 times, 1 to #table)</td><td><code>for i = 1, n do</code></td></tr><tr><td>Loop until a condition changes</td><td><code>while cond do</code></td></tr><tr><td>Must run at least once, then check</td><td><code>repeat &hellip; until</code></td></tr><tr><td>Run forever (a game loop, a spinning part)</td><td><code>while true do</code> + <code>task.wait()</code></td></tr><tr><td>Loop over a table or GetChildren()</td><td><code>for i, v in</code> &mdash; Episode 19</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class="bh">Real examples</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- spinning part</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> part <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">script</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Parent
<span class="c-kw">while</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	part<span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">CFrame</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> part<span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">CFrame</span> <span class="c-op">*</span> <span class="c-api">CFrame</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">Angles</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">rad</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">2</span><span class="c-op">),</span> <span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">wait</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- countdown timer</span>
<span class="c-kw">for</span> i <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">10</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-op">-</span><span class="c-num">1</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>i <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">"..."</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">wait</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"GO!"</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- fade a part out then delete it</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> part <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">script</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Parent
<span class="c-kw">for</span> i <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">0.05</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	part<span class="c-op">.</span>Transparency <span class="c-op">=</span> i
	<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">wait</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">0.03</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>
part<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Destroy</span><span class="c-op">()</span></code></pre></div><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- build a staircase</span>
<span class="c-kw">for</span> i <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">20</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> step <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Part"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	step<span class="c-op">.</span>Size <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Vector3</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">4</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">4</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	step<span class="c-op">.</span>Position <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Vector3</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">,</span> i <span class="c-op">*</span> <span class="c-num">2</span><span class="c-op">,</span> i <span class="c-op">*</span> <span class="c-num">4</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	step<span class="c-op">.</span>Anchored <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span>
	step<span class="c-op">.</span>Parent <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">workspace</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>Loops that run every frame are usually better written as <code>RunService.Heartbeat:Connect(...)</code>, which is properly synced to the frame rate and gives you the delta time.</div></div><div class="callout try"><span class="ci">&#9654;</span><div><b>Try it yourself &mdash; </b>Write a loop that makes a part rise 50 studs over 5 seconds, then a <code>repeat</code> loop that waits until a player's Humanoid exists before doing anything.</div></div></div></div></article>
<article class="card" id="ep13" data-n="13" data-search="13 more events player joins, character spawns, deaths, property changes, and talking between server and client. events playeradded characteradded died changed remoteevent playeradded and playerremoving local players = game:getservice(&quot;players&quot;) players.playeradded:connect(function(player) print(player.name .. &quot; joined the game&quot;) print(&quot;userid: &quot; .. player.userid) end) players.playerremoving:connect(function(player) print(player.name .. &quot; left&quot;) -- save their data here end) in studio, your own player often joins before the script connects, so playeradded never fires for you. the bulletproof pattern loops over anyone already present: local function onplayeradded(player) print(player.name .. &quot; is here&quot;) end for _, player in players:getplayers() do onplayeradded(player) -- catch anyone already connected end players.playeradded:connect(onplayeradded) characteradded a player is not their avatar. the player object persists for the whole session; the character model is destroyed and rebuilt on every respawn. anything you attach to the character must be reattached each time. players.playeradded:connect(function(player) player.characteradded:connect(function(character) local humanoid = character:waitforchild(&quot;humanoid&quot;) humanoid.walkspeed = 32 humanoid.usejumppower = true -- jumppower is ignored without this humanoid.jumppower = 75 humanoid.died:connect(function() print(player.name .. &quot; died&quot;) end) end) end) player.character can be nil if you check it before they&#x27;ve spawned. use player.characteradded:wait() or the connection above rather than assuming it exists. humanoid events humanoid.died:connect(function() print(&quot;died&quot;) end) humanoid.healthchanged:connect(function(newhealth) print(newhealth) end) humanoid.jumping:connect(function(active) print(&quot;jump:&quot;, active) end) humanoid.running:connect(function(speed) print(&quot;speed:&quot;, speed) end) humanoid.statechanged:connect(function(old, new) print(old, &quot;-&gt;&quot;, new) end) humanoid.seated:connect(function(isseated, seat) print(isseated) end) watching a property change -- generic: fires for any property change (and for value objects, their .value) part.changed:connect(function(propertyname) print(propertyname .. &quot; changed&quot;) end) -- specific: much better, fires only for the one you care about part:getpropertychangedsignal(&quot;transparency&quot;):connect(function() print(&quot;now &quot; .. part.transparency) end) -- intvalue / stringvalue / boolvalue only coinsvalue.changed:connect(function(newvalue) print(&quot;coins are now &quot; .. newvalue) end) for an intvalue and friends, .changed passes you the new value . for everything else it passes the property name . that inconsistency catches people out. hierarchy events folder.childadded:connect(function(child) print(child.name .. &quot; added&quot;) end) folder.childremoved:connect(function(child) print(child.name .. &quot; removed&quot;) end) folder.descendantadded:connect(function(d) print(d.name) end) part.ancestrychanged:connect(function(child, parent) if not parent then print(&quot;it was destroyed or removed&quot;) end end) gui events (localscript) local button = script.parent button.mousebutton1click:connect(function() print(&quot;clicked&quot;) end) button.mouseenter:connect(function() button.backgroundtransparency = 0.2 end) button.mouseleave:connect(function() button.backgroundtransparency = 0 end) textbox.focuslost:connect(function(enterpressed) if enterpressed then print(textbox.text) end end) remoteevent: client talking to server the server and each client are separate machines that cannot read each other&#x27;s variables. a remoteevent (put one in replicatedstorage) is the bridge. this is beyond the strict scope of the beginner series, but it&#x27;s the very next thing you&#x27;ll need. -- localscript (client) -- asks the server to do something local replicatedstorage = game:getservice(&quot;replicatedstorage&quot;) local buyevent = replicatedstorage:waitforchild(&quot;buyitem&quot;) buyevent:fireserver(&quot;sword&quot;, 100) -- script (server) -- receives it. &#x27;player&#x27; is added automatically. local replicatedstorage = game:getservice(&quot;replicatedstorage&quot;) local buyevent = replicatedstorage:waitforchild(&quot;buyitem&quot;) buyevent.onserverevent:connect(function(player, itemname, price) -- never trust the client. re-check everything here. local coins = player.leaderstats.coins if coins.value &gt;= price then coins.value -= price print(player.name .. &quot; bought &quot; .. itemname) end end) the server never trusts data from a client. a hacker can call fireserver(&quot;sword&quot;, 0) with any values they like. always re-validate price, ownership, distance, and cooldowns on the server side. -- server -&gt; client remote:fireclient(player, data) -- one player remote:fireallclients(data) -- everyone -- localscript listens remote.onclientevent:connect(function(data) print(data) end) bindableevent: script talking to script on the same side -- script a bindable:fire(&quot;hello&quot;) -- script b (same side: both server, or both client) bindable.event:connect(function(msg) print(msg) end) give every joining player a leaderstats folder with a deaths counter that goes up by 1 every time their humanoid dies — including after respawns."><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge">13</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">More Events</span><span class="ctag">Player joins, character spawns, deaths, property changes, and talking between server and client.</span></span><span class="cmeta">12:52</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button><button class="yt" type="button" data-vid="V9NxvxndAZw" data-n="13" data-title="More Events"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M8 5v14l11-7z"/></svg>Watch</button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">events</span><span class="chip">playeradded</span><span class="chip">characteradded</span><span class="chip">died</span><span class="chip">changed</span><span class="chip">remoteevent</span></div><h3 class="bh">PlayerAdded and PlayerRemoving</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-api">Players</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetService</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Players"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-api">Players</span><span class="c-op">.</span>PlayerAdded<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" joined the game"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"UserId: "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> player<span class="c-op">.</span>UserId<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-api">Players</span><span class="c-op">.</span>PlayerRemoving<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" left"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-com">-- save their data here</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>In Studio, your own player often joins <b>before</b> the script connects, so PlayerAdded never fires for you. The bulletproof pattern loops over anyone already present:</div></div><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">onPlayerAdded</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" is here"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-kw">for</span> _<span class="c-op">,</span> player <span class="c-kw">in</span> <span class="c-api">Players</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetPlayers</span><span class="c-op">()</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-fn">onPlayerAdded</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">)</span>          <span class="c-com">-- catch anyone already connected</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-api">Players</span><span class="c-op">.</span>PlayerAdded<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span>onPlayerAdded<span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">CharacterAdded</h3><p>A Player is not their avatar. The <b>Player</b> object persists for the whole session; the <b>Character</b> model is destroyed and rebuilt on every respawn. Anything you attach to the character must be reattached each time.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-api">Players</span><span class="c-op">.</span>PlayerAdded<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">)</span>
	player<span class="c-op">.</span>CharacterAdded<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>character<span class="c-op">)</span>
		<span class="c-kw">local</span> humanoid <span class="c-op">=</span> character<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">WaitForChild</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Humanoid"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
		humanoid<span class="c-op">.</span>WalkSpeed <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">32</span>
		humanoid<span class="c-op">.</span>UseJumpPower <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span>   <span class="c-com">-- JumpPower is ignored without this</span>
		humanoid<span class="c-op">.</span>JumpPower <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">75</span>

		humanoid<span class="c-op">.</span>Died<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
			<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" died"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
		<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div><code>player.Character</code> can be <code>nil</code> if you check it before they've spawned. Use <code>player.CharacterAdded:Wait()</code> or the connection above rather than assuming it exists.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Humanoid events</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code>humanoid<span class="c-op">.</span>Died<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">()</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"died"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
humanoid<span class="c-op">.</span>HealthChanged<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>newHealth<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>newHealth<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
humanoid<span class="c-op">.</span>Jumping<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>active<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"jump:"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> active<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
humanoid<span class="c-op">.</span>Running<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>speed<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"speed:"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> speed<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
humanoid<span class="c-op">.</span>StateChanged<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>old<span class="c-op">,</span> new<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>old<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"-&gt;"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> new<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
humanoid<span class="c-op">.</span>Seated<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>isSeated<span class="c-op">,</span> seat<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>isSeated<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Watching a property change</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- generic: fires for ANY property change (and for Value objects, their .Value)</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Changed<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>propertyName<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>propertyName <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" changed"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- specific: much better, fires only for the one you care about</span>
part<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetPropertyChangedSignal</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Transparency"</span><span class="c-op">):</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"now "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> part<span class="c-op">.</span>Transparency<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- IntValue / StringValue / BoolValue only</span>
coinsValue<span class="c-op">.</span>Changed<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>newValue<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"coins are now "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> newValue<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><p>For an <code>IntValue</code> and friends, <code>.Changed</code> passes you the <b>new value</b>. For everything else it passes the <b>property name</b>. That inconsistency catches people out.</p><h3 class="bh">Hierarchy events</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code>folder<span class="c-op">.</span>ChildAdded<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>child<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>child<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" added"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
folder<span class="c-op">.</span>ChildRemoved<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>child<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>child<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" removed"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
folder<span class="c-op">.</span>DescendantAdded<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>d<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>d<span class="c-op">.</span>Name<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>AncestryChanged<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>child<span class="c-op">,</span> parent<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-kw">not</span> parent <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"it was destroyed or removed"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">GUI events (LocalScript)</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> button <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">script</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Parent

button<span class="c-op">.</span>MouseButton1Click<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"clicked"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

button<span class="c-op">.</span>MouseEnter<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">()</span> button<span class="c-op">.</span>BackgroundTransparency <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">0.2</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
button<span class="c-op">.</span>MouseLeave<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">()</span> button<span class="c-op">.</span>BackgroundTransparency <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">0</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

textBox<span class="c-op">.</span>FocusLost<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>enterPressed<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> enterPressed <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>textBox<span class="c-op">.</span>Text<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">RemoteEvent: client talking to server</h3><p>The server and each client are separate machines that cannot read each other's variables. A <b>RemoteEvent</b> (put one in ReplicatedStorage) is the bridge. This is beyond the strict scope of the beginner series, but it's the very next thing you'll need.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- LocalScript (client) -- asks the server to do something</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-api">ReplicatedStorage</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetService</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"ReplicatedStorage"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> buyEvent <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">ReplicatedStorage</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">WaitForChild</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"BuyItem"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

buyEvent<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">FireServer</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Sword"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">100</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- Script (server) -- receives it. 'player' is added automatically.</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-api">ReplicatedStorage</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetService</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"ReplicatedStorage"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> buyEvent <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">ReplicatedStorage</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">WaitForChild</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"BuyItem"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

buyEvent<span class="c-op">.</span>OnServerEvent<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">,</span> itemName<span class="c-op">,</span> price<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-com">-- NEVER trust the client. Re-check everything here.</span>
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> coins <span class="c-op">=</span> player<span class="c-op">.</span>leaderstats<span class="c-op">.</span>Coins
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Value <span class="c-op">&gt;=</span> price <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Value <span class="c-op">-=</span> price
		<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" bought "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> itemName<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>The server <b>never</b> trusts data from a client. A hacker can call <code>FireServer("Sword", 0)</code> with any values they like. Always re-validate price, ownership, distance, and cooldowns on the server side.</div></div><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- server -&gt; client</span>
remote<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">FireClient</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">,</span> data<span class="c-op">)</span>      <span class="c-com">-- one player</span>
remote<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">FireAllClients</span><span class="c-op">(</span>data<span class="c-op">)</span>          <span class="c-com">-- everyone</span>

<span class="c-com">-- LocalScript listens</span>
remote<span class="c-op">.</span>OnClientEvent<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>data<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>data<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">BindableEvent: script talking to script on the same side</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- Script A</span>
bindable<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Fire</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"hello"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- Script B (same side: both server, or both client)</span>
bindable<span class="c-op">.</span>Event<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>msg<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>msg<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout try"><span class="ci">&#9654;</span><div><b>Try it yourself &mdash; </b>Give every joining player a leaderstats folder with a Deaths counter that goes up by 1 every time their Humanoid dies &mdash; including after respawns.</div></div></div></div></article>
<article class="card" id="ep14" data-n="14" data-search="14 instances creating, copying, moving, and deleting objects from code instead of by hand. instance new clone destroy hierarchy what an instance is every single thing in explorer is an instance: parts, models, folders, scripts, humanoids, guis, sounds, values. they all share the same core methods and the same parent-based hierarchy. instance.new local part = instance.new(&quot;part&quot;) part.name = &quot;mypart&quot; part.size = vector3.new(4, 4, 4) part.position = vector3.new(0, 10, 0) part.brickcolor = brickcolor.new(&quot;bright blue&quot;) part.material = enum.material.neon part.anchored = true part.parent = workspace -- set parent last a new instance has parent = nil , meaning it exists in memory but is nowhere in the game. nothing appears until you set parent. forgetting this line is the classic &quot;my script runs but nothing happens&quot; bug. set parent last , after all other properties. parenting first makes roblox replicate every subsequent property change to all clients one at a time. parenting last sends one clean update. things you&#x27;ll create often instance.new(&quot;part&quot;) -- a brick instance.new(&quot;folder&quot;) -- organization instance.new(&quot;intvalue&quot;) -- a whole number container (.value) instance.new(&quot;numbervalue&quot;) -- a decimal container instance.new(&quot;stringvalue&quot;) -- a text container instance.new(&quot;boolvalue&quot;) -- a true/false container instance.new(&quot;objectvalue&quot;) -- a reference to another instance instance.new(&quot;sound&quot;) -- audio instance.new(&quot;clickdetector&quot;) -- makes a part clickable instance.new(&quot;proximityprompt&quot;)-- &quot;press e&quot; interaction instance.new(&quot;attachment&quot;) -- anchor point for effects/constraints instance.new(&quot;highlight&quot;) -- outline glow on a model instance.new(&quot;billboardgui&quot;) -- ui that floats in 3d space -- instance.new accepts the parent as a second argument, -- but for the performance reason above, avoid it: local part = instance.new(&quot;part&quot;, workspace) -- works, but not recommended clone copying a pre-built template is far easier than constructing complex objects property by property. store the original in serverstorage or replicatedstorage so it isn&#x27;t in the world. local serverstorage = game:getservice(&quot;serverstorage&quot;) local template = serverstorage:waitforchild(&quot;coinmodel&quot;) for i = 1, 10 do local coin = template:clone() coin:pivotto(cframe.new(i * 10, 5, 0)) -- models use pivotto, not position coin.parent = workspace end :clone() copies the object and everything inside it — scripts, children, all of it. the copy also starts with parent = nil . a model has no position property — only parts do. setting model.position throws &quot;position is not a valid member of model&quot; . move a whole model with model:pivotto(cframe.new(x, y, z)) . an object with archivable = false returns nil from :clone() . player characters are the common case — you can&#x27;t simply clone a player&#x27;s avatar. destroy part:destroy() -- removes it, disconnects its events, frees memory part.parent = nil -- removes it from the world but keeps it alive -- debris: auto-destroy after n seconds local debris = game:getservice(&quot;debris&quot;) debris:additem(part, 5) -- gone in 5 seconds, no waiting needed :destroy() is permanent. there is no undo, and any variable still pointing at the object now refers to a locked, unusable instance. :remove() is deprecated — don&#x27;t use it. navigating the hierarchy game -- the root of everything workspace -- shortcut for game.workspace script -- the script this code is in script.parent -- whatever contains the script script.parent.parent -- keep going up workspace.mymodel.head -- dot notation, errors if missing workspace:findfirstchild(&quot;mymodel&quot;) -- returns nil if missing workspace:waitforchild(&quot;mymodel&quot;) -- yields until it exists method returns nil if missing? yields? use when .name (dot) no — it errors no you are certain it exists right now :findfirstchild(name) yes no it might not exist and that&#x27;s fine :waitforchild(name) only on timeout yes client code waiting on things still loading rule of thumb: in localscripts , use :waitforchild() for anything you didn&#x27;t create yourself — the client often runs before objects have replicated. in server scripts , dot notation is usually safe. listing children for _, child in workspace:getchildren() do if child:isa(&quot;basepart&quot;) then child.brickcolor = brickcolor.random() end end -- getdescendants goes all the way down, not just one level for _, obj in model:getdescendants() do if obj:isa(&quot;basepart&quot;) then obj.anchored = true end end :isa() understands inheritance. part , wedgepart , meshpart , and unionoperation are all basepart , so :isa(&quot;basepart&quot;) catches every kind of brick at once. attributes (the modern alternative to value objects) part:setattribute(&quot;damage&quot;, 25) print(part:getattribute(&quot;damage&quot;)) --&gt; 25 part:getattributechangedsignal(&quot;damage&quot;):connect(function() print(&quot;damage is now &quot; .. part:getattribute(&quot;damage&quot;)) end) for name, value in part:getattributes() do print(name, value) end attributes are lighter than intvalue/stringvalue objects, show up in the properties window, and replicate automatically. for storing simple data on an object, prefer them over value instances. a full example -- spawn a ring of glowing pillars that vanish after 10 seconds local debris = game:getservice(&quot;debris&quot;) local function spawnring(center, count, radius) local folder = instance.new(&quot;folder&quot;) folder.name = &quot;ring&quot; for i = 1, count do local angle = (i / count) * math.pi * 2 local pillar = instance.new(&quot;part&quot;) pillar.size = vector3.new(2, 12, 2) pillar.anchored = true pillar.material = enum.material.neon pillar.color = color3.fromhsv(i / count, 1, 1) pillar.position = center + vector3.new( math.cos(angle) * radius, 6, math.sin(angle) * radius ) pillar.parent = folder end folder.parent = workspace debris:additem(folder, 10) return folder end spawnring(vector3.new(0, 0, 0), 12, 20) write a script that creates 5 parts in a vertical stack, each a different color, each named &quot;block1&quot; through &quot;block5&quot;, all inside a folder called &quot;tower&quot;."><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge">14</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">Instances</span><span class="ctag">Creating, copying, moving, and deleting objects from code instead of by hand.</span></span><span class="cmeta">9:26</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button><button class="yt" type="button" data-vid="dt6KkNXOeEs" data-n="14" data-title="Instances"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M8 5v14l11-7z"/></svg>Watch</button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">instance</span><span class="chip">new</span><span class="chip">clone</span><span class="chip">destroy</span><span class="chip">hierarchy</span></div><h3 class="bh">What an Instance is</h3><p>Every single thing in Explorer is an Instance: Parts, Models, Folders, Scripts, Humanoids, GUIs, Sounds, Values. They all share the same core methods and the same Parent-based hierarchy.</p><h3 class="bh">Instance.new</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> part <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Part"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

part<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"MyPart"</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Size <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Vector3</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">4</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">4</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">4</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Position <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Vector3</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">10</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">BrickColor</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">BrickColor</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Bright blue"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Material <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Enum</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Material<span class="c-op">.</span>Neon
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Anchored <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span>

part<span class="c-op">.</span>Parent <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">workspace</span>   <span class="c-com">-- set Parent LAST</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>A new Instance has <code>Parent = nil</code>, meaning it exists in memory but is nowhere in the game. <b>Nothing appears until you set Parent.</b> Forgetting this line is the classic "my script runs but nothing happens" bug.</div></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>Set <code>Parent</code> <b>last</b>, after all other properties. Parenting first makes Roblox replicate every subsequent property change to all clients one at a time. Parenting last sends one clean update.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Things you'll create often</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Part"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>           <span class="c-com">-- a brick</span>
<span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Folder"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>         <span class="c-com">-- organization</span>
<span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"IntValue"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>       <span class="c-com">-- a whole number container (.Value)</span>
<span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"NumberValue"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>    <span class="c-com">-- a decimal container</span>
<span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"StringValue"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>    <span class="c-com">-- a text container</span>
<span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"BoolValue"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>      <span class="c-com">-- a true/false container</span>
<span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"ObjectValue"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>    <span class="c-com">-- a reference to another Instance</span>
<span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Sound"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>          <span class="c-com">-- audio</span>
<span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"ClickDetector"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>  <span class="c-com">-- makes a part clickable</span>
<span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"ProximityPrompt"</span><span class="c-op">)--</span> <span class="c-str">"press E"</span> interaction
<span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Attachment"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>     <span class="c-com">-- anchor point for effects/constraints</span>
<span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Highlight"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>      <span class="c-com">-- outline glow on a model</span>
<span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"BillboardGui"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">-- UI that floats in 3D space</span></code></pre></div><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- Instance.new accepts the parent as a second argument,</span>
<span class="c-com">-- but for the performance reason above, avoid it:</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> part <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Part"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-api">workspace</span><span class="c-op">)</span>  <span class="c-com">-- works, but not recommended</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Clone</h3><p>Copying a pre-built template is far easier than constructing complex objects property by property. Store the original in ServerStorage or ReplicatedStorage so it isn't in the world.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-api">ServerStorage</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetService</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"ServerStorage"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> template <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">ServerStorage</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">WaitForChild</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"CoinModel"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-kw">for</span> i <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">10</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> coin <span class="c-op">=</span> template<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Clone</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
	coin<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">PivotTo</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">CFrame</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span>i <span class="c-op">*</span> <span class="c-num">10</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">5</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">))</span>   <span class="c-com">-- Models use PivotTo, not Position</span>
	coin<span class="c-op">.</span>Parent <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">workspace</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><p><code>:Clone()</code> copies the object <b>and everything inside it</b> &mdash; scripts, children, all of it. The copy also starts with <code>Parent = nil</code>.</p><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>A <b>Model</b> has no <code>Position</code> property &mdash; only Parts do. Setting <code>model.Position</code> throws <i>"Position is not a valid member of Model"</i>. Move a whole model with <code>model:PivotTo(CFrame.new(x, y, z))</code>.</div></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>An object with <code>Archivable = false</code> returns <code>nil</code> from <code>:Clone()</code>. Player Characters are the common case &mdash; you can't simply clone a player's avatar.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Destroy</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code>part<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Destroy</span><span class="c-op">()</span>      <span class="c-com">-- removes it, disconnects its events, frees memory</span>

part<span class="c-op">.</span>Parent <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">nil</span>   <span class="c-com">-- removes it from the world but keeps it alive</span>

<span class="c-com">-- Debris: auto-destroy after N seconds</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-api">Debris</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetService</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Debris"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-api">Debris</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">AddItem</span><span class="c-op">(</span>part<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">5</span><span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">-- gone in 5 seconds, no waiting needed</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div><code>:Destroy()</code> is permanent. There is no undo, and any variable still pointing at the object now refers to a locked, unusable instance. <code>:Remove()</code> is deprecated &mdash; don't use it.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Navigating the hierarchy</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-api">game</span>                             <span class="c-com">-- the root of everything</span>
<span class="c-api">workspace</span>                        <span class="c-com">-- shortcut for game.Workspace</span>
<span class="c-api">script</span>                           <span class="c-com">-- the Script this code is in</span>
<span class="c-api">script</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Parent                    <span class="c-com">-- whatever contains the Script</span>
<span class="c-api">script</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Parent<span class="c-op">.</span>Parent             <span class="c-com">-- keep going up</span>

<span class="c-api">workspace</span><span class="c-op">.</span>MyModel<span class="c-op">.</span>Head           <span class="c-com">-- dot notation, errors if missing</span>
<span class="c-api">workspace</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">FindFirstChild</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"MyModel"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">-- returns nil if missing</span>
<span class="c-api">workspace</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">WaitForChild</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"MyModel"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>     <span class="c-com">-- yields until it exists</span></code></pre></div><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Returns nil if missing?</th><th>Yields?</th><th>Use when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>.Name</code> (dot)</td><td>No &mdash; it <b>errors</b></td><td>No</td><td>You are certain it exists right now</td></tr><tr><td><code>:FindFirstChild(name)</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>It might not exist and that's fine</td></tr><tr><td><code>:WaitForChild(name)</code></td><td>Only on timeout</td><td><b>Yes</b></td><td>Client code waiting on things still loading</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>Rule of thumb: in <b>LocalScripts</b>, use <code>:WaitForChild()</code> for anything you didn't create yourself &mdash; the client often runs before objects have replicated. In <b>server Scripts</b>, dot notation is usually safe.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Listing children</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">for</span> _<span class="c-op">,</span> child <span class="c-kw">in</span> <span class="c-api">workspace</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetChildren</span><span class="c-op">()</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> child<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">IsA</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"BasePart"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		child<span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">BrickColor</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">BrickColor</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-com">-- GetDescendants goes all the way down, not just one level</span>
<span class="c-kw">for</span> _<span class="c-op">,</span> obj <span class="c-kw">in</span> model<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetDescendants</span><span class="c-op">()</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> obj<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">IsA</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"BasePart"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		obj<span class="c-op">.</span>Anchored <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><p><code>:IsA()</code> understands inheritance. <code>Part</code>, <code>WedgePart</code>, <code>MeshPart</code>, and <code>UnionOperation</code> are all <code>BasePart</code>, so <code>:IsA("BasePart")</code> catches every kind of brick at once.</p><h3 class="bh">Attributes (the modern alternative to Value objects)</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code>part<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">SetAttribute</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Damage"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">25</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>part<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetAttribute</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Damage"</span><span class="c-op">))</span>   <span class="c-com">--&gt; 25</span>

part<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetAttributeChangedSignal</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Damage"</span><span class="c-op">):</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"damage is now "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> part<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetAttribute</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Damage"</span><span class="c-op">))</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-kw">for</span> name<span class="c-op">,</span> value <span class="c-kw">in</span> part<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetAttributes</span><span class="c-op">()</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>name<span class="c-op">,</span> value<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>Attributes are lighter than IntValue/StringValue objects, show up in the Properties window, and replicate automatically. For storing simple data on an object, prefer them over Value instances.</div></div><h3 class="bh">A full example</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- Spawn a ring of glowing pillars that vanish after 10 seconds</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-api">Debris</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetService</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Debris"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">spawnRing</span><span class="c-op">(</span>center<span class="c-op">,</span> count<span class="c-op">,</span> radius<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> folder <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Folder"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	folder<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Ring"</span>

	<span class="c-kw">for</span> i <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> count <span class="c-kw">do</span>
		<span class="c-kw">local</span> angle <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">(</span>i <span class="c-op">/</span> count<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-op">*</span> <span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span>pi <span class="c-op">*</span> <span class="c-num">2</span>
		<span class="c-kw">local</span> pillar <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Part"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
		pillar<span class="c-op">.</span>Size <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Vector3</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">2</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">12</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">2</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
		pillar<span class="c-op">.</span>Anchored <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span>
		pillar<span class="c-op">.</span>Material <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Enum</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Material<span class="c-op">.</span>Neon
		pillar<span class="c-op">.</span>Color <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Color3</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">fromHSV</span><span class="c-op">(</span>i <span class="c-op">/</span> count<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
		pillar<span class="c-op">.</span>Position <span class="c-op">=</span> center <span class="c-op">+</span> <span class="c-api">Vector3</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span>
			<span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">cos</span><span class="c-op">(</span>angle<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-op">*</span> radius<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">6</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">sin</span><span class="c-op">(</span>angle<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-op">*</span> radius
		<span class="c-op">)</span>
		pillar<span class="c-op">.</span>Parent <span class="c-op">=</span> folder
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>

	folder<span class="c-op">.</span>Parent <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">workspace</span>
	<span class="c-api">Debris</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">AddItem</span><span class="c-op">(</span>folder<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">10</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">return</span> folder
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-fn">spawnRing</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">Vector3</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">),</span> <span class="c-num">12</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">20</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout try"><span class="ci">&#9654;</span><div><b>Try it yourself &mdash; </b>Write a script that creates 5 parts in a vertical stack, each a different color, each named "Block1" through "Block5", all inside a Folder called "Tower".</div></div></div></div></article>
<article class="card" id="ep15" data-n="15" data-search="15 breaks / loop breaking escaping a loop early, and skipping a single pass without leaving. break continue loops break break exits the loop immediately. everything after it in the current pass is skipped, and the loop does not run again. for i = 1, 10 do if i == 5 then break end print(i) end --&gt; 1 2 3 4 (5 through 10 never happen) the classic search pattern local target = nil for _, part in workspace:getchildren() do if part.name == &quot;treasure&quot; then target = part break -- found it, stop wasting time end end if target then print(&quot;found at &quot; .. tostring(target.position)) else print(&quot;not found&quot;) end breaking out of while true local attempts = 0 while true do attempts += 1 print(&quot;attempt &quot; .. attempts) if attempts &gt;= 5 then print(&quot;giving up&quot;) break end task.wait(1) end print(&quot;loop is done&quot;) -- execution continues here while true do … break is often more readable than cramming a complicated condition into the while line, especially when there are several different reasons to stop. continue (luau) continue skips the rest of this pass and jumps straight to the next one. the loop keeps going. plain lua doesn&#x27;t have this; luau does. for i = 1, 10 do if i % 2 == 0 then continue -- skip even numbers end print(i) end --&gt; 1 3 5 7 9 -- filtering while iterating for _, obj in workspace:getchildren() do if not obj:isa(&quot;basepart&quot;) then continue end if obj.anchored then continue end obj.brickcolor = brickcolor.new(&quot;really red&quot;) end continue must be the last statement in its block, same rule as return . inside a repeat … until loop it&#x27;s not allowed at all, because the until-condition can reference locals from the body. break vs continue vs return keyword effect scope break leave the loop entirely the innermost loop only continue skip to the next pass the innermost loop only return leave the whole function exits every enclosing loop too nested loops for i = 1, 3 do for j = 1, 3 do if j == 2 then break -- only breaks the inner loop end print(i, j) end end --&gt; 1 1 / 2 1 / 3 1 lua has no labeled break. to escape both loops at once you have two clean options: -- option a: wrap it in a function and return local function findpair() for i = 1, 10 do for j = 1, 10 do if i * j == 42 then return i, j -- exits both loops end end end end -- option b: a flag local done = false for i = 1, 10 do for j = 1, 10 do if i * j == 42 then done = true break end end if done then break end end loop through workspace looking for the first part named &quot;key&quot;. break as soon as you find it, and print how many objects you had to check."><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge">15</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">Breaks / Loop Breaking</span><span class="ctag">Escaping a loop early, and skipping a single pass without leaving.</span></span><span class="cmeta">6:30</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button><button class="yt" type="button" data-vid="PIg1PCfg1Z4" data-n="15" data-title="Breaks / Loop Breaking"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M8 5v14l11-7z"/></svg>Watch</button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">break</span><span class="chip">continue</span><span class="chip">loops</span></div><h3 class="bh">break</h3><p><code>break</code> exits the loop immediately. Everything after it in the current pass is skipped, and the loop does not run again.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">for</span> i <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">10</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> i <span class="c-op">==</span> <span class="c-num">5</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		<span class="c-kw">break</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>i<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-com">--&gt; 1 2 3 4      (5 through 10 never happen)</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">The classic search pattern</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> target <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">nil</span>

<span class="c-kw">for</span> _<span class="c-op">,</span> part <span class="c-kw">in</span> <span class="c-api">workspace</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetChildren</span><span class="c-op">()</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> part<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">==</span> <span class="c-str">"Treasure"</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		target <span class="c-op">=</span> part
		<span class="c-kw">break</span>            <span class="c-com">-- found it, stop wasting time</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-kw">if</span> target <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Found at "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-api">tostring</span><span class="c-op">(</span>target<span class="c-op">.</span>Position<span class="c-op">))</span>
<span class="c-kw">else</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Not found"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Breaking out of while true</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> attempts <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">0</span>

<span class="c-kw">while</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	attempts <span class="c-op">+=</span> <span class="c-num">1</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"attempt "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> attempts<span class="c-op">)</span>

	<span class="c-kw">if</span> attempts <span class="c-op">&gt;=</span> <span class="c-num">5</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"giving up"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
		<span class="c-kw">break</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>

	<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">wait</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"loop is done"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">-- execution continues here</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div><code>while true do &hellip; break</code> is often more readable than cramming a complicated condition into the <code>while</code> line, especially when there are several different reasons to stop.</div></div><h3 class="bh">continue (Luau)</h3><p><code>continue</code> skips the <b>rest of this pass</b> and jumps straight to the next one. The loop keeps going. Plain Lua doesn't have this; Luau does.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">for</span> i <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">10</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> i <span class="c-op">%</span> <span class="c-num">2</span> <span class="c-op">==</span> <span class="c-num">0</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		<span class="c-kw">continue</span>          <span class="c-com">-- skip even numbers</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>i<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-com">--&gt; 1 3 5 7 9</span></code></pre></div><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- filtering while iterating</span>
<span class="c-kw">for</span> _<span class="c-op">,</span> obj <span class="c-kw">in</span> <span class="c-api">workspace</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetChildren</span><span class="c-op">()</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-kw">not</span> obj<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">IsA</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"BasePart"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">continue</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> obj<span class="c-op">.</span>Anchored <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">continue</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>

	obj<span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">BrickColor</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">BrickColor</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Really red"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div><code>continue</code> must be the last statement in its block, same rule as <code>return</code>. Inside a <code>repeat &hellip; until</code> loop it's not allowed at all, because the until-condition can reference locals from the body.</div></div><h3 class="bh">break vs continue vs return</h3><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Keyword</th><th>Effect</th><th>Scope</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>break</code></td><td>Leave the loop entirely</td><td>The innermost loop only</td></tr><tr><td><code>continue</code></td><td>Skip to the next pass</td><td>The innermost loop only</td></tr><tr><td><code>return</code></td><td>Leave the whole <b>function</b></td><td>Exits every enclosing loop too</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class="bh">Nested loops</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">for</span> i <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">3</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-kw">for</span> j <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">3</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
		<span class="c-kw">if</span> j <span class="c-op">==</span> <span class="c-num">2</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
			<span class="c-kw">break</span>         <span class="c-com">-- only breaks the INNER loop</span>
		<span class="c-kw">end</span>
		<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>i<span class="c-op">,</span> j<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-com">--&gt; 1 1 / 2 1 / 3 1</span></code></pre></div><p>Lua has no labeled break. To escape both loops at once you have two clean options:</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- Option A: wrap it in a function and return</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">findPair</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
	<span class="c-kw">for</span> i <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">10</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
		<span class="c-kw">for</span> j <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">10</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
			<span class="c-kw">if</span> i <span class="c-op">*</span> j <span class="c-op">==</span> <span class="c-num">42</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
				<span class="c-kw">return</span> i<span class="c-op">,</span> j       <span class="c-com">-- exits BOTH loops</span>
			<span class="c-kw">end</span>
		<span class="c-kw">end</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-com">-- Option B: a flag</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> done <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">false</span>
<span class="c-kw">for</span> i <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">10</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-kw">for</span> j <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">10</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
		<span class="c-kw">if</span> i <span class="c-op">*</span> j <span class="c-op">==</span> <span class="c-num">42</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span> done <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span> <span class="c-kw">break</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> done <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">break</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout try"><span class="ci">&#9654;</span><div><b>Try it yourself &mdash; </b>Loop through Workspace looking for the first part named "Key". Break as soon as you find it, and print how many objects you had to check.</div></div></div></div></article>
<article class="card" id="ep16" data-n="16" data-search="16 random generating random numbers, colors, positions, and picks — the right way and the legacy way. random math.random random.new math.random math.random() --&gt; a decimal between 0 and 1, e.g. 0.472 math.random(10) --&gt; an integer from 1 to 10 math.random(5, 15) --&gt; an integer from 5 to 15 (both ends included) math.random(-10, 10) --&gt; negatives are fine math.random(1, 10) returns integers only. for a random decimal in a range, do the math yourself: local x = min + math.random() * (max - min) random.new — the modern approach random.new() creates an independent generator object. it&#x27;s cleaner, doesn&#x27;t need seeding, and won&#x27;t be affected by other scripts. prefer it in new code. local rng = random.new() print(rng:nextinteger(1, 100)) -- integer, both ends included print(rng:nextnumber()) -- decimal 0 to 1 print(rng:nextnumber(5, 10)) -- decimal in a range print(rng:nextunitvector()) -- a random direction (vector3) -- give it a seed to get the same sequence every run (great for testing) local fixed = random.new(12345) print(fixed:nextinteger(1, 100)) -- identical every time you run it the old math.randomseed(tick()) line you&#x27;ll see in older tutorials is no longer needed — roblox seeds the generator automatically now. it does no harm, it&#x27;s just noise. picking a random item from a table local weapons = {&quot;sword&quot;, &quot;bow&quot;, &quot;staff&quot;, &quot;dagger&quot;} local pick = weapons[math.random(1, #weapons)] print(pick) -- as a reusable function local function randomfrom(list) return list[math.random(1, #list)] end random colors -- brickcolor has this built in part.brickcolor = brickcolor.random() -- color3 from raw rgb part.color = color3.fromrgb( math.random(0, 255), math.random(0, 255), math.random(0, 255) ) -- hsv gives much nicer, always-vivid colors part.color = color3.fromhsv(math.random(), 1, 1) pure rgb randomness produces a lot of muddy browns and greys. color3.fromhsv(math.random(), 0.8, 1) randomizes only the hue and keeps saturation and brightness high, so every result looks good. random positions -- anywhere in a 200x200 square at ground level local pos = vector3.new( math.random(-100, 100), 5, math.random(-100, 100) ) -- a random point on a circle of radius 50 local angle = math.random() * math.pi * 2 local pos2 = vector3.new(math.cos(angle) * 50, 5, math.sin(angle) * 50) -- a random direction local dir = random.new():nextunitvector() random chance -- 25% chance if math.random(1, 100) separate random generators. if the server rolls a color and the client rolls one too, they will disagree. decide randomness on one side and send the result across with a remoteevent. build a chest that, when clicked, spawns a random item from a list of five at a random position within 10 studs, with a random neon color — and gives legendary only 5% of the time."><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge">16</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">Random</span><span class="ctag">Generating random numbers, colors, positions, and picks &amp;mdash; the right way and the legacy way.</span></span><span class="cmeta">10:31</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button><button class="yt" type="button" data-vid="4LwJ9tsyYhY" data-n="16" data-title="Random"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M8 5v14l11-7z"/></svg>Watch</button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">random</span><span class="chip">math.random</span><span class="chip">Random.new</span></div><h3 class="bh">math.random</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">()</span>          <span class="c-com">--&gt; a decimal between 0 and 1, e.g. 0.472</span>
<span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">10</span><span class="c-op">)</span>        <span class="c-com">--&gt; an integer from 1 to 10</span>
<span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">5</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">15</span><span class="c-op">)</span>     <span class="c-com">--&gt; an integer from 5 to 15 (both ends included)</span>
<span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">(-</span><span class="c-num">10</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">10</span><span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">--&gt; negatives are fine</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div><code>math.random(1, 10)</code> returns integers only. For a random decimal in a range, do the math yourself:<br><code>local x = min + math.random() * (max - min)</code></div></div><h3 class="bh">Random.new &mdash; the modern approach</h3><p><code>Random.new()</code> creates an independent generator object. It's cleaner, doesn't need seeding, and won't be affected by other scripts. Prefer it in new code.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> rng <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Random</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">()</span>

<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>rng<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">NextInteger</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">100</span><span class="c-op">))</span>    <span class="c-com">-- integer, both ends included</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>rng<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">NextNumber</span><span class="c-op">())</span>           <span class="c-com">-- decimal 0 to 1</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>rng<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">NextNumber</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">5</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">10</span><span class="c-op">))</span>      <span class="c-com">-- decimal in a range</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>rng<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">NextUnitVector</span><span class="c-op">())</span>       <span class="c-com">-- a random direction (Vector3)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- give it a seed to get the SAME sequence every run (great for testing)</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> fixed <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Random</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">12345</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>fixed<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">NextInteger</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">100</span><span class="c-op">))</span>  <span class="c-com">-- identical every time you run it</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>The old <code>math.randomseed(tick())</code> line you'll see in older tutorials is no longer needed &mdash; Roblox seeds the generator automatically now. It does no harm, it's just noise.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Picking a random item from a table</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> weapons <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span><span class="c-str">"Sword"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"Bow"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"Staff"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"Dagger"</span><span class="c-op">}</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> pick <span class="c-op">=</span> weapons<span class="c-op">[</span><span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-op">#</span>weapons<span class="c-op">)]</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>pick<span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- as a reusable function</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">randomFrom</span><span class="c-op">(</span>list<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">return</span> list<span class="c-op">[</span><span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-op">#</span>list<span class="c-op">)]</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Random colors</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- BrickColor has this built in</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">BrickColor</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">BrickColor</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">()</span>

<span class="c-com">-- Color3 from raw RGB</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Color <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Color3</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">fromRGB</span><span class="c-op">(</span>
	<span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">255</span><span class="c-op">),</span>
	<span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">255</span><span class="c-op">),</span>
	<span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">255</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- HSV gives much nicer, always-vivid colors</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Color <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Color3</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">fromHSV</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">(),</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>Pure RGB randomness produces a lot of muddy browns and greys. <code>Color3.fromHSV(math.random(), 0.8, 1)</code> randomizes only the hue and keeps saturation and brightness high, so every result looks good.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Random positions</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- anywhere in a 200x200 square at ground level</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> pos <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Vector3</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span>
	<span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">(-</span><span class="c-num">100</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">100</span><span class="c-op">),</span>
	<span class="c-num">5</span><span class="c-op">,</span>
	<span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">(-</span><span class="c-num">100</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">100</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- a random point on a circle of radius 50</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> angle <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">()</span> <span class="c-op">*</span> <span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span>pi <span class="c-op">*</span> <span class="c-num">2</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> pos2 <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Vector3</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">cos</span><span class="c-op">(</span>angle<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-op">*</span> <span class="c-num">50</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">5</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">sin</span><span class="c-op">(</span>angle<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-op">*</span> <span class="c-num">50</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- a random direction</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> dir <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Random</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">():</span><span class="c-fn">NextUnitVector</span><span class="c-op">()</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Random chance</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- 25% chance</span>
<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">100</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-op">&lt;=</span> <span class="c-num">25</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"lucky!"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-com">-- same thing with decimals</span>
<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">()</span> <span class="c-op">&lt;</span> <span class="c-num">0.25</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"lucky!"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-com">-- a coin flip</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> heads <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">2</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-op">==</span> <span class="c-num">1</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Weighted random (loot tables)</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> loot <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span>
	<span class="c-op">{</span>name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Common"</span><span class="c-op">,</span>    weight <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">70</span><span class="c-op">},</span>
	<span class="c-op">{</span>name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Rare"</span><span class="c-op">,</span>      weight <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">25</span><span class="c-op">},</span>
	<span class="c-op">{</span>name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Legendary"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> weight <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">5</span><span class="c-op">},</span>
<span class="c-op">}</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">rollLoot</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> total <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">0</span>
	<span class="c-kw">for</span> _<span class="c-op">,</span> item <span class="c-kw">in</span> loot <span class="c-kw">do</span>
		total <span class="c-op">+=</span> item<span class="c-op">.</span>weight
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>

	<span class="c-kw">local</span> roll <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">()</span> <span class="c-op">*</span> total
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> running <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">0</span>

	<span class="c-kw">for</span> _<span class="c-op">,</span> item <span class="c-kw">in</span> loot <span class="c-kw">do</span>
		running <span class="c-op">+=</span> item<span class="c-op">.</span>weight
		<span class="c-kw">if</span> roll <span class="c-op">&lt;=</span> running <span class="c-kw">then</span>
			<span class="c-kw">return</span> item<span class="c-op">.</span>name
		<span class="c-kw">end</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-fn">rollLoot</span><span class="c-op">())</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Shuffling a table</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">shuffle</span><span class="c-op">(</span>list<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">for</span> i <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">#</span>list<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">2</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-op">-</span><span class="c-num">1</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
		<span class="c-kw">local</span> j <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> i<span class="c-op">)</span>
		list<span class="c-op">[</span>i<span class="c-op">],</span> list<span class="c-op">[</span>j<span class="c-op">]</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> list<span class="c-op">[</span>j<span class="c-op">],</span> list<span class="c-op">[</span>i<span class="c-op">]</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
	<span class="c-kw">return</span> list
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> deck <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span><span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span><span class="c-num">2</span><span class="c-op">,</span><span class="c-num">3</span><span class="c-op">,</span><span class="c-num">4</span><span class="c-op">,</span><span class="c-num">5</span><span class="c-op">,</span><span class="c-num">6</span><span class="c-op">,</span><span class="c-num">7</span><span class="c-op">,</span><span class="c-num">8</span><span class="c-op">,</span><span class="c-num">9</span><span class="c-op">,</span><span class="c-num">10</span><span class="c-op">}</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">table</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">concat</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-fn">shuffle</span><span class="c-op">(</span>deck<span class="c-op">),</span> <span class="c-str">", "</span><span class="c-op">))</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>Server and client have <b>separate</b> random generators. If the server rolls a color and the client rolls one too, they will disagree. Decide randomness on <b>one</b> side and send the result across with a RemoteEvent.</div></div><div class="callout try"><span class="ci">&#9654;</span><div><b>Try it yourself &mdash; </b>Build a chest that, when clicked, spawns a random item from a list of five at a random position within 10 studs, with a random neon color &mdash; and gives Legendary only 5% of the time.</div></div></div></div></article>
<article class="card" id="ep17" data-n="17" data-search="17 leaderboards / leaderstats the player list scoreboard: exact naming rules, and how to update stats. leaderstats leaderboard intvalue playeradded how leaderstats works roblox has one hardcoded rule: if a folder named exactly leaderstats exists inside a player, every value object inside it becomes a column in the top-right player list. the value object&#x27;s name is the column header and its value is the number shown. the name must be leaderstats — all one word, lowercase &#x27;l&#x27;, lowercase &#x27;s&#x27;. leaderstats , leaderstats , and leaderstats all silently do nothing. this is the single most common mistake in this episode. the minimum working script put this in a script inside serverscriptservice . local players = game:getservice(&quot;players&quot;) players.playeradded:connect(function(player) local leaderstats = instance.new(&quot;folder&quot;) leaderstats.name = &quot;leaderstats&quot; leaderstats.parent = player local coins = instance.new(&quot;intvalue&quot;) coins.name = &quot;coins&quot; coins.value = 0 coins.parent = leaderstats local wins = instance.new(&quot;intvalue&quot;) wins.name = &quot;wins&quot; wins.value = 0 wins.parent = leaderstats end) press play. you now have a scoreboard with coins and wins columns. which value type to use type holds use for intvalue whole numbers coins, wins, kills, levels — almost always this one numbervalue decimals time survived, distance, multipliers stringvalue text rank names, team names, titles boolvalue true/false flags (won&#x27;t display usefully on the leaderboard) intvalue silently truncates decimals. setting it to 2.7 stores 2 . if you need fractions, use numbervalue. changing a stat local coins = player.leaderstats.coins coins.value += 10 -- add coins.value -= 5 -- subtract coins.value = 100 -- set outright print(coins.value) -- the .value is required, always player.leaderstats.coins = 100 errors. you&#x27;re trying to replace the instance rather than change its contents. it&#x27;s always .value . a safe helper function local function addcoins(player, amount) local stats = player:findfirstchild(&quot;leaderstats&quot;) if not stats then return end local coins = stats:findfirstchild(&quot;coins&quot;) if not coins then return end coins.value += amount end awarding coins for touching a part local players = game:getservice(&quot;players&quot;) local part = script.parent local cooldowns = {} part.touched:connect(function(hit) local player = players:getplayerfromcharacter(hit.parent) if not player then return end if cooldowns[player] then return end cooldowns[player] = true player.leaderstats.coins.value += 5 task.wait(3) cooldowns[player] = nil end) reacting to a stat change coins.changed:connect(function(newvalue) print(player.name .. &quot; now has &quot; .. newvalue .. &quot; coins&quot;) if newvalue &gt;= 1000 then print(&quot;achievement unlocked!&quot;) end end) saving stats between sessions (datastore preview) leaderstats resets to zero every time a player rejoins. making it persist needs datastoreservice , which comes after this series — but here&#x27;s the shape of it so you know what you&#x27;re aiming at. local datastoreservice = game:getservice(&quot;datastoreservice&quot;) local players = game:getservice(&quot;players&quot;) local store = datastoreservice:getdatastore(&quot;playercoins_v1&quot;) players.playeradded:connect(function(player) local leaderstats = instance.new(&quot;folder&quot;) leaderstats.name = &quot;leaderstats&quot; leaderstats.parent = player local coins = instance.new(&quot;intvalue&quot;) coins.name = &quot;coins&quot; coins.parent = leaderstats -- always wrap datastore calls in pcall: the network can fail local success, saved = pcall(function() return store:getasync(player.userid) end) if not success then -- critical: a failed load must not be treated as &quot;new player&quot;. -- if you save 0 on exit you have just deleted their progress. warn(&quot;load failed for &quot; .. player.name .. &quot;: &quot; .. tostring(saved)) player:setattribute(&quot;dataloadfailed&quot;, true) return end coins.value = saved or 0 -- nil here genuinely means a brand-new player end) players.playerremoving:connect(function(player) if player:getattribute(&quot;dataloadfailed&quot;) then return end -- never overwrite local stats = player:findfirstchild(&quot;leaderstats&quot;) if not stats then return end pcall(function() store:setasync(player.userid, stats.coins.value) end) end) datastores only work in a published game with studio access to api services enabled (game settings → security). they will silently fail in an unpublished place. for a global top-100 board displayed on a wall, you need an ordereddatastore plus a surfacegui. leaderstats only shows the players currently in your server. give players coins and a rank stringvalue. make rank auto-update to &quot;newbie&quot;, &quot;pro&quot;, or &quot;legend&quot; based on their coin count using a changed connection."><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge">17</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">Leaderboards / leaderstats</span><span class="ctag">The player list scoreboard: exact naming rules, and how to update stats.</span></span><span class="cmeta">12:47</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button><button class="yt" type="button" data-vid="2fX5DRjLMuQ" data-n="17" data-title="Leaderboards / leaderstats"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M8 5v14l11-7z"/></svg>Watch</button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">leaderstats</span><span class="chip">leaderboard</span><span class="chip">intvalue</span><span class="chip">playeradded</span></div><h3 class="bh">How leaderstats works</h3><p>Roblox has one hardcoded rule: if a <b>Folder named exactly <code>leaderstats</code></b> exists inside a Player, every Value object inside it becomes a column in the top-right player list. The Value object's <b>Name</b> is the column header and its <b>Value</b> is the number shown.</p><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>The name must be <code>leaderstats</code> &mdash; all one word, lowercase 'l', lowercase 's'. <code>LeaderStats</code>, <code>Leaderstats</code>, and <code>leaderStats</code> all silently do nothing. This is the single most common mistake in this episode.</div></div><h3 class="bh">The minimum working script</h3><p>Put this in a <b>Script</b> inside <b>ServerScriptService</b>.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-api">Players</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetService</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Players"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-api">Players</span><span class="c-op">.</span>PlayerAdded<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> leaderstats <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Folder"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	leaderstats<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"leaderstats"</span>
	leaderstats<span class="c-op">.</span>Parent <span class="c-op">=</span> player

	<span class="c-kw">local</span> coins <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"IntValue"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Coins"</span>
	coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Value <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">0</span>
	coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Parent <span class="c-op">=</span> leaderstats

	<span class="c-kw">local</span> wins <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"IntValue"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	wins<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Wins"</span>
	wins<span class="c-op">.</span>Value <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">0</span>
	wins<span class="c-op">.</span>Parent <span class="c-op">=</span> leaderstats
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><p>Press Play. You now have a scoreboard with Coins and Wins columns.</p><h3 class="bh">Which Value type to use</h3><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Holds</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>IntValue</code></td><td>Whole numbers</td><td>Coins, wins, kills, levels &mdash; almost always this one</td></tr><tr><td><code>NumberValue</code></td><td>Decimals</td><td>Time survived, distance, multipliers</td></tr><tr><td><code>StringValue</code></td><td>Text</td><td>Rank names, team names, titles</td></tr><tr><td><code>BoolValue</code></td><td>true/false</td><td>Flags (won't display usefully on the leaderboard)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div><code>IntValue</code> silently truncates decimals. Setting it to <code>2.7</code> stores <code>2</code>. If you need fractions, use NumberValue.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Changing a stat</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> coins <span class="c-op">=</span> player<span class="c-op">.</span>leaderstats<span class="c-op">.</span>Coins

coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Value <span class="c-op">+=</span> <span class="c-num">10</span>      <span class="c-com">-- add</span>
coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Value <span class="c-op">-=</span> <span class="c-num">5</span>       <span class="c-com">-- subtract</span>
coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Value <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">100</span>      <span class="c-com">-- set outright</span>

<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Value<span class="c-op">)</span>     <span class="c-com">-- the .Value is required, always</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div><code>player.leaderstats.Coins = 100</code> errors. You're trying to replace the Instance rather than change its contents. It's always <code>.Value</code>.</div></div><h3 class="bh">A safe helper function</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">addCoins</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">,</span> amount<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> stats <span class="c-op">=</span> player<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">FindFirstChild</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"leaderstats"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-kw">not</span> stats <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>

	<span class="c-kw">local</span> coins <span class="c-op">=</span> stats<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">FindFirstChild</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Coins"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-kw">not</span> coins <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>

	coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Value <span class="c-op">+=</span> amount
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Awarding coins for touching a part</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-api">Players</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetService</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Players"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> part <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">script</span><span class="c-op">.</span>Parent
<span class="c-kw">local</span> cooldowns <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{}</span>

part<span class="c-op">.</span>Touched<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>hit<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> player <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Players</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetPlayerFromCharacter</span><span class="c-op">(</span>hit<span class="c-op">.</span>Parent<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-kw">not</span> player <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> cooldowns<span class="c-op">[</span>player<span class="c-op">]</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>

	cooldowns<span class="c-op">[</span>player<span class="c-op">]</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span>
	player<span class="c-op">.</span>leaderstats<span class="c-op">.</span>Coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Value <span class="c-op">+=</span> <span class="c-num">5</span>
	<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">wait</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">3</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	cooldowns<span class="c-op">[</span>player<span class="c-op">]</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">nil</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Reacting to a stat change</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code>coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Changed<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>newValue<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" now has "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> newValue <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" coins"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

	<span class="c-kw">if</span> newValue <span class="c-op">&gt;=</span> <span class="c-num">1000</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Achievement unlocked!"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Saving stats between sessions (DataStore preview)</h3><p>leaderstats resets to zero every time a player rejoins. Making it persist needs <b>DataStoreService</b>, which comes after this series &mdash; but here's the shape of it so you know what you're aiming at.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-api">DataStoreService</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetService</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"DataStoreService"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-api">Players</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetService</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Players"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> store <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">DataStoreService</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetDataStore</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"PlayerCoins_v1"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-api">Players</span><span class="c-op">.</span>PlayerAdded<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> leaderstats <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Folder"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	leaderstats<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"leaderstats"</span>
	leaderstats<span class="c-op">.</span>Parent <span class="c-op">=</span> player

	<span class="c-kw">local</span> coins <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">Instance</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"IntValue"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Coins"</span>
	coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Parent <span class="c-op">=</span> leaderstats

	<span class="c-com">-- ALWAYS wrap DataStore calls in pcall: the network can fail</span>
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> success<span class="c-op">,</span> saved <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">pcall</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
		<span class="c-kw">return</span> store<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetAsync</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">.</span>UserId<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

	<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-kw">not</span> success <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		<span class="c-com">-- CRITICAL: a failed LOAD must not be treated as "new player".</span>
		<span class="c-com">-- If you save 0 on exit you have just deleted their progress.</span>
		<span class="c-api">warn</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Load failed for "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> player<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">": "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-api">tostring</span><span class="c-op">(</span>saved<span class="c-op">))</span>
		player<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">SetAttribute</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"DataLoadFailed"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
		<span class="c-kw">return</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>

	coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Value <span class="c-op">=</span> saved <span class="c-kw">or</span> <span class="c-num">0</span>   <span class="c-com">-- nil here genuinely means a brand-new player</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-api">Players</span><span class="c-op">.</span>PlayerRemoving<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Connect</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> player<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetAttribute</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"DataLoadFailed"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>   <span class="c-com">-- never overwrite</span>

	<span class="c-kw">local</span> stats <span class="c-op">=</span> player<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">FindFirstChild</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"leaderstats"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-kw">not</span> stats <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>

	<span class="c-api">pcall</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
		store<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">SetAsync</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">.</span>UserId<span class="c-op">,</span> stats<span class="c-op">.</span>Coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Value<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>DataStores only work in a <b>published</b> game with <b>Studio Access to API Services</b> enabled (Game Settings &rarr; Security). They will silently fail in an unpublished place.</div></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>For a global top-100 board displayed on a wall, you need an <b>OrderedDataStore</b> plus a SurfaceGui. leaderstats only shows the players currently in <i>your</i> server.</div></div><div class="callout try"><span class="ci">&#9654;</span><div><b>Try it yourself &mdash; </b>Give players Coins and a Rank StringValue. Make Rank auto-update to "Newbie", "Pro", or "Legend" based on their coin count using a <code>Changed</code> connection.</div></div></div></div></article>
<article class="card" id="ep18" data-n="18" data-search="18 tables storing many values in one variable. arrays, dictionaries, and the table library. tables arrays dictionaries table library arrays: numbered lists local fruits = {&quot;apple&quot;, &quot;banana&quot;, &quot;cherry&quot;} print(fruits[1]) --&gt; apple print(fruits[2]) --&gt; banana print(fruits[3]) --&gt; cherry print(#fruits) --&gt; 3 (# means &quot;how many&quot;) lua tables start at 1, not 0. this is the #1 thing that trips up anyone coming from python, javascript, c++, or java. fruits[0] is nil . dictionaries: named keys local player = { name = &quot;nay&quot;, level = 7, coins = 250, isadmin = false, } print(player.name) --&gt; nay print(player[&quot;level&quot;]) --&gt; 7 (same thing, bracket syntax) player.coins += 100 player.rank = &quot;pro&quot; -- adding a new key is just assignment player.isadmin = nil -- setting to nil deletes the key dot syntax t.name is shorthand for t[&quot;name&quot;] . use brackets when the key has spaces, starts with a digit, or is held in a variable: t[keyvariable] . # does not work on dictionaries. #player above returns 0 . only arrays with consecutive integer keys have a meaningful length. mixed and nested tables local inventory = { {name = &quot;sword&quot;, damage = 25, rarity = &quot;common&quot;}, {name = &quot;bow&quot;, damage = 15, rarity = &quot;rare&quot;}, {name = &quot;staff&quot;, damage = 40, rarity = &quot;legendary&quot;}, } print(inventory[1].name) --&gt; sword print(inventory[3].damage) --&gt; 40 local config = { spawn = {x = 0, y = 10, z = 0}, weapons = {&quot;sword&quot;, &quot;bow&quot;}, maxplayers = 12, } print(config.spawn.y) --&gt; 10 print(config.weapons[2]) --&gt; bow the table library function does table.insert(t, v) add v to the end table.insert(t, pos, v) insert v at position pos, shifting the rest right table.remove(t) remove and return the last item table.remove(t, pos) remove and return the item at pos, shifting the rest left table.find(t, v) return the index of v, or nil table.concat(t, sep) join array items into one string table.sort(t) sort in place, ascending table.sort(t, fn) sort with a custom comparison function table.clear(t) empty the table but keep the same table object table.clone(t) a shallow copy (nested tables are still shared) table.create(n, v) pre-sized array of n copies of v table.unpack(t) turn an array into separate return values local list = {&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;, &quot;c&quot;} table.insert(list, &quot;d&quot;) --&gt; {a, b, c, d} table.insert(list, 1, &quot;start&quot;) --&gt; {start, a, b, c, d} table.remove(list, 1) --&gt; {a, b, c, d} print(table.find(list, &quot;c&quot;)) --&gt; 3 print(table.concat(list, &quot;, &quot;)) --&gt; a, b, c, d local nums = {5, 2, 9, 1} table.sort(nums) print(table.concat(nums, &quot; &quot;)) --&gt; 1 2 5 9 table.sort(nums, function(a, b) return a &gt; b end) print(table.concat(nums, &quot; &quot;)) --&gt; 9 5 2 1 tables are references, not copies local a = {1, 2, 3} local b = a -- not a copy: both names point at the same table table.insert(b, 4) print(#a) --&gt; 4 (a changed too!) local c = table.clone(a) -- now this is a separate table table.insert(c, 5) print(#a, #c) --&gt; 4 5 table.clone is shallow . nested tables inside are still shared between the original and the copy. for a full deep copy you need a recursive function. using tables to track per-player state local cooldowns = {} -- keys will be player objects local function canfire(player) if cooldowns[player] then return false end cooldowns[player] = true task.delay(2, function() cooldowns[player] = nil end) return true end any value can be a key — numbers, strings, instances, even other tables. keying by player object is the standard pattern for cooldowns, scores, and per-player settings. tables as configuration local weapons = { sword = {damage = 25, speed = 1.0, cost = 100}, bow = {damage = 15, speed = 2.0, cost = 250}, staff = {damage = 40, speed = 0.5, cost = 500}, } local function buy(player, weaponname) local weapon = weapons[weaponname] if not weapon then warn(&quot;unknown weapon: &quot; .. tostring(weaponname)) return end local coins = player.leaderstats.coins if coins.value elseif chains. adding a fourth weapon is one new line in the table instead of a new branch in your logic. build a shop table with 5 items (name, price, description). write a function that prints the whole catalog, and another that looks an item up by name and returns its price or nil."><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge">18</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">Tables</span><span class="ctag">Storing many values in one variable. Arrays, dictionaries, and the table library.</span></span><span class="cmeta">12:20</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button><button class="yt" type="button" data-vid="1xuECUHqxf8" data-n="18" data-title="Tables"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M8 5v14l11-7z"/></svg>Watch</button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">tables</span><span class="chip">arrays</span><span class="chip">dictionaries</span><span class="chip">table library</span></div><h3 class="bh">Arrays: numbered lists</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> fruits <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span><span class="c-str">"apple"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"banana"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"cherry"</span><span class="c-op">}</span>

<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>fruits<span class="c-op">[</span><span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">])</span>   <span class="c-com">--&gt; apple</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>fruits<span class="c-op">[</span><span class="c-num">2</span><span class="c-op">])</span>   <span class="c-com">--&gt; banana</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>fruits<span class="c-op">[</span><span class="c-num">3</span><span class="c-op">])</span>   <span class="c-com">--&gt; cherry</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(#</span>fruits<span class="c-op">)</span>     <span class="c-com">--&gt; 3   (# means "how many")</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div><b>Lua tables start at 1, not 0.</b> This is the #1 thing that trips up anyone coming from Python, JavaScript, C++, or Java. <code>fruits[0]</code> is <code>nil</code>.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Dictionaries: named keys</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> player <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span>
	Name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Nay"</span><span class="c-op">,</span>
	Level <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">7</span><span class="c-op">,</span>
	Coins <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">250</span><span class="c-op">,</span>
	IsAdmin <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">false</span><span class="c-op">,</span>
<span class="c-op">}</span>

<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">.</span>Name<span class="c-op">)</span>         <span class="c-com">--&gt; Nay</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">[</span><span class="c-str">"Level"</span><span class="c-op">])</span>     <span class="c-com">--&gt; 7   (same thing, bracket syntax)</span>

player<span class="c-op">.</span>Coins <span class="c-op">+=</span> <span class="c-num">100</span>
player<span class="c-op">.</span>Rank <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Pro"</span>        <span class="c-com">-- adding a new key is just assignment</span>
player<span class="c-op">.</span>IsAdmin <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">nil</span>       <span class="c-com">-- setting to nil DELETES the key</span></code></pre></div><p>Dot syntax <code>t.Name</code> is shorthand for <code>t["Name"]</code>. Use brackets when the key has spaces, starts with a digit, or is held in a variable: <code>t[keyVariable]</code>.</p><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div><code>#</code> does <b>not</b> work on dictionaries. <code>#player</code> above returns <code>0</code>. Only arrays with consecutive integer keys have a meaningful length.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Mixed and nested tables</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> inventory <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span>
	<span class="c-op">{</span>name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Sword"</span><span class="c-op">,</span>  damage <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">25</span><span class="c-op">,</span> rarity <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Common"</span><span class="c-op">},</span>
	<span class="c-op">{</span>name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Bow"</span><span class="c-op">,</span>    damage <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">15</span><span class="c-op">,</span> rarity <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Rare"</span><span class="c-op">},</span>
	<span class="c-op">{</span>name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Staff"</span><span class="c-op">,</span>  damage <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">40</span><span class="c-op">,</span> rarity <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Legendary"</span><span class="c-op">},</span>
<span class="c-op">}</span>

<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>inventory<span class="c-op">[</span><span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">].</span>name<span class="c-op">)</span>      <span class="c-com">--&gt; Sword</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>inventory<span class="c-op">[</span><span class="c-num">3</span><span class="c-op">].</span>damage<span class="c-op">)</span>    <span class="c-com">--&gt; 40</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> config <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span>
	Spawn <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span>x <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">,</span> y <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">10</span><span class="c-op">,</span> z <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">0</span><span class="c-op">},</span>
	Weapons <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span><span class="c-str">"Sword"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"Bow"</span><span class="c-op">},</span>
	MaxPlayers <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">12</span><span class="c-op">,</span>
<span class="c-op">}</span>

<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>config<span class="c-op">.</span>Spawn<span class="c-op">.</span>y<span class="c-op">)</span>         <span class="c-com">--&gt; 10</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>config<span class="c-op">.</span>Weapons<span class="c-op">[</span><span class="c-num">2</span><span class="c-op">])</span>      <span class="c-com">--&gt; Bow</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">The table library</h3><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>table.insert(t, v)</code></td><td>Add v to the end</td></tr><tr><td><code>table.insert(t, pos, v)</code></td><td>Insert v at position pos, shifting the rest right</td></tr><tr><td><code>table.remove(t)</code></td><td>Remove and return the last item</td></tr><tr><td><code>table.remove(t, pos)</code></td><td>Remove and return the item at pos, shifting the rest left</td></tr><tr><td><code>table.find(t, v)</code></td><td>Return the index of v, or nil</td></tr><tr><td><code>table.concat(t, sep)</code></td><td>Join array items into one string</td></tr><tr><td><code>table.sort(t)</code></td><td>Sort in place, ascending</td></tr><tr><td><code>table.sort(t, fn)</code></td><td>Sort with a custom comparison function</td></tr><tr><td><code>table.clear(t)</code></td><td>Empty the table but keep the same table object</td></tr><tr><td><code>table.clone(t)</code></td><td>A shallow copy (nested tables are still shared)</td></tr><tr><td><code>table.create(n, v)</code></td><td>Pre-sized array of n copies of v</td></tr><tr><td><code>table.unpack(t)</code></td><td>Turn an array into separate return values</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> list <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span><span class="c-str">"a"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"b"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"c"</span><span class="c-op">}</span>

<span class="c-api">table</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">insert</span><span class="c-op">(</span>list<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"d"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>           <span class="c-com">--&gt; {a, b, c, d}</span>
<span class="c-api">table</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">insert</span><span class="c-op">(</span>list<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"start"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>    <span class="c-com">--&gt; {start, a, b, c, d}</span>
<span class="c-api">table</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">remove</span><span class="c-op">(</span>list<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">)</span>             <span class="c-com">--&gt; {a, b, c, d}</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">table</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">find</span><span class="c-op">(</span>list<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"c"</span><span class="c-op">))</span>      <span class="c-com">--&gt; 3</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">table</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">concat</span><span class="c-op">(</span>list<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">", "</span><span class="c-op">))</span>   <span class="c-com">--&gt; a, b, c, d</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> nums <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span><span class="c-num">5</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">2</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">9</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">}</span>
<span class="c-api">table</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">sort</span><span class="c-op">(</span>nums<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">table</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">concat</span><span class="c-op">(</span>nums<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">" "</span><span class="c-op">))</span>    <span class="c-com">--&gt; 1 2 5 9</span>

<span class="c-api">table</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">sort</span><span class="c-op">(</span>nums<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">(</span>a<span class="c-op">,</span> b<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">return</span> a <span class="c-op">&gt;</span> b <span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-api">table</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">concat</span><span class="c-op">(</span>nums<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">" "</span><span class="c-op">))</span>    <span class="c-com">--&gt; 9 5 2 1</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Tables are references, not copies</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> a <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span><span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">2</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">3</span><span class="c-op">}</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> b <span class="c-op">=</span> a          <span class="c-com">-- NOT a copy: both names point at the same table</span>

<span class="c-api">table</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">insert</span><span class="c-op">(</span>b<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">4</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(#</span>a<span class="c-op">)</span>            <span class="c-com">--&gt; 4   (a changed too!)</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> c <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">table</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">clone</span><span class="c-op">(</span>a<span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">-- now this IS a separate table</span>
<span class="c-api">table</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">insert</span><span class="c-op">(</span>c<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">5</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(#</span>a<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-op">#</span>c<span class="c-op">)</span>        <span class="c-com">--&gt; 4  5</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div><code>table.clone</code> is <b>shallow</b>. Nested tables inside are still shared between the original and the copy. For a full deep copy you need a recursive function.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Using tables to track per-player state</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> cooldowns <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{}</span>      <span class="c-com">-- keys will be Player objects</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">canFire</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> cooldowns<span class="c-op">[</span>player<span class="c-op">]</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-kw">false</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>
	cooldowns<span class="c-op">[</span>player<span class="c-op">]</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span>
	<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">delay</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">2</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
		cooldowns<span class="c-op">[</span>player<span class="c-op">]</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">nil</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><p>Any value can be a key &mdash; numbers, strings, Instances, even other tables. Keying by Player object is the standard pattern for cooldowns, scores, and per-player settings.</p><h3 class="bh">Tables as configuration</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> WEAPONS <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span>
	Sword  <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span>damage <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">25</span><span class="c-op">,</span> speed <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">1.0</span><span class="c-op">,</span> cost <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">100</span><span class="c-op">},</span>
	Bow    <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span>damage <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">15</span><span class="c-op">,</span> speed <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">2.0</span><span class="c-op">,</span> cost <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">250</span><span class="c-op">},</span>
	Staff  <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span>damage <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">40</span><span class="c-op">,</span> speed <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">0.5</span><span class="c-op">,</span> cost <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">500</span><span class="c-op">},</span>
<span class="c-op">}</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">buy</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">,</span> weaponName<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> weapon <span class="c-op">=</span> WEAPONS<span class="c-op">[</span>weaponName<span class="c-op">]</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-kw">not</span> weapon <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		<span class="c-api">warn</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Unknown weapon: "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-api">tostring</span><span class="c-op">(</span>weaponName<span class="c-op">))</span>
		<span class="c-kw">return</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>

	<span class="c-kw">local</span> coins <span class="c-op">=</span> player<span class="c-op">.</span>leaderstats<span class="c-op">.</span>Coins
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Value <span class="c-op">&lt;</span> weapon<span class="c-op">.</span>cost <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>

	coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Value <span class="c-op">-=</span> weapon<span class="c-op">.</span>cost
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" bought "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> weaponName<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>This pattern replaces long <code>elseif</code> chains. Adding a fourth weapon is one new line in the table instead of a new branch in your logic.</div></div><div class="callout try"><span class="ci">&#9654;</span><div><b>Try it yourself &mdash; </b>Build a shop table with 5 items (name, price, description). Write a function that prints the whole catalog, and another that looks an item up by name and returns its price or nil.</div></div></div></div></article>
<article class="card" id="ep19" data-n="19" data-search="19 in pairs loop / pairs loop looping over every item in a table or every child of an object. pairs ipairs iteration loops getchildren the generic for loop local fruits = {&quot;apple&quot;, &quot;banana&quot;, &quot;cherry&quot;} for index, value in ipairs(fruits) do print(index, value) end --&gt; 1 apple --&gt; 2 banana --&gt; 3 cherry two variables: the key (or index) and the value . you can name them anything — i, v and key, value are conventional. pairs vs ipairs ipairs pairs covers numeric keys 1, 2, 3… only every key, numeric and named order guaranteed 1, 2, 3… not guaranteed — effectively random stops at the first nil (a gap) nothing, it sees everything use for arrays / lists dictionaries, or when you&#x27;re unsure local mixed = {&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;, name = &quot;nay&quot;, level = 7} for i, v in ipairs(mixed) do print(i, v) end --&gt; 1 a --&gt; 2 b (skips name and level entirely) for k, v in pairs(mixed) do print(k, v) end --&gt; 1 a / 2 b / name nay / level 7 (in any order) never rely on pairs order. it genuinely varies between runs. if order matters, use an array with ipairs , or collect the keys and table.sort them first. generalized iteration (modern luau) luau lets you drop pairs / ipairs entirely. it picks the right behavior and it&#x27;s slightly faster. thedevking&#x27;s videos predate this, so you&#x27;ll see the old form everywhere — both work. for index, value in fruits do print(index, value) end for _, child in workspace:getchildren() do print(child.name) end the underscore _ is a normal variable name that programmers use to mean &quot;i have to declare this but i&#x27;m not using it.&quot; very common as the index when you only want the value. looping over children for _, child in workspace:getchildren() do if child:isa(&quot;basepart&quot;) then child.anchored = true child.brickcolor = brickcolor.random() end end -- everything nested, all the way down for _, obj in model:getdescendants() do if obj:isa(&quot;basepart&quot;) then obj.cancollide = false elseif obj:isa(&quot;decal&quot;) then obj.transparency = 1 end end looping over players local players = game:getservice(&quot;players&quot;) for _, player in players:getplayers() do local stats = player:findfirstchild(&quot;leaderstats&quot;) if stats then stats.coins.value += 10 end end looping over a dictionary local scores = {nay = 250, alex = 180, sam = 420} for name, score in pairs(scores) do print(name .. &quot;: &quot; .. score) end -- find the highest local bestname, bestscore = nil, -math.huge for name, score in pairs(scores) do if score &gt; bestscore then bestname, bestscore = name, score end end print(bestname .. &quot; wins with &quot; .. bestscore) removing while iterating removing items from an array with table.remove while looping forward skips elements , because every removal shifts everything left. loop backwards instead. -- wrong for i, v in ipairs(list) do if v == &quot;remove me&quot; then table.remove(list, i) -- silently skips the next item end end -- right: iterate backwards for i = #list, 1, -1 do if list[i] == &quot;remove me&quot; then table.remove(list, i) end end destroying instances while looping over :getchildren() is safe, because getchildren returns a fresh snapshot table rather than a live view. next() -- pairs is really just a wrapper around next() local t = {} if next(t) == nil then print(&quot;table is empty&quot;) -- the cheapest emptiness check there is end putting it all together local players = game:getservice(&quot;players&quot;) -- every 30 seconds, pay every player based on how many -- &quot;coinpad&quot; parts they are currently standing on. while true do task.wait(30) for _, player in players:getplayers() do local character = player.character if not character then continue end local stats = player:findfirstchild(&quot;leaderstats&quot;) if not stats then continue end local earned = 0 for _, pad in workspace:getchildren() do if pad.name ~= &quot;coinpad&quot; then continue end local root = character:findfirstchild(&quot;humanoidrootpart&quot;) if root and (root.position - pad.position).magnitude 0 then stats.coins.value += earned print(player.name .. &quot; earned &quot; .. earned) end end end loop through every part in workspace and print a count of how many are anchored vs unanchored. then loop through a dictionary of item prices and print only the ones under 100 coins."><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge">19</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">in pairs loop / pairs loop</span><span class="ctag">Looping over every item in a table or every child of an object.</span></span><span class="cmeta">8:38</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button><button class="yt" type="button" data-vid="rYn-H6L8GUI" data-n="19" data-title="in pairs loop / pairs loop"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M8 5v14l11-7z"/></svg>Watch</button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">pairs</span><span class="chip">ipairs</span><span class="chip">iteration</span><span class="chip">loops</span><span class="chip">getchildren</span></div><h3 class="bh">The generic for loop</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> fruits <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span><span class="c-str">"apple"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"banana"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"cherry"</span><span class="c-op">}</span>

<span class="c-kw">for</span> index<span class="c-op">,</span> value <span class="c-kw">in</span> <span class="c-api">ipairs</span><span class="c-op">(</span>fruits<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>index<span class="c-op">,</span> value<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-com">--&gt; 1  apple</span>
<span class="c-com">--&gt; 2  banana</span>
<span class="c-com">--&gt; 3  cherry</span></code></pre></div><p>Two variables: the <b>key</b> (or index) and the <b>value</b>. You can name them anything &mdash; <code>i, v</code> and <code>key, value</code> are conventional.</p><h3 class="bh">pairs vs ipairs</h3><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>ipairs</code></th><th><code>pairs</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Covers</td><td>Numeric keys 1, 2, 3&hellip; only</td><td><b>Every</b> key, numeric and named</td></tr><tr><td>Order</td><td>Guaranteed 1, 2, 3&hellip;</td><td><b>Not guaranteed</b> &mdash; effectively random</td></tr><tr><td>Stops at</td><td>The first nil (a gap)</td><td>Nothing, it sees everything</td></tr><tr><td>Use for</td><td>Arrays / lists</td><td>Dictionaries, or when you're unsure</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> mixed <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span><span class="c-str">"a"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-str">"b"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> Name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Nay"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> Level <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">7</span><span class="c-op">}</span>

<span class="c-kw">for</span> i<span class="c-op">,</span> v <span class="c-kw">in</span> <span class="c-api">ipairs</span><span class="c-op">(</span>mixed<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>i<span class="c-op">,</span> v<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-com">--&gt; 1 a</span>
<span class="c-com">--&gt; 2 b       (skips Name and Level entirely)</span>

<span class="c-kw">for</span> k<span class="c-op">,</span> v <span class="c-kw">in</span> <span class="c-api">pairs</span><span class="c-op">(</span>mixed<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span> <span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>k<span class="c-op">,</span> v<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-com">--&gt; 1 a / 2 b / Name Nay / Level 7   (in ANY order)</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>Never rely on <code>pairs</code> order. It genuinely varies between runs. If order matters, use an array with <code>ipairs</code>, or collect the keys and <code>table.sort</code> them first.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Generalized iteration (modern Luau)</h3><p>Luau lets you drop <code>pairs</code>/<code>ipairs</code> entirely. It picks the right behavior and it's slightly faster. TheDevKing's videos predate this, so you'll see the old form everywhere &mdash; both work.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">for</span> index<span class="c-op">,</span> value <span class="c-kw">in</span> fruits <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>index<span class="c-op">,</span> value<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-kw">for</span> _<span class="c-op">,</span> child <span class="c-kw">in</span> <span class="c-api">workspace</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetChildren</span><span class="c-op">()</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>child<span class="c-op">.</span>Name<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>The underscore <code>_</code> is a normal variable name that programmers use to mean "I have to declare this but I'm not using it." Very common as the index when you only want the value.</div></div><h3 class="bh">Looping over children</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">for</span> _<span class="c-op">,</span> child <span class="c-kw">in</span> <span class="c-api">workspace</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetChildren</span><span class="c-op">()</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> child<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">IsA</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"BasePart"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		child<span class="c-op">.</span>Anchored <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span>
		child<span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">BrickColor</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">BrickColor</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">random</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-com">-- everything nested, all the way down</span>
<span class="c-kw">for</span> _<span class="c-op">,</span> obj <span class="c-kw">in</span> model<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetDescendants</span><span class="c-op">()</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> obj<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">IsA</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"BasePart"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		obj<span class="c-op">.</span>CanCollide <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">false</span>
	<span class="c-kw">elseif</span> obj<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">IsA</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Decal"</span><span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		obj<span class="c-op">.</span>Transparency <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">1</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Looping over players</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-api">Players</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetService</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Players"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-kw">for</span> _<span class="c-op">,</span> player <span class="c-kw">in</span> <span class="c-api">Players</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetPlayers</span><span class="c-op">()</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-kw">local</span> stats <span class="c-op">=</span> player<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">FindFirstChild</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"leaderstats"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> stats <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		stats<span class="c-op">.</span>Coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Value <span class="c-op">+=</span> <span class="c-num">10</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Looping over a dictionary</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> scores <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span>Nay <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">250</span><span class="c-op">,</span> Alex <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">180</span><span class="c-op">,</span> Sam <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">420</span><span class="c-op">}</span>

<span class="c-kw">for</span> name<span class="c-op">,</span> score <span class="c-kw">in</span> <span class="c-api">pairs</span><span class="c-op">(</span>scores<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>name <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">": "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> score<span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-com">-- find the highest</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> bestName<span class="c-op">,</span> bestScore <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-kw">nil</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-op">-</span><span class="c-api">math</span><span class="c-op">.</span>huge
<span class="c-kw">for</span> name<span class="c-op">,</span> score <span class="c-kw">in</span> <span class="c-api">pairs</span><span class="c-op">(</span>scores<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> score <span class="c-op">&gt;</span> bestScore <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		bestName<span class="c-op">,</span> bestScore <span class="c-op">=</span> name<span class="c-op">,</span> score
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>bestName <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" wins with "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> bestScore<span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Removing while iterating</h3><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>Removing items from an array with <code>table.remove</code> while looping forward <b>skips elements</b>, because every removal shifts everything left. Loop <b>backwards</b> instead.</div></div><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- WRONG</span>
<span class="c-kw">for</span> i<span class="c-op">,</span> v <span class="c-kw">in</span> <span class="c-api">ipairs</span><span class="c-op">(</span>list<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> v <span class="c-op">==</span> <span class="c-str">"remove me"</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		<span class="c-api">table</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">remove</span><span class="c-op">(</span>list<span class="c-op">,</span> i<span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">-- silently skips the next item</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-com">-- RIGHT: iterate backwards</span>
<span class="c-kw">for</span> i <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">#</span>list<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-num">1</span><span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-op">-</span><span class="c-num">1</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> list<span class="c-op">[</span>i<span class="c-op">]</span> <span class="c-op">==</span> <span class="c-str">"remove me"</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		<span class="c-api">table</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">remove</span><span class="c-op">(</span>list<span class="c-op">,</span> i<span class="c-op">)</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><p>Destroying <b>Instances</b> while looping over <code>:GetChildren()</code> is safe, because GetChildren returns a fresh snapshot table rather than a live view.</p><h3 class="bh">next()</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- pairs is really just a wrapper around next()</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> t <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{}</span>

<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-api">next</span><span class="c-op">(</span>t<span class="c-op">)</span> <span class="c-op">==</span> <span class="c-kw">nil</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
	<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"table is empty"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">-- the cheapest emptiness check there is</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Putting it all together</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-api">Players</span> <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-api">game</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetService</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Players"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- Every 30 seconds, pay every player based on how many</span>
<span class="c-com">-- "CoinPad" parts they are currently standing on.</span>
<span class="c-kw">while</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
	<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">wait</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-num">30</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

	<span class="c-kw">for</span> _<span class="c-op">,</span> player <span class="c-kw">in</span> <span class="c-api">Players</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetPlayers</span><span class="c-op">()</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
		<span class="c-kw">local</span> character <span class="c-op">=</span> player<span class="c-op">.</span>Character
		<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-kw">not</span> character <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">continue</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>

		<span class="c-kw">local</span> stats <span class="c-op">=</span> player<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">FindFirstChild</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"leaderstats"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
		<span class="c-kw">if</span> <span class="c-kw">not</span> stats <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">continue</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>

		<span class="c-kw">local</span> earned <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">0</span>
		<span class="c-kw">for</span> _<span class="c-op">,</span> pad <span class="c-kw">in</span> <span class="c-api">workspace</span><span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">GetChildren</span><span class="c-op">()</span> <span class="c-kw">do</span>
			<span class="c-kw">if</span> pad<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">~=</span> <span class="c-str">"CoinPad"</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span> <span class="c-kw">continue</span> <span class="c-kw">end</span>

			<span class="c-kw">local</span> root <span class="c-op">=</span> character<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">FindFirstChild</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"HumanoidRootPart"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
			<span class="c-kw">if</span> root <span class="c-kw">and</span> <span class="c-op">(</span>root<span class="c-op">.</span>Position <span class="c-op">-</span> pad<span class="c-op">.</span>Position<span class="c-op">).</span>Magnitude <span class="c-op">&lt;</span> <span class="c-num">10</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
				earned <span class="c-op">+=</span> <span class="c-num">5</span>
			<span class="c-kw">end</span>
		<span class="c-kw">end</span>

		<span class="c-kw">if</span> earned <span class="c-op">&gt;</span> <span class="c-num">0</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
			stats<span class="c-op">.</span>Coins<span class="c-op">.</span>Value <span class="c-op">+=</span> earned
			<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span>player<span class="c-op">.</span>Name <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" earned "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> earned<span class="c-op">)</span>
		<span class="c-kw">end</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout try"><span class="ci">&#9654;</span><div><b>Try it yourself &mdash; </b>Loop through every part in Workspace and print a count of how many are Anchored vs unanchored. Then loop through a dictionary of item prices and print only the ones under 100 coins.</div></div></div></div></article>
<article class="card apx" id="epA" data-n="A" data-search="a modern luau: what changed since 2018 the series is from 2018. everything in it still works, but these are the newer, better ways. modern luau task deprecated old vs new, side by side in the videos (still works) use this today why wait(1) task.wait(1) more accurate, never throttled spawn(fn) task.spawn(fn) runs immediately instead of on a delay delay(3, fn) task.delay(3, fn) accurate timing x = x + 1 x += 1 shorter, same result &quot;a&quot; .. b .. &quot;c&quot; backtick string interpolation far easier to read for i,v in pairs(t) for i,v in t generalized iteration, slightly faster math.randomseed(tick()) nothing, or random.new() auto-seeded now clickdetector proximityprompt works on console and mobile automatically intvalue for metadata :setattribute() lighter, visible in properties game.players game:getservice(&quot;players&quot;) can&#x27;t break if something is renamed :remove() :destroy() remove is deprecated and leaks memory obj:connect() lowercase obj:connect() lowercase form is removed a separate localscript script with runcontext = client runs client code from anywhere string interpolation local name = &quot;nay&quot; local coins = 250 -- old print(&quot;hello &quot; .. name .. &quot;, you have &quot; .. coins .. &quot; coins&quot;) -- new (backticks, not quotes) print(`hello {name}, you have {coins} coins`) print(`total after bonus: {coins + 50}`) -- expressions work too type annotations luau is gradually typed. adding types catches mistakes before you press play and makes autocomplete much smarter. fully optional. local function damage(target: humanoid, amount: number): boolean if target.health --!strict as the very first line of a script to turn on full type checking for that file. start with --!nonstrict if strict is too noisy. the task library in full task.wait(n) -- yield for n seconds, returns actual elapsed task.wait() -- yield until the next frame task.spawn(fn, ...) -- run fn on a new thread right now task.defer(fn, ...) -- run fn at the end of the current frame task.delay(n, fn) -- run fn after n seconds, non-blocking task.cancel(thread) -- stop a scheduled thread task.synchronize() -- parallel luau: switch to serial task.desynchronize() -- parallel luau: switch to parallel"><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge apxb">A</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">Modern Luau: what changed since 2018</span><span class="ctag">The series is from 2018. Everything in it still works, but these are the newer, better ways.</span></span><span class="cmeta">reference</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">modern</span><span class="chip">luau</span><span class="chip">task</span><span class="chip">deprecated</span></div><h3 class="bh">Old vs new, side by side</h3><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>In the videos (still works)</th><th>Use this today</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>wait(1)</code></td><td><code>task.wait(1)</code></td><td>More accurate, never throttled</td></tr><tr><td><code>spawn(fn)</code></td><td><code>task.spawn(fn)</code></td><td>Runs immediately instead of on a delay</td></tr><tr><td><code>delay(3, fn)</code></td><td><code>task.delay(3, fn)</code></td><td>Accurate timing</td></tr><tr><td><code>x = x + 1</code></td><td><code>x += 1</code></td><td>Shorter, same result</td></tr><tr><td><code>"a" .. b .. "c"</code></td><td>backtick string interpolation</td><td>Far easier to read</td></tr><tr><td><code>for i,v in pairs(t)</code></td><td><code>for i,v in t</code></td><td>Generalized iteration, slightly faster</td></tr><tr><td><code>math.randomseed(tick())</code></td><td>nothing, or <code>Random.new()</code></td><td>Auto-seeded now</td></tr><tr><td><code>ClickDetector</code></td><td><code>ProximityPrompt</code></td><td>Works on console and mobile automatically</td></tr><tr><td><code>IntValue</code> for metadata</td><td><code>:SetAttribute()</code></td><td>Lighter, visible in Properties</td></tr><tr><td><code>game.Players</code></td><td><code>game:GetService("Players")</code></td><td>Can't break if something is renamed</td></tr><tr><td><code>:Remove()</code></td><td><code>:Destroy()</code></td><td>Remove is deprecated and leaks memory</td></tr><tr><td><code>obj:connect()</code> lowercase</td><td><code>obj:Connect()</code></td><td>Lowercase form is removed</td></tr><tr><td>A separate LocalScript</td><td><code>Script</code> with <code>RunContext = Client</code></td><td>Runs client code from anywhere</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class="bh">String interpolation</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Nay"</span>
<span class="c-kw">local</span> coins <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">250</span>

<span class="c-com">-- old</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Hello "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> name <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">", you have "</span> <span class="c-op">..</span> coins <span class="c-op">..</span> <span class="c-str">" coins"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- new (backticks, not quotes)</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">`Hello {name}, you have {coins} coins`</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
<span class="c-api">print</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">`Total after bonus: {coins + 50}`</span><span class="c-op">)</span>   <span class="c-com">-- expressions work too</span></code></pre></div><h3 class="bh">Type annotations</h3><p>Luau is gradually typed. Adding types catches mistakes before you press Play and makes autocomplete much smarter. Fully optional.</p><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-kw">local</span> <span class="c-kw">function</span> <span class="c-fn">damage</span><span class="c-op">(</span>target<span class="c-op">:</span> Humanoid<span class="c-op">,</span> amount<span class="c-op">:</span> number<span class="c-op">):</span> boolean
	<span class="c-kw">if</span> target<span class="c-op">.</span>Health <span class="c-op">&lt;=</span> <span class="c-num">0</span> <span class="c-kw">then</span>
		<span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-kw">false</span>
	<span class="c-kw">end</span>
	target<span class="c-op">.</span>Health <span class="c-op">-=</span> amount
	<span class="c-kw">return</span> <span class="c-kw">true</span>
<span class="c-kw">end</span>

<span class="c-kw">type</span> Weapon <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span>
	name<span class="c-op">:</span> <span class="c-api">string</span><span class="c-op">,</span>
	damage<span class="c-op">:</span> number<span class="c-op">,</span>
	rarity<span class="c-op">:</span> <span class="c-api">string</span>?<span class="c-op">,</span>        <span class="c-com">-- the ? means optional</span>
<span class="c-op">}</span>

<span class="c-kw">local</span> sword<span class="c-op">:</span> Weapon <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-op">{</span>name <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-str">"Sword"</span><span class="c-op">,</span> damage <span class="c-op">=</span> <span class="c-num">25</span><span class="c-op">}</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout tip"><span class="ci">&#9733;</span><div>Add <code>--!strict</code> as the very first line of a script to turn on full type checking for that file. Start with <code>--!nonstrict</code> if strict is too noisy.</div></div><h3 class="bh">The task library in full</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">wait</span><span class="c-op">(</span>n<span class="c-op">)</span>         <span class="c-com">-- yield for n seconds, returns actual elapsed</span>
<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">wait</span><span class="c-op">()</span>          <span class="c-com">-- yield until the next frame</span>
<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">spawn</span><span class="c-op">(</span>fn<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-op">...)</span>  <span class="c-com">-- run fn on a new thread RIGHT NOW</span>
<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">defer</span><span class="c-op">(</span>fn<span class="c-op">,</span> <span class="c-op">...)</span>  <span class="c-com">-- run fn at the end of the current frame</span>
<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-api">delay</span><span class="c-op">(</span>n<span class="c-op">,</span> fn<span class="c-op">)</span>    <span class="c-com">-- run fn after n seconds, non-blocking</span>
<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">cancel</span><span class="c-op">(</span>thread<span class="c-op">)</span>  <span class="c-com">-- stop a scheduled thread</span>
<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">synchronize</span><span class="c-op">()</span>   <span class="c-com">-- parallel Luau: switch to serial</span>
<span class="c-api">task</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">desynchronize</span><span class="c-op">()</span> <span class="c-com">-- parallel Luau: switch to parallel</span></code></pre></div></div></div></article>
<article class="card apx" id="epB" data-n="B" data-search="b quick reference everything at a glance. operators, keywords, services, common properties, common methods. reference cheatsheet operators services all operators category operators arithmetic + - * / // % ^ compound += -= *= /= //= %= ^= ..= comparison == ~= &lt; &gt; &lt;= &gt;= logical and or not concat .. length # member access . (property/child) : (method) dot vs colon -- dot: properties and children part.name part.size model.head -- colon: methods (functions that belong to the object) part:destroy() model:findfirstchild(&quot;head&quot;) humanoid:moveto(pos) -- the colon secretly passes the object as the first argument. -- these two lines are identical: part:destroy() part.destroy(part) using . where a : belongs gives &quot;expected &#x27;:&#x27; not &#x27;.&#x27; calling member function&quot; . if it does something, it&#x27;s a colon. if it is something, it&#x27;s a dot. all keywords and break continue do else elseif end false for function if in local nil not or repeat return then true until while export type properties you&#x27;ll set constantly property type notes name string how other scripts find it parent instance where it lives. nil = nowhere position vector3 center point, no rotation cframe cframe position + rotation together size vector3 in studs color color3 color3.fromrgb(r,g,b) brickcolor brickcolor named palette material enum.material neon, plastic, glass, forcefield… transparency number 0-1 1 = invisible reflectance number 0-1 mirror-ness anchored boolean true = ignores physics cancollide boolean false = walk through it cantouch boolean false = .touched won&#x27;t fire canquery boolean false = raycasts ignore it shape enum.parttype block, ball, cylinder, wedge humanoid.health number 0 triggers .died humanoid.maxhealth number default 100 humanoid.walkspeed number default 16 humanoid.jumppower number default 50 instance methods you&#x27;ll use constantly method returns :findfirstchild(name) child or nil :findfirstchild(name, true) searches all descendants :waitforchild(name, timeout?) child, yielding until it exists :findfirstchildofclass(class) first child of that exact class :findfirstchildwhichisa(class) first child of that class or a subclass :findfirstancestor(name) searches upward :getchildren() table of direct children :getdescendants() table of everything inside, recursively :isa(class) boolean, respects inheritance :isdescendantof(obj) boolean :clone() a copy with parent = nil :destroy() nothing; permanent :getpropertychangedsignal(prop) a signal you can :connect to :setattribute(name, value) nothing :getattribute(name) the value or nil constructors vector3.new(x, y, z) vector2.new(x, y) cframe.new(x, y, z) cframe.new(position, lookatposition) cframe.angles(rx, ry, rz) -- radians! color3.new(r, g, b) -- 0 to 1 color3.fromrgb(r, g, b) -- 0 to 255 color3.fromhsv(h, s, v) -- 0 to 1 brickcolor.new(&quot;bright red&quot;) brickcolor.random() udim2.new(xscale, xoffset, yscale, yoffset) udim2.fromscale(x, y) udim2.fromoffset(x, y) instance.new(&quot;classname&quot;) random.new(seed) -- seed is optional numberrange.new(min, max) ray.new(origin, direction) useful vector3 math local a = vector3.new(0, 0, 0) local b = vector3.new(3, 4, 0) print((b - a).magnitude) --&gt; 5 distance between two points print(b.unit) --&gt; direction only, length 1 print(a:lerp(b, 0.5)) --&gt; the midpoint print(b * 2) --&gt; vector3(6, 8, 0) print(vector3.zero, vector3.one, vector3.yaxis) -- distance from a player to a part local root = character:findfirstchild(&quot;humanoidrootpart&quot;) local dist = (root.position - part.position).magnitude if dist &lt; 20 then print(&quot;close enough&quot;) end"><header class="chead"><label class="done"><input type="checkbox" class="dchk"><span></span></label><button class="ctoggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span class="badge apxb">B</span><span class="ctitles"><span class="ctitle">Quick reference</span><span class="ctag">Everything at a glance. Operators, keywords, services, common properties, common methods.</span></span><span class="cmeta">reference</span><span class="caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span></button></header><div class="cbody"><div class="cinner"><div class="chips"><span class="chip">reference</span><span class="chip">cheatsheet</span><span class="chip">operators</span><span class="chip">services</span></div><h3 class="bh">All operators</h3><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Operators</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Arithmetic</td><td><code>+</code> <code>-</code> <code>*</code> <code>/</code> <code>//</code> <code>%</code> <code>^</code></td></tr><tr><td>Compound</td><td><code>+=</code> <code>-=</code> <code>*=</code> <code>/=</code> <code>//=</code> <code>%=</code> <code>^=</code> <code>..=</code></td></tr><tr><td>Comparison</td><td><code>==</code> <code>~=</code> <code>&lt;</code> <code>&gt;</code> <code>&lt;=</code> <code>&gt;=</code></td></tr><tr><td>Logical</td><td><code>and</code> <code>or</code> <code>not</code></td></tr><tr><td>Concat</td><td><code>..</code></td></tr><tr><td>Length</td><td><code>#</code></td></tr><tr><td>Member access</td><td><code>.</code> (property/child) &nbsp; <code>:</code> (method)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class="bh">Dot vs colon</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-com">-- DOT: properties and children</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Name
part<span class="c-op">.</span>Size
model<span class="c-op">.</span>Head

<span class="c-com">-- COLON: methods (functions that belong to the object)</span>
part<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Destroy</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
model<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">FindFirstChild</span><span class="c-op">(</span><span class="c-str">"Head"</span><span class="c-op">)</span>
humanoid<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">MoveTo</span><span class="c-op">(</span>pos<span class="c-op">)</span>

<span class="c-com">-- The colon secretly passes the object as the first argument.</span>
<span class="c-com">-- These two lines are identical:</span>
part<span class="c-op">:</span><span class="c-fn">Destroy</span><span class="c-op">()</span>
part<span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">Destroy</span><span class="c-op">(</span>part<span class="c-op">)</span></code></pre></div><div class="callout warn"><span class="ci">!</span><div>Using <code>.</code> where a <code>:</code> belongs gives <i>"Expected ':' not '.' calling member function"</i>. If it <b>does</b> something, it's a colon. If it <b>is</b> something, it's a dot.</div></div><h3 class="bh">All keywords</h3><p><code>and</code> <code>break</code> <code>continue</code> <code>do</code> <code>else</code> <code>elseif</code> <code>end</code> <code>false</code> <code>for</code> <code>function</code> <code>if</code> <code>in</code> <code>local</code> <code>nil</code> <code>not</code> <code>or</code> <code>repeat</code> <code>return</code> <code>then</code> <code>true</code> <code>until</code> <code>while</code> <code>export</code> <code>type</code></p><h3 class="bh">Properties you'll set constantly</h3><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Type</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Name</code></td><td>string</td><td>How other scripts find it</td></tr><tr><td><code>Parent</code></td><td>Instance</td><td>Where it lives. nil = nowhere</td></tr><tr><td><code>Position</code></td><td>Vector3</td><td>Center point, no rotation</td></tr><tr><td><code>CFrame</code></td><td>CFrame</td><td>Position + rotation together</td></tr><tr><td><code>Size</code></td><td>Vector3</td><td>In studs</td></tr><tr><td><code>Color</code></td><td>Color3</td><td><code>Color3.fromRGB(r,g,b)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>BrickColor</code></td><td>BrickColor</td><td>Named palette</td></tr><tr><td><code>Material</code></td><td>Enum.Material</td><td>Neon, Plastic, Glass, ForceField&hellip;</td></tr><tr><td><code>Transparency</code></td><td>number 0-1</td><td>1 = invisible</td></tr><tr><td><code>Reflectance</code></td><td>number 0-1</td><td>Mirror-ness</td></tr><tr><td><code>Anchored</code></td><td>boolean</td><td>true = ignores physics</td></tr><tr><td><code>CanCollide</code></td><td>boolean</td><td>false = walk through it</td></tr><tr><td><code>CanTouch</code></td><td>boolean</td><td>false = .Touched won't fire</td></tr><tr><td><code>CanQuery</code></td><td>boolean</td><td>false = raycasts ignore it</td></tr><tr><td><code>Shape</code></td><td>Enum.PartType</td><td>Block, Ball, Cylinder, Wedge</td></tr><tr><td><code>Humanoid.Health</code></td><td>number</td><td>0 triggers .Died</td></tr><tr><td><code>Humanoid.MaxHealth</code></td><td>number</td><td>Default 100</td></tr><tr><td><code>Humanoid.WalkSpeed</code></td><td>number</td><td>Default 16</td></tr><tr><td><code>Humanoid.JumpPower</code></td><td>number</td><td>Default 50</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class="bh">Instance methods you'll use constantly</h3><div class="tblwrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>:FindFirstChild(name)</code></td><td>Child or nil</td></tr><tr><td><code>:FindFirstChild(name, true)</code></td><td>Searches all descendants</td></tr><tr><td><code>:WaitForChild(name, timeout?)</code></td><td>Child, yielding until it exists</td></tr><tr><td><code>:FindFirstChildOfClass(class)</code></td><td>First child of that exact class</td></tr><tr><td><code>:FindFirstChildWhichIsA(class)</code></td><td>First child of that class or a subclass</td></tr><tr><td><code>:FindFirstAncestor(name)</code></td><td>Searches upward</td></tr><tr><td><code>:GetChildren()</code></td><td>Table of direct children</td></tr><tr><td><code>:GetDescendants()</code></td><td>Table of everything inside, recursively</td></tr><tr><td><code>:IsA(class)</code></td><td>boolean, respects inheritance</td></tr><tr><td><code>:IsDescendantOf(obj)</code></td><td>boolean</td></tr><tr><td><code>:Clone()</code></td><td>A copy with Parent = nil</td></tr><tr><td><code>:Destroy()</code></td><td>nothing; permanent</td></tr><tr><td><code>:GetPropertyChangedSignal(prop)</code></td><td>A signal you can :Connect to</td></tr><tr><td><code>:SetAttribute(name, value)</code></td><td>nothing</td></tr><tr><td><code>:GetAttribute(name)</code></td><td>The value or nil</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class="bh">Constructors</h3><div class="codewrap"><button class="copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button><pre class="code"><code><span class="c-api">Vector3</span><span class="c-op">.</span><span class="c-fn">new</span><span class="c-op">(</span>x<span class="c-op">,</span> y<span class="c-op">,</span> z<span class="c-op">)</span>
